window.COURSE = {"parts":[{"id":1,"title":"Getting Started","description":"What iOS is, how apps get onto an iPhone, installing Xcode, and running your very first app in the Simulator and on your own phone."},{"id":2,"title":"Swift Essentials","description":"The language of modern Apple development. Learn it properly first: values and types, control flow, collections, optionals, structs and classes, protocols, errors and concurrency."},{"id":3,"title":"Building UI with SwiftUI","description":"Build screens declaratively: views and modifiers, stacks and layout, controls, state and bindings, lists and forms, styling, and animation."},{"id":4,"title":"App Architecture","description":"How real apps are structured: the app lifecycle, navigation between screens, the Observation framework and MVVM, and dependency injection."},{"id":5,"title":"Data & Networking","description":"Fetch JSON from web APIs with URLSession and Codable, persist data with SwiftData, store settings and secrets, and make your app work offline."},{"id":6,"title":"The iOS Platform","description":"Links, sharing, permissions and the photo picker, notifications and background work, widgets, and UIKit interop."},{"id":7,"title":"Quality & Shipping","description":"Test with Swift Testing, debug and profile with Instruments, then sign, upload, test with TestFlight and release on the App Store."},{"id":8,"title":"Capstone: RecipeBox","description":"Put everything together and build a complete, production-style SwiftUI app from an empty project to a TestFlight build."}],"lessons":[{"id":"welcome","title":"Welcome & What Is iOS?","part":1,"minutes":15,"summary":"How to get the most out of this course, what iOS actually is, how an app gets onto an iPhone, and the modern Apple toolkit you're about to learn.","body":":::goals\n- How to use this course so you actually *learn*, not just read\n- What iOS is and how an app runs on an iPhone\n- What you need to get started (and what you don't)\n- Why we use Swift and SwiftUI\n- iOS versions and deployment targets\n:::\n\n## Welcome!\n\nBy the end of this course you'll be able to design, build, test and ship a complete native iOS app. You don't need any previous iOS experience. It helps if you've programmed a little before (in any language), but Part 2 teaches Swift from scratch, so a complete beginner can follow along too.\n\nThe course has **8 parts**. The first seven teach one topic at a time. The eighth is a **capstone project**: you'll build *RecipeBox*, a real app that loads recipes from the internet, works offline, saves favourites with SwiftData, supports dark mode, and is ready for TestFlight.\n\n### How to learn effectively\n\n:::tip The golden rule: type the code yourself\nDon't copy and paste. Typing code forces your brain to process every line, and the mistakes you make (and fix) are where most of the learning happens.\n:::\n\n1. **Read the lesson once** to get the big picture.\n2. **Code along** in Xcode (from Lesson 2 onwards). For pure-Swift examples, press **▶ Try online**: it copies the code and opens an online Swift playground. You can also use an Xcode Playground (**File → New → Playground**).\n3. **Do the practice tasks.** Try each one before you open the solution. Being stuck for 10 minutes is normal and useful.\n4. **Take the quiz** at the end of each lesson, then click **Mark as complete**. Your progress is saved in this browser.\n5. **Experiment.** Change values, break things, read the error messages. That's how professionals learn too.\n\n## What is iOS?\n\n**iOS** is the operating system that runs on the iPhone. Its close siblings, iPadOS, macOS, watchOS, tvOS and visionOS, share most of the same frameworks, so what you learn here carries over to every Apple platform.\n\nLike any OS, it's built in layers. Your app sits at the top and uses the layers below through Apple's frameworks:\n\n<div class=\"diagram\">\n<div class=\"stack\">\n<div class=\"hl\">Your app<small>Swift code + assets (images, colours, strings)</small></div>\n<div>UI frameworks<small>SwiftUI (modern) and UIKit (classic)</small></div>\n<div>App services<small>Foundation, SwiftData, MapKit, WidgetKit, StoreKit, HealthKit, …</small></div>\n<div>Core OS & media<small>Networking, security, graphics (Metal), audio, camera</small></div>\n<div>Darwin kernel<small>Memory, processes, drivers, power management</small></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"diagram-caption\">The iOS stack. You work almost entirely in the top three layers.</div>\n</div>\n\n## What you need\n\n- **A Mac.** Xcode, Apple's development app, only runs on macOS. Xcode 27 needs macOS Tahoe 26.6 or later. An Apple silicon Mac (M1 or newer) is strongly recommended.\n- **Xcode** (free, from the Mac App Store). It includes everything: editor, compiler, iPhone Simulator, design previews, debugger and profiler.\n- **An Apple Account** (free). It's enough to run apps on your *own* iPhone.\n- **Optional: an iPhone.** The Simulator covers most learning. A real device is best for testing the camera, performance and how the app feels.\n- **Later: the Apple Developer Program** (paid yearly membership). Only needed to publish on the App Store or share builds through TestFlight. You don't need it for this course until Lesson 33.\n\n## How an app gets onto an iPhone\n\nWhen you press **Run** in Xcode, a lot happens automatically:\n\n<div class=\"diagram\">\n<div class=\"flow\">\n<div>Swift source<small>.swift files</small></div>\n<div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>Swift compiler<small>native ARM machine code</small></div>\n<div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>App bundle<small>.app = code + assets + Info.plist</small></div>\n<div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>Code signing<small>proves who built it</small></div>\n<div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Installed<small>Simulator or iPhone</small></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"diagram-caption\">Swift compiles straight to fast native code. There's no virtual machine.</div>\n</div>\n\n- The **app bundle** (`.app`) is a folder containing your compiled code, images, the app icon and **Info.plist**, the app's description of itself: its name, version and permission messages.\n- **Code signing** is mandatory. iPhones only run apps signed with a certificate Apple trusts. Xcode handles this for you with *automatic signing*.\n- For the App Store you upload an **archive** to App Store Connect; Apple reviews it, then delivers optimised versions to each device.\n\nEach app runs in its own **sandbox**: a private folder for its files, no access to other apps' data, and no access to sensitive things (camera, location, photos, contacts) without the user's permission. iOS shows the permission prompts; your job is to explain *why* you need access.\n\n## Native vs. cross-platform\n\n\"Native\" iOS development means building with Apple's own tools: **Swift**, **SwiftUI** and **Xcode**. Alternatives include Flutter, React Native and Kotlin Multiplatform. Here's why native is a great place to start:\n\n- **Day-one access** to every new Apple API, from widgets and Live Activities to Apple Intelligence features.\n- **The best performance** and the most \"at home\" look and feel, which iPhone users notice.\n- Most iOS jobs require it, and it makes you better at cross-platform tools later.\n- Swift and SwiftUI also build apps for iPad, Mac, Apple Watch, Apple TV and Vision Pro.\n\n## The modern Apple toolkit\n\n| Tool | What it's for | Where you'll learn it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Swift** | The programming language: fast, safe and expressive | Part 2 |\n| **Swift Concurrency** | `async`/`await` and actors for slow work without freezing the UI | Lesson 11 |\n| **SwiftUI** | Building UI declaratively in Swift code | Part 3 |\n| **Observation** | `@Observable` models that SwiftUI tracks automatically | Lesson 21 |\n| **NavigationStack & TabView** | Moving between screens | Lesson 20 |\n| **URLSession & Codable** | Networking and JSON | Lesson 23 |\n| **SwiftData** | Persisting your app's data | Lesson 24 |\n| **Swift Testing** | Modern unit tests | Lesson 31 |\n| **Xcode, Instruments, TestFlight** | Build, debug, profile and ship | Parts 1 & 7 |\n\n## iOS versions & deployment targets\n\nApple releases a major iOS version every September. In 2025 Apple switched to **year-based version numbers**, jumping from iOS 18 to **iOS 26**, so all its platforms share one number.\n\n| Version | Released | Highlights for developers |\n|---|---|---|\n| iOS 27 | Sept 2026 | The current release (Xcode 27, Swift 6.4) |\n| iOS 26 | Sept 2025 | *Liquid Glass* design, the year-based naming |\n| iOS 18 | Sept 2024 | New `Tab` API, Swift 6, Swift Testing |\n| iOS 17 | Sept 2023 | Observation (`@Observable`) and SwiftData |\n| iOS 16 | Sept 2022 | `NavigationStack` |\n\nYour project's **deployment target** (minimum iOS version) decides the oldest iOS your app runs on. iPhone users update quickly, so most apps support **the current version and one or two before it**. This course uses APIs from iOS 18 and later, and a deployment target of **iOS 18** is a good choice.\n\n:::analogy\nThe deployment target is the oldest phone software you promise to support. The **SDK** is the newest dictionary of APIs you're allowed to look words up in. Xcode always builds with the latest SDK, and you guard newer APIs with `if #available(iOS 27, *)` when you need them.\n:::\n\n## Your roadmap\n\n1. **Getting Started**: install Xcode and run an app (you're here).\n2. **Swift Essentials**: the language, from values to concurrency.\n3. **Building UI with SwiftUI**: layout, controls, state, lists, styling, animation.\n4. **App Architecture**: lifecycle, navigation, Observation & MVVM, dependency injection.\n5. **Data & Networking**: URLSession, Codable, SwiftData, settings, offline caching.\n6. **The iOS Platform**: sharing, permissions, notifications, widgets, UIKit.\n7. **Quality & Shipping**: testing, debugging, App Store and TestFlight.\n8. **Capstone: RecipeBox**: build a complete app from scratch.\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: Which of these do you need to build iOS apps with Xcode?\n- [ ] A Windows PC with Visual Studio\n- [x] A Mac\n- [ ] A paid Apple Developer Program membership from day one\n- [ ] An iPhone\n> Xcode runs only on macOS. A free Apple Account is enough to run apps on your own device; the paid program is only needed for the App Store and TestFlight.\n\nQ: What is Info.plist?\n- [ ] The compiled Swift code\n- [x] A file in the app bundle describing the app: name, version, permission messages and more\n- [ ] A list of the app's users\n- [ ] The Xcode project file\n> Info.plist is the app's self-description that iOS reads before running any code.\n\nQ: Your app's deployment target is iOS 18. What does that mean?\n- [ ] It only runs on iOS 18\n- [x] It can be installed on iOS 18 and newer\n- [ ] It's built with the iOS 18 SDK\n- [ ] It needs an iPhone released in 2024\n> The deployment target is the minimum supported version. You always build with the latest SDK.\n\nQ: Why does an app need the user's permission to use the camera?\n- [ ] Because Swift requires it\n- [ ] Because the camera is slow\n- [x] Because each app runs in a sandbox and can't access sensitive resources without consent\n- [ ] Because Xcode blocks camera code by default\n> iOS protects privacy-sensitive resources and asks the user, showing your explanation from Info.plist.\n```\n"},{"id":"setup","title":"Install Xcode & Run Your First App","part":1,"minutes":30,"summary":"Install Xcode, create a SwiftUI project, run it in the iPhone Simulator and on your own iPhone, and see changes instantly with live previews.","body":":::goals\n- How to install Xcode and the iOS platform\n- How to create a new SwiftUI app project\n- How to run your app in the Simulator *and* on a real iPhone\n- How to use SwiftUI previews to see changes instantly\n- The Xcode shortcuts you'll use every day\n:::\n\n## 1. Install Xcode\n\n1. Open the **App Store** on your Mac, search for **Xcode** and click **Get**. It's a large download (well over 10 GB once installed), so use a good connection and make sure you have plenty of free disk space.\n   - Alternatively, download it from [developer.apple.com/xcode](https://developer.apple.com/xcode/). You can also find beta versions there.\n2. Launch Xcode. On first launch it asks which **platforms** to install. Make sure **iOS** is selected (it includes the iPhone Simulator).\n3. Let it finish installing components. Grab a coffee. ☕\n\n:::note System requirements\nXcode 27 needs **macOS Tahoe 26.6 or later**. 16 GB of RAM is comfortable, and **keep at least 40–50 GB free**: Xcode, simulators and build caches grow quickly. You can reclaim space later from **Xcode → Settings → Components** and by deleting old simulators.\n:::\n\n## 2. Create your first project\n\n1. In the Welcome window click **Create New Project…** (or **File → New → Project**).\n2. Choose the **iOS** tab → **App** → **Next**.\n3. Fill in the options:\n\n| Field | Value | What it means |\n|---|---|---|\n| Product Name | `HelloiOS` | The app's name |\n| Team | None (for now) | Your signing team. We'll set it when running on a device |\n| Organization Identifier | `com.yourname` | Usually your reversed domain |\n| Bundle Identifier | `com.yourname.HelloiOS` | Generated automatically. Your app's unique ID, which you can't change after publishing |\n| Interface | **SwiftUI** | The modern UI framework |\n| Language | **Swift** | |\n| Testing System | Swift Testing | The modern testing framework (Lesson 31) |\n| Storage | None | We'll add SwiftData later |\n\n4. Click **Next**, choose where to save, and click **Create**.\n\n## 3. Run it in the Simulator ▶\n\nAt the top of the Xcode window there's a **run destination** menu. Choose a recent **iPhone** simulator, then click the **▶ Run** button, or press [[Cmd]] + [[R]].\n\nXcode builds the app, boots the Simulator (a virtual iPhone on your Mac) and launches it. You'll see a globe icon and **\"Hello, world!\"**. Congratulations, you've just run your first iOS app! 🎉\n\n:::tip Simulator tips\n[[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[H]] goes to the Home Screen, [[Cmd]] + [[←]] / [[→]] rotates, [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[A]] toggles dark mode, and [[Cmd]] + [[S]] takes a screenshot. Use **Features** and **I/O** in the Simulator's menu bar for things like Face ID, location and the keyboard.\n:::\n\n## 4. Look at the code\n\nXcode created two Swift files. First, the app's entry point:\n\n```swift title=\"HelloiOSApp.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\n@main                                  // 1. where the app starts\nstruct HelloiOSApp: App {              // 2. the app itself\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup {                  // 3. the app's window(s)\n            ContentView()              // 4. the first screen\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n1. `@main` marks the type where the program begins.\n2. The app is a struct conforming to the `App` protocol.\n3. A `WindowGroup` scene manages your app's window. On iPad and Mac there can be several.\n4. `ContentView()` is the first view shown.\n\nAnd the first screen:\n\n```swift title=\"ContentView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct ContentView: View {             // 1. a view is a struct conforming to View\n    var body: some View {              // 2. body describes what it looks like\n        VStack {                       // 3. a vertical stack\n            Image(systemName: \"globe\") // 4. an SF Symbols icon\n                .imageScale(.large)\n                .foregroundStyle(.tint)\n            Text(\"Hello, world!\")\n        }\n        .padding()                     // 5. a modifier\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {                             // 6. a live preview in Xcode's canvas\n    ContentView()\n}\n```\n\n1. SwiftUI screens are small **structs** that conform to `View`.\n2. `body` returns what the view looks like. SwiftUI calls it whenever it needs to redraw.\n3. `VStack` stacks its children vertically.\n4. `Image(systemName:)` shows one of Apple's thousands of built-in **SF Symbols** icons.\n5. **Modifiers** like `.padding()` customise a view. You'll use them everywhere.\n6. `#Preview` renders the view in Xcode's **canvas**, with no Simulator needed.\n\n## 5. Live previews\n\nOpen `ContentView.swift`. If the canvas isn't visible on the right, press [[Option]] + [[Cmd]] + [[Enter]]. If the preview is paused, press [[Option]] + [[Cmd]] + [[P]] to resume.\n\nNow change `\"Hello, world!\"` to `\"Hello, <your name>!\"` and watch the preview update as you type. Previews are interactive: buttons work, and you can switch to dark mode or different devices using the controls under the preview.\n\n## 6. Run on your own iPhone (optional but recommended)\n\n1. In Xcode, open **Xcode → Settings → Accounts**, click **+** and sign in with your Apple Account.\n2. Select the project in the navigator (the blue icon at the top), then the **HelloiOS** target → **Signing & Capabilities**. Tick **Automatically manage signing** and choose your **Team** (your name, *Personal Team*).\n3. Connect your iPhone with a cable (after the first time, it also works over Wi-Fi) and tap **Trust** on the phone.\n4. On the iPhone, enable **Settings → Privacy & Security → Developer Mode** and restart when asked.\n5. Choose your iPhone as the run destination and press [[Cmd]] + [[R]].\n6. With a free account, the first launch may be blocked: on the iPhone open **Settings → General → VPN & Device Management**, tap your developer profile and choose **Trust**.\n\n:::note Free accounts\nApps installed with a free Personal Team expire after **7 days**. Just run them from Xcode again. The paid Developer Program removes this limit and unlocks TestFlight and the App Store.\n:::\n\n## Shortcuts worth learning now\n\n| Action | Shortcut |\n|---|---|\n| Run | [[Cmd]] + [[R]] |\n| Stop | [[Cmd]] + [[.]] |\n| Build | [[Cmd]] + [[B]] |\n| Clean build folder | [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[K]] |\n| Open any file or symbol quickly | [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[O]] |\n| Show/hide canvas | [[Option]] + [[Cmd]] + [[Enter]] |\n| Resume preview | [[Option]] + [[Cmd]] + [[P]] |\n| Library of views, modifiers, symbols | [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[L]] |\n| Re-indent selection | [[Ctrl]] + [[I]] |\n| Comment/uncomment | [[Cmd]] + [[/]] |\n| Quick Help for a symbol | [[Option]] + click |\n| Jump to definition | [[Cmd]] + click |\n| Show fix-it / code actions | [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[A]] |\n\n:::tip Read the red and yellow\nXcode shows errors (red) and warnings (yellow) inline. Click the icon to see the message, and often a **Fix** button that corrects the code for you.\n:::\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **\"No such module 'SwiftUI'\" or strange build errors after an update**: **Product → Clean Build Folder** ([[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[K]]) and build again.\n- **No simulators in the destination menu**: open **Xcode → Settings → Components** and install the iOS platform.\n- **Preview failed**: click **Diagnostics** on the error banner; it's usually a compile error elsewhere in the file. Fix it, then resume ([[Option]] + [[Cmd]] + [[P]]).\n- **\"Untrusted Developer\" on the iPhone**: trust your profile in Settings → General → VPN & Device Management.\n- **Signing errors**: make sure your Team is selected and the bundle identifier is unique (add your name to it).\n\n:::exercise\n1. Change the text to `\"Hello, <your name>! Welcome to iOS.\"` and the symbol to `\"swift\"` (the Swift bird).\n2. Add a second `#Preview(\"Dark\") { ContentView().preferredColorScheme(.dark) }` and look at both previews.\n3. Run the app in the Simulator and, if you can, on your iPhone.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nstruct ContentView: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack {\n            Image(systemName: \"swift\")\n                .imageScale(.large)\n                .foregroundStyle(.tint)\n            Text(\"Hello, Ada! Welcome to iOS.\")\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Light\") {\n    ContentView()\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Dark\") {\n    ContentView()\n        .preferredColorScheme(.dark)\n}\n```\nEach `#Preview` appears as a tab in the canvas. Give them names to tell them apart.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What does the `@main` attribute do?\n- [ ] Marks the main screen of the app\n- [x] Marks the type where the program starts\n- [ ] Makes a view appear in previews\n- [ ] Runs the app on a device\n> Exactly one type in the app is marked @main. For SwiftUI apps, it's your App struct.\n\nQ: Which property describes what a SwiftUI view looks like?\n- [ ] `view`\n- [ ] `render()`\n- [x] `body`\n- [ ] `layout`\n> Every View has a `body` that returns other views.\n\nQ: What is `#Preview` for?\n- [ ] It runs the app in the Simulator\n- [x] It renders a view live in Xcode's canvas while you edit\n- [ ] It's required for the app to compile\n- [ ] It uploads a screenshot to the App Store\n> Previews are a development tool. They don't affect the running app.\n\nQ: What do you need to run an app on your own iPhone?\n- [ ] A paid Developer Program membership\n- [x] An Apple Account added to Xcode, a selected Team, and Developer Mode enabled on the phone\n- [ ] A jailbroken iPhone\n- [ ] TestFlight\n> A free Personal Team is enough for your own devices (apps expire after 7 days).\n```\n"},{"id":"xcode-tour","title":"Tour of Xcode & Your Project","part":1,"minutes":25,"summary":"Find your way around Xcode, understand every file in a new project, and learn about targets, build settings, Info.plist, asset catalogs and Swift packages.","body":":::goals\n- The main areas of the Xcode window\n- What each file in a new project is for\n- Targets, schemes and build settings\n- Version and build numbers, deployment target, Info.plist and capabilities\n- Asset catalogs, String Catalogs and adding Swift packages\n:::\n\n## The Xcode window\n\n<div class=\"diagram\">\n<div class=\"flow\">\n<div>Navigator<small>left: files, search, issues, tests, debug</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Editor<small>centre: code + canvas previews</small></div>\n<div>Inspectors<small>right: file info, Quick Help, attributes</small></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"flow\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">\n<div>Toolbar<small>top: run/stop, scheme, destination, status</small></div>\n<div>Debug area<small>bottom: console output and variables</small></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\nToggle panels with [[Cmd]] + [[0]] (navigator), [[Cmd]] + [[Option]] + [[0]] (inspectors) and [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[Y]] (debug area). The navigator's tabs are [[Cmd]] + [[1]] to [[Cmd]] + [[9]]: [[Cmd]] + [[1]] is files, [[Cmd]] + [[3]] find, [[Cmd]] + [[5]] issues, [[Cmd]] + [[6]] tests.\n\n## Your project's files\n\n<div class=\"tree\">HelloiOS/\n├── HelloiOS.xcodeproj           ← the project: targets, settings, file references\n├── HelloiOS/                    ← your app's source folder\n│   ├── HelloiOSApp.swift        ← @main App entry point\n│   ├── ContentView.swift        ← the first screen\n│   └── Assets.xcassets          ← asset catalog: app icon, accent colour, images, colours\n├── HelloiOSTests/               ← unit tests (Swift Testing)\n└── HelloiOSUITests/             ← UI tests (XCTest)</div>\n\nRecent Xcode versions keep the navigator **in sync with the folder on disk**: create a folder in Finder or Xcode and it shows up in both. Organise code into folders like `Models`, `Views` and `Services` as the app grows.\n\n:::note Where's Info.plist?\nNew projects don't have a separate `Info.plist` file. Its values live in the target's **Info** tab and build settings, and Xcode generates the real `Info.plist` when it builds. You'll add keys there, like permission descriptions (Lesson 27).\n:::\n\n## Targets and schemes\n\n- A **target** is one thing Xcode builds: your app, a test bundle, a widget extension. Each target has its own settings and files.\n- A **scheme** says what to build, run, test and profile. The scheme menu sits next to the run destination in the toolbar.\n\nClick the blue **project icon** at the top of the navigator, then select the **HelloiOS** target. The tabs across the top are where you'll configure your app.\n\n### General\n\n- **Display Name**: the name under the icon on the Home Screen.\n- **Bundle Identifier**: the app's unique ID, e.g. `com.yourname.HelloiOS`.\n- **Version** (e.g. `1.0`, shown to users) and **Build** (e.g. `1`, must increase for every upload to App Store Connect).\n- **Minimum Deployments**: the deployment target. Set it to **iOS 18.0** for this course.\n- **Supported Destinations**: iPhone, iPad, Mac (Designed for iPad), Vision.\n\n### Signing & Capabilities\n\nYour signing team and certificates, plus **capabilities**: special permissions an app needs Apple to grant, like Push Notifications, iCloud, App Groups, Sign in with Apple or HealthKit. Add them with **+ Capability**.\n\n### Info\n\nThe keys that go into Info.plist: permission messages, supported orientations, URL types for deep links, background modes, and more.\n\n### Build Settings\n\nHundreds of compiler and build options. You'll rarely need to change them. Use the search field (e.g. search \"Swift Language Version\" or \"deployment\").\n\n## Asset catalogs\n\n`Assets.xcassets` manages images and colours:\n\n- **AppIcon**: drop in a single 1024×1024 image; Xcode generates every size. You can provide separate light, dark and tinted variants.\n- **AccentColor**: your app's tint colour for buttons, links and toggles.\n- **Image sets**: drag PNG, JPEG, PDF or SVG files in. Use them with `Image(\"name\")`.\n- **Color sets**: named colours with separate light and dark values. Use them with `Color(\"name\")`.\n\nXcode also generates Swift symbols for your assets, so you can write `Image(.pancakes)` and `Color(.brandPrimary)` with autocomplete and compile-time checking.\n\n:::tip Prefer SF Symbols for icons\nApple's [SF Symbols](https://developer.apple.com/sf-symbols/) library has thousands of icons that match the system font, scale with Dynamic Type and support multiple colours. Download the free **SF Symbols** Mac app to browse them, then use `Image(systemName: \"heart.fill\")`.\n:::\n\n## Text and localisation: String Catalogs\n\nEvery string you write in SwiftUI, like `Text(\"Hello\")`, is automatically **localisable**. Add a **String Catalog** (**File → New → File from Template → String Catalog**, named `Localizable`), then build. Xcode finds your strings and lists them in the catalog, ready for translation. Add a language with **+** at the bottom of the catalog, and Xcode tracks which strings still need translating.\n\n## Swift Package Manager\n\nTo use an open-source library, choose **File → Add Package Dependencies…**, paste the package's Git URL, choose a version rule (usually *Up to Next Major Version*) and add it to your target. Xcode downloads it and keeps the version in `Package.resolved`.\n\nApple's frameworks cover a huge amount, so you'll need fewer third-party libraries than on other platforms. This course only uses Apple frameworks.\n\n## Building from the command line\n\n`xcodebuild` builds and tests from the Terminal, which is how CI servers do it:\n\n```bash\nxcodebuild -scheme HelloiOS -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=iPhone 17' build\nxcodebuild -scheme HelloiOS -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=iPhone 17' test\n```\n\n(Use the name of any simulator you've installed. `xcrun simctl list devices` lists them.)\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Navigator (left), editor + canvas (centre), inspectors (right), debug area (bottom).\n- The **project** holds **targets** (app, tests, extensions); **schemes** decide what to build and run.\n- **General** tab: display name, bundle ID, version/build, deployment target.\n- **Info** tab: Info.plist keys. **Signing & Capabilities**: team and special entitlements.\n- **Assets.xcassets** for icons, images and colours; **String Catalogs** for translations; **Swift packages** for libraries.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Change the **Display Name** to \"My First App\", run, and check the Home Screen name.\n2. Set **Minimum Deployments** to iOS 18.0.\n3. In `Assets.xcassets`, set **AccentColor** to a colour you like and see the globe icon change (it uses `.tint`).\n4. Add a Color Set called `Brand` with different light and dark values, and use it: `Text(\"Hello\").foregroundStyle(Color(\"Brand\"))`. Toggle dark mode in the preview.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What's the difference between Version and Build?\n- [ ] They're the same thing\n- [x] Version (e.g. 1.2) is shown to users; Build (e.g. 42) must increase with every upload\n- [ ] Build is shown to users; Version is internal\n- [ ] Version is for iOS, Build is for macOS\n> App Store Connect rejects an upload whose build number was already used for that version.\n\nQ: Where do you add a capability like Push Notifications?\n- [ ] In ContentView.swift\n- [ ] In Assets.xcassets\n- [x] In the target's Signing & Capabilities tab\n- [ ] It's automatic\n> Capabilities are entitlements Apple must grant to your signed app.\n\nQ: How do you add a third-party Swift library to your app?\n- [ ] Copy its files into your folder\n- [x] File → Add Package Dependencies… with the package's Git URL\n- [ ] Download a .framework from a website\n- [ ] Install it with Homebrew\n> Swift Package Manager is built into Xcode.\n\nQ: What's the recommended source for app icons like a heart or a trash can?\n- [ ] Screenshots of other apps\n- [x] SF Symbols, via `Image(systemName:)`\n- [ ] Emoji\n- [ ] PNG files at every size\n> SF Symbols scale with text, support weights and multicolour rendering, and look native.\n```\n"},{"id":"swift-basics","title":"Swift Basics: Constants, Types & Functions","part":2,"minutes":30,"summary":"Start writing Swift, the language of every Apple platform. Constants and variables, basic types, strings, and functions with Swift's distinctive argument labels.","body":":::goals\n- The difference between `let` and `var` (and why you should prefer `let`)\n- Swift's basic types and type inference\n- String interpolation and multi-line strings\n- Functions, including **argument labels**, default values and `inout`\n:::\n\n## Why learn Swift before iOS?\n\nEvery Apple API you'll use is called from Swift, and SwiftUI leans on Swift features like closures, protocols and property wrappers. Spending a few lessons on pure Swift first will make Parts 3–8 far smoother.\n\n:::tip Run the examples\nPress **▶ Try online** on any example: it copies the code and opens [SwiftFiddle](https://swiftfiddle.com), where you paste and run it. You can also use an **Xcode Playground** (**File → New → Playground → Blank**), which runs Swift code as you type.\n:::\n\n## Your first Swift program\n\n```swift runnable\nprint(\"Hello, Swift!\")\n```\n\nThat's a complete program. In a playground or script, top-level code simply runs from top to bottom. No `main` function or semicolons needed.\n\n## Constants and variables: `let` and `var`\n\n```swift runnable\nlet name = \"Ada\"        // let = constant (can't be changed)\nvar score = 10          // var = variable (can be changed)\n\nscore = score + 5       // ✅ fine\nscore += 1              // ✅ shorthand\n// name = \"Grace\"       // ❌ error: cannot assign to value: 'name' is a 'let' constant\n\nprint(name)\nprint(score)\n```\n\n:::tip Prefer `let`\nUse `let` by default and switch to `var` only when the value really needs to change. Xcode even warns you: *\"Variable was never mutated; consider changing to 'let'\"*. Code where values don't change unexpectedly is easier to reason about, and it's safer with concurrency.\n:::\n\n## Basic types\n\nSwift is **type-safe**: every value has a type known at compile time. You rarely write it, because Swift **infers** it:\n\n```swift runnable\nlet age = 30                 // Int\nlet price = 19.99            // Double\nlet isMember = true          // Bool\nlet initial: Character = \"K\" // Character (needs the annotation)\nlet city = \"Lagos\"           // String\nlet population = 15_000_000  // underscores make big numbers readable\n\n// You can also write the type explicitly:\nlet temperature: Double = 21\nlet count: Int = 3\n\nprint(age, price, isMember, initial, city, population, temperature, count)\n```\n\n| Type | Holds | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Int` | Whole numbers (64-bit on modern devices) | `42` |\n| `Double` | Decimal numbers (the default for decimals) | `3.14` |\n| `Float` | Less precise decimals (graphics) | `Float(3.14)` |\n| `Bool` | `true` or `false` | `true` |\n| `String` | Text | `\"Hello\"` |\n| `Character` | A single character | `\"A\"` |\n\n### Converting between types\n\nSwift never converts numbers implicitly. Create a new value of the type you want:\n\n```swift runnable\nlet whole = 7\nlet decimal = Double(whole)      // 7.0\nlet text = String(whole)         // \"7\"\nlet parsed = Int(\"123\")          // Optional(123): parsing can fail\nlet failed = Int(\"abc\")          // nil\n\nprint(decimal)\nprint(text + \"!\")\nprint(parsed ?? 0)               // ?? gives a fallback (Lesson 7)\nprint(failed ?? 0)\nprint(7 / 2)                     // 3   (integer division!)\nprint(7.0 / 2)                   // 3.5\nprint(7 % 2)                     // 1   (remainder)\n// print(whole + 1.5)            // ❌ error: can't add Int and Double\nprint(Double(whole) + 1.5)       // ✅ 8.5\n```\n\n:::danger Integer division\n`7 / 2` is `3`, because both numbers are `Int`. If you need decimals, make both sides `Double`: `Double(7) / 2`.\n:::\n\n## Strings\n\n### String interpolation\n\nInsert any value into a string with `\\( )`:\n\n```swift runnable\nlet name = \"Sam\"\nlet items = 3\nlet price = 4.5\n\nprint(\"Hi \\(name)!\")\nprint(\"You have \\(items) items\")\nprint(\"Total: \\(Double(items) * price)\")\nprint(\"Name has \\(name.count) letters\")\nprint(\"Shouting: \\(name.uppercased())\")\n```\n\n### Useful string operations\n\n```swift runnable\nlet text = \"Swift Development\"\nprint(text.count)                       // 17\nprint(text.lowercased())                // \"swift development\"\nprint(text.contains(\"Dev\"))             // true\nprint(text.hasPrefix(\"Swift\"))          // true\nprint(text.split(separator: \" \"))       // [\"Swift\", \"Development\"]\nprint(text.replacing(\" \", with: \"_\"))   // \"Swift_Development\"\nprint(text.isEmpty)                     // false\nprint(text.first!)                      // \"S\"\nprint(String(text.reversed()))          // \"tnempoleveD tfiwS\"\n\nvar greeting = \"Hello\"\ngreeting += \", world\"                   // strings declared with var can change\nprint(greeting)\n```\n\n### Multi-line strings\n\n```swift runnable\nlet poem = \"\"\"\n    Roses are red,\n    Swift is so neat,\n    Optionals make\n    my code complete.\n    \"\"\"\nprint(poem)\n```\n\nThe indentation of the closing `\"\"\"` is removed from every line, so you can indent the string along with your code.\n\n## Functions\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc greet(person: String) -> String {\n    return \"Hello, \\(person)!\"\n}\n\nprint(greet(person: \"Ada\"))\n```\n\n- `func` declares a function; parameters have a name and type; `-> Type` is the return type.\n- If the body is a **single expression**, you can omit `return`:\n\n```swift\nfunc square(_ x: Int) -> Int { x * x }\n```\n\n### Argument labels ⭐\n\nSwift functions read like sentences because each parameter can have an **argument label** (used when calling) separate from its **parameter name** (used inside):\n\n```swift runnable\n// label  name\nfunc move(from start: String, to end: String) -> String {\n    \"Moving from \\(start) to \\(end)\"\n}\nprint(move(from: \"Paris\", to: \"Rome\"))\n\n// Use _ to omit the label when it reads naturally without one\nfunc square(_ number: Int) -> Int {\n    number * number\n}\nprint(square(9))\n\n// No label written = the parameter name is the label\nfunc area(width: Double, height: Double) -> Double {\n    width * height\n}\nprint(area(width: 3, height: 4))\n```\n\n:::note You'll see this everywhere\nApple's APIs are designed around labels: `Image(systemName: \"star\")`, `list.insert(item, at: 0)`, `.padding(.horizontal, 16)`. Reading them aloud tells you what they do.\n:::\n\n### Default values\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc orderCoffee(size: String = \"Medium\", sugar: Int = 0, milk: Bool = true) -> String {\n    \"\\(size) coffee, \\(sugar) sugar, milk: \\(milk)\"\n}\n\nprint(orderCoffee())\nprint(orderCoffee(size: \"Large\"))\nprint(orderCoffee(sugar: 2))\nprint(orderCoffee(size: \"Small\", milk: false))\n```\n\nArguments must appear in the same order as the parameters, but you can skip any that have defaults.\n\n### Variadic and `inout` parameters\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc average(_ numbers: Double...) -> Double {   // variadic: any number of values\n    guard !numbers.isEmpty else { return 0 }\n    return numbers.reduce(0, +) / Double(numbers.count)\n}\nprint(average(4, 8, 6))\n\nfunc doubleInPlace(_ value: inout Int) {          // inout: can modify the caller's variable\n    value *= 2\n}\nvar points = 21\ndoubleInPlace(&points)                            // & marks it as modifiable\nprint(points)\n```\n\nParameters are constants by default. `inout` is the explicit exception.\n\n## Comments\n\n```swift\n// Single-line comment\n\n/*\n Multi-line comment\n */\n\n/// Documentation comment: shown in Xcode's Quick Help (Option-click).\n/// - Parameter name: The person to greet.\nfunc greet(_ name: String) { print(\"Hi \\(name)\") }\n```\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `let` for constants, `var` for variables. Prefer `let`.\n- Types are inferred but strict; convert explicitly with `Double(x)`, `String(x)`, `Int(\"42\")`.\n- String interpolation: `\"\\(value)\"`.\n- Functions have **argument labels**; use `_` to omit them and give defaults with `= value`.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Write `func celsiusToFahrenheit(_ c: Double) -> Double` (formula: `c * 9 / 5 + 32`) and print the result for 0, 37 and 100.\n2. Write `func describe(name: String, age: Int, city: String = \"Unknown\") -> String` that returns e.g. *\"Ada is 36 and lives in London.\"* Call it with and without `city`.\n3. Write `func initials(of first: String, and last: String) -> String` that returns e.g. `\"A.L.\"`. Call it as `initials(of: \"Ada\", and: \"Lovelace\")`.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solutions\n```swift runnable\nfunc celsiusToFahrenheit(_ c: Double) -> Double {\n    c * 9 / 5 + 32\n}\n\nfunc describe(name: String, age: Int, city: String = \"Unknown\") -> String {\n    \"\\(name) is \\(age) and lives in \\(city).\"\n}\n\nfunc initials(of first: String, and last: String) -> String {\n    \"\\(first.prefix(1)).\\(last.prefix(1)).\"\n}\n\nprint(celsiusToFahrenheit(0))\nprint(celsiusToFahrenheit(37))\nprint(celsiusToFahrenheit(100))\nprint(describe(name: \"Ada\", age: 36, city: \"London\"))\nprint(describe(name: \"Alan\", age: 41))\nprint(initials(of: \"Ada\", and: \"Lovelace\"))\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What happens with `let x = 5` followed by `x = 6`?\n- [ ] x becomes 6\n- [x] Compile error: x is a constant\n- [ ] Runtime crash\n- [ ] x becomes 11\n> Constants declared with `let` can't be reassigned.\n\nQ: What is the type of `Int(\"42\")`?\n- [ ] Int\n- [x] Int? (an optional Int), because the conversion can fail\n- [ ] String\n- [ ] Double\n> Parsing a string may not produce a number, so the result is optional (Lesson 7).\n\nQ: Given `func move(from start: String, to end: String)`, how do you call it?\n- [ ] `move(start: \"A\", end: \"B\")`\n- [x] `move(from: \"A\", to: \"B\")`\n- [ ] `move(\"A\", \"B\")`\n- [ ] `move(from start: \"A\", to end: \"B\")`\n> Callers use argument labels; the parameter names are used inside the function.\n\nQ: How do you insert a value into a string?\n- [ ] `\"Total: $total\"`\n- [ ] `\"Total: {total}\"`\n- [x] `\"Total: \\(total)\"`\n- [ ] `\"Total: \" + total` for any type\n> Swift uses backslash-parentheses for interpolation.\n```\n"},{"id":"swift-control-flow","title":"Control Flow: if, switch & Loops","part":2,"minutes":25,"summary":"Make decisions and repeat work with if, guard, Swift's powerful switch, ranges, and loops.","body":":::goals\n- `if` / `else`, and `if` and `switch` as expressions\n- `switch`: exhaustive, with ranges, multiple values, tuples and `where`\n- Ranges: `...`, `..<` and `stride`\n- `for-in`, `while` and `repeat-while` loops, `break` and `continue`\n:::\n\n## `if` / `else`\n\n```swift runnable\nlet temperature = 28\n\nif temperature > 30 {\n    print(\"It's hot!\")\n} else if temperature > 20 {\n    print(\"It's warm.\")\n} else {\n    print(\"It's cool.\")\n}\n```\n\nNo parentheses are needed around the condition, but the braces are **always** required. Comparison operators: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`. Logical operators: `&&` (and), `||` (or), `!` (not).\n\n### `if` as an expression\n\nYou can assign the result of an `if` directly, and there's also the classic ternary `?:` for short cases:\n\n```swift runnable\nlet age = 17\nlet status = if age >= 18 { \"adult\" } else { \"minor\" }\nlet ticket = age < 12 ? \"child\" : \"standard\"\nprint(status, ticket)\n```\n\n## `switch`\n\nSwift's `switch` is powerful and safe: it must be **exhaustive** (handle every possible value), and cases don't fall through to the next one, so there's no `break` needed.\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc describe(day: Int) -> String {\n    switch day {\n    case 1:\n        return \"Monday\"\n    case 2:\n        return \"Tuesday\"\n    case 3, 4:                  // several values\n        return \"Midweek\"\n    case 5...7:                 // a range\n        return \"Almost weekend\"\n    default:                    // everything else\n        return \"Not a valid day\"\n    }\n}\n\nfor d in [1, 4, 6, 99] {\n    print(describe(day: d))\n}\n```\n\n### `switch` as an expression, with `where`\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc grade(_ score: Int) -> String {\n    switch score {\n    case 90...: \"A\"                 // 90 and above\n    case 80..<90: \"B\"\n    case 70..<80: \"C\"\n    case let s where s < 0: \"Invalid\"\n    default: \"Keep going\"\n    }\n}\n\nfor s in [95, 83, 71, 40, -5] {\n    print(s, grade(s))\n}\n```\n\n### Matching tuples\n\n```swift runnable\nlet point = (x: 0, y: 5)\n\nswitch point {\ncase (0, 0):\n    print(\"Origin\")\ncase (0, _):                    // _ matches anything\n    print(\"On the y-axis\")\ncase (_, 0):\n    print(\"On the x-axis\")\ncase let (x, y) where x == y:\n    print(\"On the diagonal\")\ndefault:\n    print(\"Somewhere else\")\n}\n```\n\nYou'll use `switch` constantly with **enums** (Lesson 8), for example to show a loading, content or error view.\n\n## Ranges\n\n```swift runnable\nlet closed = 1...5              // 1, 2, 3, 4, 5  (includes the end)\nlet halfOpen = 1..<5            // 1, 2, 3, 4     (excludes the end)\nlet fromThree = 3...            // one-sided: 3 and up\n\nprint(Array(closed))\nprint(Array(halfOpen))\nprint(closed.contains(3))       // true\nprint(fromThree.contains(100))  // true\n\nfor i in stride(from: 0, through: 20, by: 5) {   // 0, 5, 10, 15, 20\n    print(i, terminator: \" \")\n}\nprint()\nfor i in stride(from: 10, to: 0, by: -3) {       // 10, 7, 4, 1\n    print(i, terminator: \" \")\n}\nprint()\n```\n\n`..<` is perfect for indices: `for i in 0..<array.count`.\n\n## `for-in` loops\n\n```swift runnable\nfor i in 1...3 {\n    print(\"Round \\(i)\")\n}\n\nlet fruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\"]\nfor fruit in fruits {\n    print(fruit)\n}\n\nfor (index, fruit) in fruits.enumerated() {     // index and value\n    print(\"\\(index): \\(fruit)\")\n}\n\nfor _ in 1...3 {                                 // don't need the value\n    print(\"🎉\", terminator: \"\")\n}\nprint()\n```\n\n## `while` and `repeat-while`\n\n```swift runnable\nvar countdown = 3\nwhile countdown > 0 {\n    print(\"\\(countdown)…\")\n    countdown -= 1\n}\nprint(\"Liftoff! 🚀\")\n\nvar attempts = 0\nrepeat {                 // runs at least once\n    attempts += 1\n    print(\"Attempt \\(attempts)\")\n} while attempts < 2\n```\n\nSwift has no `++` or `--` operators. Use `+= 1` and `-= 1`.\n\n## `break` and `continue`\n\n```swift runnable\nfor n in 1...10 {\n    if n % 2 == 0 { continue }   // skip even numbers\n    if n > 7 { break }           // stop the loop\n    print(n)\n}\n```\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `if` needs braces, not parentheses. `if` and `switch` can produce values.\n- `switch` must be exhaustive and doesn't fall through. It matches values, ranges, tuples and `where` conditions.\n- Ranges: `a...b` includes `b`, `a..<b` excludes it. `stride` counts by steps.\n- `for-in` iterates anything iterable; `enumerated()` adds indices.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. **FizzBuzz** for 1 to 20: print \"Fizz\" for multiples of 3, \"Buzz\" for multiples of 5, \"FizzBuzz\" for both, otherwise the number. Use a `switch` on the tuple `(n % 3, n % 5)`.\n2. Write `func bmiCategory(_ bmi: Double) -> String` using `switch` with ranges: under 18.5 → \"Underweight\", 18.5 to under 25 → \"Normal\", 25 to under 30 → \"Overweight\", 30+ → \"Obese\".\n3. Print the 7 times table (7 × 1 up to 7 × 10).\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solutions\n```swift runnable\nfor n in 1...20 {\n    switch (n % 3, n % 5) {\n    case (0, 0): print(\"FizzBuzz\")\n    case (0, _): print(\"Fizz\")\n    case (_, 0): print(\"Buzz\")\n    default: print(n)\n    }\n}\n\nfunc bmiCategory(_ bmi: Double) -> String {\n    switch bmi {\n    case ..<18.5: \"Underweight\"\n    case 18.5..<25: \"Normal\"\n    case 25..<30: \"Overweight\"\n    default: \"Obese\"\n    }\n}\nprint(bmiCategory(22.4))\n\nfor i in 1...10 {\n    print(\"7 × \\(i) = \\(7 * i)\")\n}\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What happens when a switch doesn't handle every possible value and has no default?\n- [ ] Unmatched values are ignored\n- [x] It's a compile error, because switch must be exhaustive\n- [ ] It crashes at runtime\n- [ ] It falls through to the first case\n> Swift requires every possible value to be handled.\n\nQ: Which range contains exactly 0, 1, 2, 3, 4?\n- [ ] `0...5`\n- [x] `0..<5`\n- [ ] `1...4`\n- [ ] `stride(from: 0, to: 4, by: 1)`\n> The half-open range `..<` excludes its upper bound.\n\nQ: How do you increase a variable by one in Swift?\n- [ ] `count++`\n- [x] `count += 1`\n- [ ] `++count`\n- [ ] `count.increment()`\n> Swift removed ++ and -- to avoid confusion.\n\nQ: What does `continue` do in a loop?\n- [ ] Exits the loop\n- [x] Skips to the next iteration\n- [ ] Restarts the loop\n- [ ] Pauses execution\n> `break` exits; `continue` skips the rest of the current iteration.\n```\n"},{"id":"swift-collections","title":"Collections & Closures","part":2,"minutes":35,"summary":"Arrays, sets and dictionaries, plus closures and the functional operations (map, filter, sorted, reduce and friends) you'll use in every screen.","body":":::goals\n- Arrays, sets and dictionaries, and when to use each\n- Why `let` collections can't change and `var` ones can\n- Closure syntax, shorthand arguments (`$0`) and trailing closures\n- `map`, `filter`, `sorted`, `reduce`, `compactMap`, `first(where:)`, `Dictionary(grouping:by:)` and more\n- Tuples\n:::\n\n## Arrays\n\nAn **array** is an ordered list of values of the same type.\n\n```swift runnable\nvar colors = [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]      // [String]\nprint(colors[0])                           // \"red\"\nprint(colors.count)                        // 3\nprint(colors.first ?? \"none\")              // \"red\"\nprint(colors.contains(\"green\"))            // true\n\ncolors.append(\"yellow\")\ncolors.insert(\"purple\", at: 0)\ncolors.remove(at: 1)                       // removes \"red\"\ncolors[0] = \"violet\"\nprint(colors)\n\nlet empty: [Int] = []                      // an empty array needs a type\nprint(empty.isEmpty)\n\nlet combined = [1, 2] + [3, 4]             // [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(combined)\n```\n\n:::tip `let` vs `var` for collections\nA collection declared with `let` can't be changed at all: no appends, no removals. Arrays, sets and dictionaries are **value types** (Lesson 8), so copying one gives you an independent copy. Changing the copy never affects the original. This makes SwiftUI state predictable.\n:::\n\n:::danger Index out of range\n`colors[10]` on a 4-element array **crashes** the app. Check `indices.contains(i)` first, or use `first`/`last`, which return optionals.\n:::\n\n## Sets\n\nA **set** is an unordered collection of **unique** values, which makes \"is this in there?\" checks very fast.\n\n```swift runnable\nvar tags: Set = [\"swift\", \"ios\", \"swift\"]\nprint(tags.count)                 // 2: duplicates are ignored\ntags.insert(\"xcode\")\nprint(tags.contains(\"ios\"))       // true\n\nlet a: Set = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nlet b: Set = [3, 4, 5]\nprint(a.intersection(b).sorted()) // [3, 4]\nprint(a.union(b).sorted())        // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(a.subtracting(b).sorted())  // [1, 2]\n```\n\n## Dictionaries\n\nA **dictionary** maps unique **keys** to **values**.\n\n```swift runnable\nvar capitals = [\n    \"France\": \"Paris\",\n    \"Japan\": \"Tokyo\",\n    \"Kenya\": \"Nairobi\"\n]                                                   // [String: String]\n\nprint(capitals[\"Japan\"] ?? \"?\")                     // Tokyo\nprint(capitals[\"Mars\"] ?? \"Unknown\")                // lookups return optionals\nprint(capitals[\"Mars\", default: \"Unknown\"])         // or give a default\n\ncapitals[\"Peru\"] = \"Lima\"                           // add\ncapitals[\"Kenya\"] = nil                             // remove\n\nfor (country, city) in capitals.sorted(by: { $0.key < $1.key }) {\n    print(\"\\(city) is the capital of \\(country)\")\n}\n\nvar stock = [\"apples\": 5]\nstock[\"apples\", default: 0] += 1                    // update with a default\nstock[\"pears\", default: 0] += 3\nprint(stock.sorted { $0.key < $1.key })\n```\n\nDictionaries (and sets) are **unordered**, so sort them when you need a stable order.\n\n## Closures\n\nA **closure** is a block of code you can store and pass around, like a function without a name. The full syntax:\n\n```swift runnable\nlet double = { (x: Int) -> Int in\n    return x * 2\n}\nprint(double(21))\n\nlet sayHello = { print(\"Hello!\") }     // no parameters\nsayHello()\n```\n\nParameters and return type go before `in`, and the body after it.\n\n### Getting shorter\n\nSwift lets you drop anything it can infer. These all do the same thing:\n\n```swift runnable\nlet numbers = [5, 2, 8, 1]\n\nlet a = numbers.sorted(by: { (x: Int, y: Int) -> Bool in return x < y })\nlet b = numbers.sorted(by: { x, y in x < y })   // types inferred, implicit return\nlet c = numbers.sorted(by: { $0 < $1 })         // shorthand argument names\nlet d = numbers.sorted { $0 < $1 }              // trailing closure\nlet e = numbers.sorted(by: <)                   // an operator is a function too!\n\nprint(a, b, c, d, e)\n```\n\n- **`$0`, `$1`** refer to the first and second arguments.\n- **Trailing closures**: if the last argument is a closure, write it after the parentheses, and drop the parentheses if it's the only argument.\n\n:::note This is exactly how SwiftUI looks\n`VStack { Text(\"Hi\") }` and `Button(\"Save\") { save() }` are just functions and initialisers taking trailing closures. Once you're comfortable with closures, SwiftUI code reads naturally.\n:::\n\n## Functional operations\n\nThese methods take closures and return **new** collections. They're the bread and butter of Swift.\n\n```swift runnable\nstruct Product {\n    let name: String\n    let price: Double\n    let category: String\n    let inStock: Bool\n}\n\nlet products = [\n    Product(name: \"Laptop\", price: 1200, category: \"Tech\", inStock: true),\n    Product(name: \"Mouse\", price: 25, category: \"Tech\", inStock: true),\n    Product(name: \"Desk\", price: 300, category: \"Furniture\", inStock: false),\n    Product(name: \"Chair\", price: 150, category: \"Furniture\", inStock: true),\n    Product(name: \"Monitor\", price: 280, category: \"Tech\", inStock: false)\n]\n\n// filter: keep matching items\nprint(products.filter { $0.inStock }.map(\\.name))\n\n// map: transform each item\nprint(products.map { $0.name.uppercased() })\n\n// sorted(by:)\nprint(products.sorted { $0.price < $1.price }.map(\\.name))\n\n// first(where:) returns an optional\nprint(products.first { $0.price > 500 }?.name ?? \"none\")\n\n// contains(where:), allSatisfy\nprint(products.contains { !$0.inStock })\nprint(products.allSatisfy { $0.price > 10 })\n\n// reduce: combine into one value\nlet total = products.reduce(0) { $0 + $1.price }\nprint(total)\n\n// max(by:), min(by:)\nprint(products.max { $0.price < $1.price }?.name ?? \"\")\n\n// Dictionary(grouping:by:): [Key: [Item]]\nlet byCategory = Dictionary(grouping: products, by: \\.category)\nfor (category, items) in byCategory.sorted(by: { $0.key < $1.key }) {\n    print(category, items.count)\n}\n\n// compactMap: transform and drop nils\nlet inputs = [\"4\", \"x\", \"15\", \"\", \"8\"]\nprint(inputs.compactMap { Int($0) })        // [4, 15, 8]\n```\n\n`\\.name` is a **key path**, a reference to a property. `map(\\.name)` is shorthand for `map { $0.name }`. SwiftUI uses key paths a lot, e.g. `ForEach(items, id: \\.id)`.\n\n### Chaining\n\n```swift runnable\nimport Foundation   // for .capitalized\n\nlet words = [\"swift\", \"is\", \"a\", \"fast\", \"and\", \"expressive\", \"language\"]\n\nlet result = words\n    .filter { $0.count > 3 }\n    .map { $0.capitalized }\n    .sorted { $0.count < $1.count }\n    .joined(separator: \" · \")\n\nprint(result)\n```\n\n:::note Foundation\nSome handy string features, like `capitalized`, come from **Foundation**, Apple's base library of dates, URLs, formatting, JSON and more. Every iOS app has it: `import SwiftUI` imports Foundation for you. In a standalone Swift file you add `import Foundation` yourself.\n:::\n\n## Tuples\n\nA **tuple** groups a few values without defining a type, which is handy for returning multiple values:\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc minMax(of values: [Int]) -> (min: Int, max: Int)? {\n    guard let first = values.first else { return nil }\n    var lo = first, hi = first\n    for v in values { lo = min(lo, v); hi = max(hi, v) }\n    return (lo, hi)\n}\n\nif let range = minMax(of: [8, 3, 12, 5]) {\n    print(\"From \\(range.min) to \\(range.max)\")\n}\n\nlet (name, age) = (\"Ada\", 36)     // destructuring\nprint(name, age)\n```\n\nFor anything more than a quick return value, prefer a `struct` (Lesson 8).\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- **Array** (ordered), **Set** (unique, fast lookup), **Dictionary** (key → value). Lookups that can fail return optionals.\n- Collections are value types; `let` makes them fully immutable.\n- Closures: `{ params in body }`, shortened with `$0` and trailing closure syntax.\n- `map`, `filter`, `sorted`, `reduce`, `compactMap`, `first(where:)`: chain them for readable data pipelines.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\nGiven:\n\n```swift\nstruct Student { let name: String; let grade: Int; let house: String }\n\nlet students = [\n    Student(name: \"Amara\", grade: 88, house: \"Red\"), Student(name: \"Ben\", grade: 72, house: \"Blue\"),\n    Student(name: \"Chen\", grade: 95, house: \"Red\"), Student(name: \"Dara\", grade: 64, house: \"Green\"),\n    Student(name: \"Eli\", grade: 79, house: \"Blue\"), Student(name: \"Fay\", grade: 91, house: \"Green\")\n]\n```\n\n1. Print the names of students with a grade of 80 or more, sorted alphabetically.\n2. Print the average grade.\n3. Print each house with its best student.\n4. Print whether any student failed (below 65).\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift runnable\nstruct Student { let name: String; let grade: Int; let house: String }\n\nlet students = [\n    Student(name: \"Amara\", grade: 88, house: \"Red\"), Student(name: \"Ben\", grade: 72, house: \"Blue\"),\n    Student(name: \"Chen\", grade: 95, house: \"Red\"), Student(name: \"Dara\", grade: 64, house: \"Green\"),\n    Student(name: \"Eli\", grade: 79, house: \"Blue\"), Student(name: \"Fay\", grade: 91, house: \"Green\")\n]\n\n// 1\nprint(students.filter { $0.grade >= 80 }.map(\\.name).sorted())\n\n// 2\nlet average = Double(students.reduce(0) { $0 + $1.grade }) / Double(students.count)\nprint(average)\n\n// 3\nlet houses = Dictionary(grouping: students, by: \\.house)\nfor (house, members) in houses.sorted(by: { $0.key < $1.key }) {\n    let best = members.max { $0.grade < $1.grade }!\n    print(\"\\(house): \\(best.name)\")\n}\n\n// 4\nprint(students.contains { $0.grade < 65 })\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What does `[1, 2, 3, 4].filter { $0 % 2 == 0 }.map { $0 * 10 }` return?\n- [ ] [10, 20, 30, 40]\n- [x] [20, 40]\n- [ ] [2, 4]\n- [ ] [10, 30]\n> filter keeps 2 and 4; map multiplies each by 10.\n\nQ: What is the type of `capitals[\"Japan\"]` for a `[String: String]` dictionary?\n- [ ] String\n- [x] String? (the key might not exist)\n- [ ] [String]\n- [ ] Bool\n> Dictionary lookups return optionals.\n\nQ: What does `$0` mean inside a closure?\n- [ ] The closure's return value\n- [x] The closure's first argument\n- [ ] The first element of the array\n- [ ] A global variable\n> Shorthand argument names $0, $1, … refer to the closure's parameters in order.\n\nQ: What does `[\"1\", \"a\", \"3\"].compactMap { Int($0) }` return?\n- [ ] [1, nil, 3]\n- [x] [1, 3]\n- [ ] [\"1\", \"3\"]\n- [ ] It crashes\n> compactMap transforms each element and drops the nil results.\n```\n"},{"id":"swift-optionals","title":"Optionals","part":2,"minutes":25,"summary":"Swift's answer to \"no value\". Optionals make missing data explicit and remove a whole class of crashes. Learn if let, guard let, ??, optional chaining, and why ! is dangerous.","body":":::goals\n- What an optional is and why Swift has them\n- Unwrapping safely with `if let` and `guard let`\n- The nil-coalescing operator `??`\n- Optional chaining with `?.`\n- Why force-unwrapping `!` is dangerous\n:::\n\n## The problem: missing values\n\nSometimes a value might not exist: a user without a nickname, a number that failed to parse, a search with no result. Many languages let *any* variable secretly be `null`, and using it crashes the app.\n\nSwift makes this **explicit in the type**. A regular `String` *always* contains a string. A `String?` (an **optional** String) contains either a string **or `nil`**:\n\n```swift runnable\nvar nickname: String? = \"Addy\"\nprint(nickname as Any)      // Optional(\"Addy\")\nnickname = nil              // ✅ allowed for optionals\n\nvar name: String = \"Ada\"\n// name = nil               // ❌ error: 'nil' cannot be assigned to type 'String'\nprint(name)\n```\n\nYou can't use an optional as if it were a plain value. The compiler makes you handle the `nil` case first:\n\n```swift\nlet length = nickname.count   // ❌ error: value of optional type 'String?' must be unwrapped\n```\n\nThat turns a potential crash into a compile-time error you fix before shipping. 🎉\n\n:::analogy\nAn optional is a gift box. It might contain something, or it might be empty. Before you can use what's inside, you have to open the box and check.\n:::\n\n## `if let`: unwrap when present\n\n```swift runnable\nlet input = \"42\"\n\nif let number = Int(input) {             // Int(input) is Int?\n    print(\"Got a number: \\(number)\")     // number is a plain Int here\n} else {\n    print(\"Not a number\")\n}\n\nlet nickname: String? = \"Addy\"\nif let nickname {                        // shorthand: unwrap into the same name\n    print(\"Hi, \\(nickname)!\")\n}\n\n// Unwrap several at once, with an extra condition:\nlet a: Int? = 4, b: Int? = 6\nif let a, let b, a < b {\n    print(\"\\(a) < \\(b)\")\n}\n```\n\n## `guard let`: exit early\n\n`guard` checks a condition and requires you to **leave the scope** (usually `return`) if it fails. The unwrapped value stays available for the rest of the function, which keeps the \"happy path\" unindented:\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc greet(_ name: String?) {\n    guard let name, !name.isEmpty else {\n        print(\"Hello, stranger!\")\n        return\n    }\n    print(\"Hello, \\(name)!\")    // name is a non-optional String from here on\n}\n\ngreet(\"Ada\")\ngreet(nil)\ngreet(\"\")\n```\n\n:::tip `if let` or `guard let`?\nUse `guard` for **preconditions**: \"if I don't have this, I can't continue.\" Use `if let` when you do something extra only if the value exists. You'll see `guard` a lot at the top of functions.\n:::\n\n## Nil-coalescing: `??`\n\n`a ?? b` means \"use `a` if it has a value, otherwise `b`\":\n\n```swift runnable\nlet input: String? = nil\nlet name = input ?? \"Guest\"\nprint(\"Welcome, \\(name)\")\n\nlet scores = [\"Ada\": 91]\nprint(scores[\"Grace\"] ?? 0)\nprint(Int(\"abc\") ?? -1)\n```\n\n## Optional chaining: `?.`\n\nCall properties and methods on an optional. If it's `nil`, the whole expression becomes `nil` instead of crashing:\n\n```swift runnable\nstruct Address { var city: String }\nstruct User { var name: String; var address: Address? }\n\nlet ada = User(name: \"Ada\", address: Address(city: \"London\"))\nlet bob = User(name: \"Bob\", address: nil)\n\nprint(ada.address?.city ?? \"no city\")                 // London\nprint(bob.address?.city ?? \"no city\")                 // no city\nprint(bob.address?.city.uppercased() ?? \"-\")          // chains stop at the first nil\n\nlet users: [User] = [ada, bob]\nprint(users.first?.name ?? \"empty\")\n```\n\n## Force unwrapping: `!` (avoid!)\n\n`value!` says \"I'm *certain* this isn't nil, so give me the value.\" If you're wrong, **the app crashes**:\n\n```swift\nlet number = Int(\"abc\")!     // 💥 Fatal error: Unexpectedly found nil while unwrapping an Optional value\n```\n\n:::danger Treat `!` as a code smell\nEvery `!` is a potential crash. Almost always there's a safer option: `if let`, `guard let`, `??` or optional chaining. The rare acceptable cases are values that can *only* be nil because of a programmer mistake, like a hard-coded URL you know is valid: `URL(string: \"https://apple.com\")!`.\n:::\n\n## Optionals and collections\n\n```swift runnable\nlet values = [3, 8, 1]\nprint(values.first as Any)        // Optional(3)\nprint(values.max() ?? 0)          // 8\nprint([Int]().max() ?? 0)         // 0: empty array, so max() is nil\n\nlet maybeNumbers = [\"1\", \"two\", \"3\"].map { Int($0) }   // [Int?]\nprint(maybeNumbers)\nprint(maybeNumbers.compactMap { $0 })                   // [1, 3]\n```\n\nNote the difference: `[Int?]` is an array that may *contain* nils; `[Int]?` is an array that may itself *be* nil.\n\n## Optional `map`\n\nTransform an optional's value only if it exists:\n\n```swift runnable\nlet text: String? = \"42\"\nlet doubled = text.flatMap { Int($0) }.map { $0 * 2 }\nprint(doubled ?? 0)   // 84\n```\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `T?` means \"a `T` or `nil`\". Non-optional types can never be nil.\n- Unwrap with `if let x` / `guard let x else { return }`.\n- `??` supplies a fallback; `?.` chains safely through optionals.\n- `!` crashes on nil, so avoid it.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Write `func lengthOrZero(_ s: String?) -> Int` using optional chaining and `??`.\n2. Write `func parseAge(_ input: String?) -> String` that returns `\"Age: N\"` for a valid number, or `\"Invalid age\"` otherwise. Use `guard let`.\n3. Given `let users = [1: \"Ada\", 2: \"Linus\"]`, print the uppercased name for id 3, falling back to `\"UNKNOWN\"`, in one line.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solutions\n```swift runnable\nfunc lengthOrZero(_ s: String?) -> Int {\n    s?.count ?? 0\n}\n\nfunc parseAge(_ input: String?) -> String {\n    guard let input, let age = Int(input) else {\n        return \"Invalid age\"\n    }\n    return \"Age: \\(age)\"\n}\n\nprint(lengthOrZero(nil))\nprint(lengthOrZero(\"hey\"))\nprint(parseAge(\"42\"))\nprint(parseAge(\"forty\"))\nprint(parseAge(nil))\n\nlet users = [1: \"Ada\", 2: \"Linus\"]\nprint(users[3]?.uppercased() ?? \"UNKNOWN\")\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: Which declaration can hold nil?\n- [ ] `let a: String = nil`\n- [x] `let a: String? = nil`\n- [ ] `let a: String! = nil` is the only way\n- [ ] `let a = nil`\n> Only optional types can hold nil. `let a = nil` doesn't compile because there's no type to infer.\n\nQ: What does `let x = s?.count ?? -1` give when `s` is nil?\n- [ ] nil\n- [ ] 0\n- [x] -1\n- [ ] It crashes\n> The optional chain produces nil, so ?? returns the fallback.\n\nQ: What must the `else` branch of a `guard` statement do?\n- [ ] Print an error\n- [x] Exit the current scope, e.g. with return, throw, break or continue\n- [ ] Nothing special\n- [ ] Unwrap the value\n> guard requires you to leave the scope if the condition fails, which is what makes the unwrapped value safe afterwards.\n\nQ: Why should you avoid `!` force unwrapping?\n- [ ] It's slower\n- [x] It crashes the app if the value is nil\n- [ ] It's deprecated\n- [ ] It makes the value optional\n> Force unwrapping bypasses the safety optionals provide.\n```\n"},{"id":"swift-types","title":"Structs, Classes & Enums","part":2,"minutes":40,"summary":"Model your app's data with Swift's three kinds of types. Understand value vs. reference semantics, properties and methods, and enums with associated values, which power every SwiftUI screen.","body":":::goals\n- Structs: properties, methods, initialisers, `mutating`\n- Classes: reference semantics, inheritance, `final`\n- **Value vs. reference types**, the key difference\n- Computed properties and property observers\n- Enums with raw values and **associated values**\n- Static members, and choosing between struct, class and enum\n:::\n\n## Structs\n\nA **struct** groups related data and behaviour into a new type. Most of your models, and every SwiftUI view, will be structs.\n\n```swift runnable\nstruct Recipe {\n    let name: String            // stored properties\n    var minutes: Int\n    var isFavorite = false      // default value\n\n    func summary() -> String {                   // a method\n        \"\\(name) · \\(minutes) min\" + (isFavorite ? \" ♥\" : \"\")\n    }\n\n    mutating func toggleFavorite() {             // mutating: it changes self\n        isFavorite.toggle()\n    }\n}\n\nvar pancakes = Recipe(name: \"Pancakes\", minutes: 20)   // free \"memberwise\" initialiser\npancakes.minutes = 25\npancakes.toggleFavorite()\nprint(pancakes.summary())\n```\n\n- Swift generates a **memberwise initialiser** automatically: `Recipe(name:minutes:isFavorite:)`, where parameters with defaults are optional.\n- Methods that modify the struct must be marked **`mutating`**.\n- A struct stored in a `let` constant can't change at all, even its `var` properties.\n\n## Classes\n\nA **class** is similar, but with important differences: classes are **reference types**, support **inheritance**, and need an initialiser if their properties have no defaults.\n\n```swift runnable\nclass Animal {\n    let name: String\n    init(name: String) {                  // classes need an explicit init\n        self.name = name\n    }\n    func sound() -> String { \"...\" }\n    func describe() -> String { \"\\(name) says \\(sound())\" }\n}\n\nfinal class Cat: Animal {                 // final = can't be subclassed further\n    override func sound() -> String { \"Meow\" }\n}\n\nclass Cow: Animal {\n    override func sound() -> String { \"Moo\" }\n}\n\nlet animals: [Animal] = [Cat(name: \"Tom\"), Cow(name: \"Daisy\")]\nfor animal in animals {\n    print(animal.describe())\n}\n```\n\n## ⭐ Value types vs. reference types\n\nThis is one of the most important ideas in Swift.\n\n- **Structs, enums, arrays, dictionaries, strings** are **value types**: assigning or passing them **copies** the value.\n- **Classes** are **reference types**: assigning or passing them shares **the same object**.\n\n```swift runnable\nstruct PointStruct { var x = 0 }\nclass PointClass { var x = 0 }\n\nvar a = PointStruct()\nvar b = a            // copy\nb.x = 99\nprint(\"struct:\", a.x, b.x)   // 0 99: a is unaffected\n\nlet c = PointClass()\nlet d = c            // same object\nd.x = 99\nprint(\"class:\", c.x, d.x)    // 99 99: both names point to one object\n```\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div class=\"hl\">Value type<small>each variable owns its own copy</small></div>\n<div>Reference type<small>variables share one object in memory</small></div>\n</div></div>\n\n:::tip Default to structs\nApple's guidance: **use structs by default**. They're simpler, safer (no surprising shared changes) and fast. Reach for a class when you need shared, identity-based state that several parts of the app observe and change. A typical example is an `@Observable` view model or data store (Lesson 21).\n:::\n\n## Computed properties & property observers\n\n```swift runnable\nstruct Rectangle {\n    var width: Double\n    var height: Double {\n        didSet { print(\"Height changed from \\(oldValue) to \\(height)\") }   // observer\n    }\n\n    var area: Double { width * height }            // computed: calculated every time\n\n    var isSquare: Bool { width == height }\n}\n\nvar r = Rectangle(width: 3, height: 4)\nprint(r.area)\nr.height = 3\nprint(r.isSquare)\n```\n\n- A **computed property** has no storage; it calculates its value each time it's read.\n- `willSet` / `didSet` run code before or after a stored property changes.\n\n## Custom initialisers\n\n```swift runnable\nstruct Temperature {\n    var celsius: Double\n\n    init(celsius: Double) {\n        self.celsius = celsius\n    }\n\n    init(fahrenheit: Double) {                    // another way to create one\n        celsius = (fahrenheit - 32) * 5 / 9\n    }\n\n    init?(text: String) {                         // failable init: may return nil\n        guard let value = Double(text) else { return nil }\n        celsius = value\n    }\n}\n\nprint(Temperature(fahrenheit: 212).celsius)\nprint(Temperature(text: \"21.5\")?.celsius ?? .nan)\nprint(Temperature(text: \"hot\")?.celsius ?? .nan)\n```\n\n## Enums\n\nAn **enum** defines a type with a fixed set of cases:\n\n```swift runnable\nenum Difficulty: String, CaseIterable {      // raw values + list of all cases\n    case easy, medium, hard\n\n    var stars: String {                      // enums can have computed properties\n        switch self {\n        case .easy: \"★\"\n        case .medium: \"★★\"\n        case .hard: \"★★★\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nlet d = Difficulty.medium\nprint(d.rawValue, d.stars)                    // \"medium ★★\"\nprint(Difficulty(rawValue: \"hard\") ?? .easy)  // create from a raw value (optional)\nprint(Difficulty.allCases.map(\\.rawValue))    // from CaseIterable\n\nvar level: Difficulty = .easy                 // type known, so .easy is enough\nlevel = .hard\nprint(level)\n```\n\nThe leading-dot syntax (`.easy`) works whenever Swift already knows the type. That's why SwiftUI code is full of things like `.padding(.horizontal)` and `.font(.title)`.\n\n### Associated values ⭐\n\nEach case can carry its **own data**. This is perfect for modelling states, such as what a screen is showing:\n\n```swift runnable\nenum LoadState {\n    case loading\n    case loaded([String])\n    case failed(message: String)\n}\n\nfunc render(_ state: LoadState) -> String {\n    switch state {                                // exhaustive: every case handled\n    case .loading:\n        \"⏳ Loading…\"\n    case .loaded(let items) where items.isEmpty:\n        \"📭 Nothing here yet\"\n    case .loaded(let items):\n        \"✅ \\(items.count) items: \\(items.joined(separator: \", \"))\"\n    case .failed(let message):\n        \"❌ \\(message)\"\n    }\n}\n\nprint(render(.loading))\nprint(render(.loaded([\"Pasta\", \"Curry\"])))\nprint(render(.loaded([])))\nprint(render(.failed(message: \"No internet\")))\n```\n\nBecause `switch` must be exhaustive, if you later add a case (say `.offline`), every `switch` that doesn't handle it becomes a compile error. You can't forget to update the UI.\n\n## Static members\n\n`static` properties and methods belong to the **type**, not to instances:\n\n```swift runnable\nstruct AppConfig {\n    static let apiURL = \"https://dummyjson.com\"\n    static let maxRetries = 3\n}\n\nstruct Recipe {\n    var name: String\n    static let sample = Recipe(name: \"Sample Pancakes\")    // handy for previews!\n}\n\nprint(AppConfig.apiURL)\nprint(Recipe.sample.name)\n```\n\n## Struct, class or enum?\n\n| Use a… | When |\n|---|---|\n| **struct** | Data and most models: a recipe, a user, settings, **SwiftUI views** |\n| **enum** | A fixed set of options or states: difficulty, a loading state, a tab |\n| **class** | Shared mutable state with identity: `@Observable` view models and stores, or when an Apple API requires it (UIKit) |\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Structs are value types (copied); classes are reference types (shared). Default to structs.\n- `mutating` methods change a struct; `final` stops subclassing; `override` replaces superclass behaviour.\n- Computed properties calculate values; `didSet` reacts to changes; `init?` can fail.\n- Enums have cases, raw values and associated values, and `switch` over them is exhaustive.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Create `struct Book` with `title`, `author`, `pages` and `isRead` (default `false`), plus a `mutating func markRead()`. Make an array of four books, mark one as read, and print the total pages read.\n2. Create `enum PaymentResult` with cases `success(amount: Double)`, `declined(reason: String)` and `pending`, and a function that returns a friendly message for each.\n3. Demonstrate value semantics: copy a `Book` into a new variable, change the copy's title, and print both titles.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift runnable\nstruct Book {\n    let title: String\n    let author: String\n    let pages: Int\n    var isRead = false\n\n    mutating func markRead() { isRead = true }\n}\n\nenum PaymentResult {\n    case success(amount: Double)\n    case declined(reason: String)\n    case pending\n}\n\nfunc message(for result: PaymentResult) -> String {\n    switch result {\n    case .success(let amount): \"Paid $\\(amount). Thank you!\"\n    case .declined(let reason): \"Payment declined: \\(reason)\"\n    case .pending: \"Payment is processing…\"\n    }\n}\n\nvar books = [\n    Book(title: \"Dune\", author: \"Herbert\", pages: 412, isRead: true),\n    Book(title: \"The Swift Programming Language\", author: \"Apple\", pages: 800),\n    Book(title: \"Clean Code\", author: \"Martin\", pages: 464),\n    Book(title: \"The Hobbit\", author: \"Tolkien\", pages: 310)\n]\nbooks[3].markRead()\nprint(\"Pages read:\", books.filter(\\.isRead).reduce(0) { $0 + $1.pages })\n\nprint(message(for: .success(amount: 9.99)))\nprint(message(for: .declined(reason: \"Insufficient funds\")))\nprint(message(for: .pending))\n\nlet original = books[0]\nvar copy = original\ncopy.isRead = false\nprint(original.isRead, copy.isRead)   // true false: independent copies\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: After `var b = a` where `a` is a struct, you change `b.x`. What happens to `a.x`?\n- [x] Nothing, because b is an independent copy\n- [ ] It changes too\n- [ ] It becomes nil\n- [ ] Compile error\n> Structs are value types: assignment copies them.\n\nQ: Why must some struct methods be marked `mutating`?\n- [ ] To make them faster\n- [x] Because they modify the struct's own properties, which Swift requires you to mark explicitly\n- [ ] So they can be overridden\n- [ ] To make them asynchronous\n> Structs are immutable by default inside methods; mutating opts in to changing self.\n\nQ: Which is the best fit for \"a screen that's loading, showing results, or showing an error message\"?\n- [ ] A class with three Booleans\n- [x] An enum with associated values, e.g. `.loading`, `.loaded([Item])`, `.failed(String)`\n- [ ] A dictionary\n- [ ] A tuple\n> Enums make impossible states impossible, and switch forces you to handle every case.\n\nQ: What is Apple's general advice for choosing between struct and class?\n- [ ] Always use classes\n- [x] Default to structs; use classes when you need shared, identity-based state\n- [ ] Use structs only for numbers\n- [ ] They're interchangeable\n> Value semantics are simpler and safer; classes are for shared mutable state.\n```\n"},{"id":"swift-protocols","title":"Protocols, Extensions & Generics","part":2,"minutes":35,"summary":"The features at the heart of Swift and SwiftUI. Define contracts with protocols, add behaviour with extensions, write reusable code with generics, and understand `some` and `any`.","body":":::goals\n- Defining and adopting protocols\n- Protocol extensions and default implementations\n- The standard protocols: `Equatable`, `Hashable`, `Comparable`, `Identifiable`, `Codable`\n- Extensions on any type\n- Generics, `some` and `any`\n:::\n\n## Protocols\n\nA **protocol** is a contract: a list of properties and methods a type promises to provide. It says *what* something can do, not *how*.\n\n```swift runnable\nprotocol Describable {\n    var title: String { get }          // a read-only property requirement\n    func describe() -> String          // a method requirement\n}\n\nstruct Recipe: Describable {           // \"Recipe conforms to Describable\"\n    let title: String\n    let minutes: Int\n    func describe() -> String { \"\\(title), ready in \\(minutes) min\" }\n}\n\nstruct Article: Describable {\n    let title: String\n    let author: String\n    func describe() -> String { \"\\(title) by \\(author)\" }\n}\n\nlet things: [any Describable] = [\n    Recipe(title: \"Ramen\", minutes: 30),\n    Article(title: \"Why Swift?\", author: \"Ada\")\n]\nfor thing in things {\n    print(thing.describe())\n}\n```\n\nAny type (struct, class or enum) can conform to any number of protocols. You've already used one: every SwiftUI view is a struct conforming to the `View` protocol, whose single requirement is `var body: some View`.\n\n:::note Why protocols matter so much\nArchitecture relies on them. A view model can depend on a protocol like `RecipeService`: the real app passes an implementation that calls the network, while previews and tests pass a fake with sample data. You'll do exactly this in Parts 4, 5 and 7.\n:::\n\n## Protocol extensions: default implementations\n\nYou can **extend** a protocol to give every conforming type behaviour for free:\n\n```swift runnable\nprotocol Greeter {\n    var name: String { get }\n}\n\nextension Greeter {\n    func greet() -> String { \"Hello, I'm \\(name)!\" }   // every Greeter gets this\n}\n\nstruct Person: Greeter { let name: String }\nstruct Robot: Greeter {\n    let name: String\n    func greet() -> String { \"BEEP. UNIT \\(name.uppercased()) ONLINE.\" }   // customise\n}\n\nprint(Person(name: \"Ada\").greet())\nprint(Robot(name: \"r2\").greet())\n```\n\nThis is how SwiftUI gives every view hundreds of modifiers like `.padding()` and `.font()`: they're defined in an extension on `View`.\n\n## Standard protocols you'll use constantly\n\n```swift runnable\nstruct Ingredient: Equatable, Hashable, Comparable, Identifiable, Codable {\n    let id: Int\n    let name: String\n\n    static func < (lhs: Ingredient, rhs: Ingredient) -> Bool {   // Comparable\n        lhs.name < rhs.name\n    }\n}\n\nlet flour = Ingredient(id: 1, name: \"Flour\")\nlet eggs = Ingredient(id: 2, name: \"Eggs\")\n\nprint(flour == Ingredient(id: 1, name: \"Flour\"))      // Equatable: == generated for you\nprint(Set([flour, eggs, flour]).count)                // Hashable: usable in sets and as dict keys\nprint([flour, eggs].sorted().map(\\.name))             // Comparable: sorted() works\nprint(flour.id)                                       // Identifiable: has a stable id\n```\n\n| Protocol | Gives you | Used by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Equatable` | `==` (usually generated automatically) | comparisons, `.onChange`, animations |\n| `Hashable` | use in `Set` and as dictionary keys | navigation values, `ForEach` |\n| `Comparable` | `<`, so `sorted()` and `max()` work | sorting |\n| `Identifiable` | a stable `id` property | `List` and `ForEach` in SwiftUI |\n| `Codable` | conversion to and from JSON | networking (Lesson 23) |\n| `CustomStringConvertible` | a custom `description` for printing | debugging |\n\nFor structs and enums, Swift **synthesises** `Equatable`, `Hashable` and `Codable` automatically when all their properties conform. You just write the protocol name.\n\n## Extensions\n\nAn **extension** adds functionality to any type, including ones you don't own, like `String`, `Int` or `Date`:\n\n```swift runnable\nextension Int {\n    var minutesText: String {\n        let h = self / 60, m = self % 60\n        return h > 0 ? \"\\(h)h \\(m)m\" : \"\\(m) min\"\n    }\n}\n\nextension String {\n    var isValidEmail: Bool {\n        contains(\"@\") && (split(separator: \"@\").last?.contains(\".\") ?? false)\n    }\n}\n\nextension Array where Element == Int {              // only for arrays of Ints\n    var average: Double { isEmpty ? 0 : Double(reduce(0, +)) / Double(count) }\n}\n\nprint(135.minutesText)\nprint(45.minutesText)\nprint(\"ada@example.com\".isValidEmail)\nprint(\"nope\".isValidEmail)\nprint([4, 8, 6].average)\n```\n\nExtensions are also great for **organising** code: many developers put each protocol conformance in its own extension (`extension Recipe: Codable { … }`).\n\n## Generics\n\n**Generics** let you write code that works with any type while staying type-safe. You've been using them all along: `Array<Element>`, `Dictionary<Key, Value>`, `Optional<Wrapped>`.\n\n```swift runnable\n// A generic function: works for any T\nfunc firstOrDefault<T>(_ items: [T], default fallback: T) -> T {\n    items.first ?? fallback\n}\n\n// With a constraint: T must be Comparable\nfunc largest<T: Comparable>(_ items: [T]) -> T? {\n    items.max()\n}\n\n// A generic type\nstruct Box<Content> {\n    let content: Content\n    func describe() -> String { \"Box containing \\(content)\" }\n}\n\nprint(firstOrDefault([3, 4], default: 0))\nprint(firstOrDefault([String](), default: \"nothing\"))\nprint(largest([\"pear\", \"apple\", \"zucchini\"]) ?? \"\")\nprint(Box(content: 42).describe())\nprint(Box(content: [\"a\", \"b\"]).describe())\n```\n\n## `some` and `any`\n\nYou'll see both keywords in SwiftUI and modern Swift:\n\n- **`some Protocol`** (an *opaque type*) means \"one specific type that conforms, known to the compiler but not written out.\" `var body: some View` returns one concrete view type, which could be a `VStack` of many things, without you spelling out its huge generic type name. It's fast because the compiler knows the exact type.\n- **`any Protocol`** (an *existential*) means \"a box that can hold *any* conforming type, and which one can vary at runtime.\" Use it for mixed collections like `[any Describable]`, or for stored dependencies like `let service: any RecipeService`.\n\n```swift runnable\nprotocol Shape { func area() -> Double }\nstruct Circle: Shape { let r: Double; func area() -> Double { 3.14159 * r * r } }\nstruct Square: Shape { let side: Double; func area() -> Double { side * side } }\n\nfunc makeUnitSquare() -> some Shape { Square(side: 1) }     // always the same concrete type\n\nlet shapes: [any Shape] = [Circle(r: 1), Square(side: 2)]  // mixed types\nprint(makeUnitSquare().area())\nprint(shapes.map { $0.area() }.reduce(0, +))\n```\n\n:::tip Rule of thumb\nReturning a view or a single concrete type → `some`. Storing different types together, or a dependency that could be real or fake → `any`.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- A **protocol** is a contract; types conform by implementing its requirements.\n- **Protocol extensions** provide default behaviour to every conforming type.\n- `Equatable`, `Hashable`, `Identifiable` and `Codable` are often synthesised for free.\n- **Extensions** add computed properties and methods to existing types.\n- **Generics** (`<T>`) make reusable, type-safe code; `some` hides a single concrete type, `any` holds any conforming type.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Define `protocol Shape { var name: String { get }; func area() -> Double }` and add a protocol extension method `func summary() -> String` returning e.g. `\"Circle: 3.14\"`. Create `Circle` and `Rectangle` and print the summaries of an `[any Shape]`.\n2. Add an extension to `Double` with a computed property `rounded2: String` that formats to two decimal places. (Hint: build it with `(self * 100).rounded() / 100`.)\n3. Write a generic function `func countMatches<T: Equatable>(_ items: [T], of target: T) -> Int`.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift runnable\nprotocol Shape {\n    var name: String { get }\n    func area() -> Double\n}\n\nextension Shape {\n    func summary() -> String { \"\\(name): \\(area().rounded2)\" }\n}\n\nstruct Circle: Shape {\n    let radius: Double\n    var name: String { \"Circle\" }\n    func area() -> Double { Double.pi * radius * radius }\n}\n\nstruct Rectangle: Shape {\n    let width, height: Double\n    var name: String { \"Rectangle\" }\n    func area() -> Double { width * height }\n}\n\nextension Double {\n    var rounded2: String { String((self * 100).rounded() / 100) }\n}\n\nfunc countMatches<T: Equatable>(_ items: [T], of target: T) -> Int {\n    items.filter { $0 == target }.count\n}\n\nlet shapes: [any Shape] = [Circle(radius: 1), Rectangle(width: 2, height: 3.5)]\nshapes.forEach { print($0.summary()) }\nprint(countMatches([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"], of: \"a\"))\nprint(countMatches([1, 2, 3, 2, 2], of: 2))\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What is a protocol?\n- [ ] A kind of class\n- [x] A contract of properties and methods that conforming types must provide\n- [ ] A network standard\n- [ ] A SwiftUI view\n> Types adopt protocols and implement their requirements.\n\nQ: What does `var body: some View` mean?\n- [ ] body can return a different view type each time it runs\n- [x] body returns one specific concrete View type that the compiler knows but you don't have to write out\n- [ ] body returns an optional view\n- [ ] body is any object at all\n> `some` is an opaque type: one fixed type hidden behind the protocol.\n\nQ: Which protocol does SwiftUI's `ForEach` want your data to conform to so it can track items?\n- [ ] Codable\n- [ ] Comparable\n- [x] Identifiable\n- [ ] CustomStringConvertible\n> Identifiable provides a stable `id` so SwiftUI can tell items apart.\n\nQ: How do you add a new computed property to `Int`, a type you don't own?\n- [ ] Subclass Int\n- [x] Write an extension on Int\n- [ ] It's impossible\n- [ ] Use a protocol\n> Extensions add functionality to existing types without modifying their source.\n```\n"},{"id":"swift-errors","title":"Error Handling","part":2,"minutes":20,"summary":"Things go wrong, like no internet, bad input or missing files. Learn Swift's explicit error handling with throws, do/catch, try?, custom errors and Result.","body":":::goals\n- Defining errors with enums that conform to `Error`\n- Throwing functions and `do` / `try` / `catch`\n- `try?` and `try!`\n- Typed throws\n- `defer` for cleanup, and `Result`\n:::\n\n## Defining errors\n\nAn error is any type conforming to the `Error` protocol. Enums are ideal because they list everything that can go wrong:\n\n```swift\nenum LoginError: Error {\n    case emptyPassword\n    case tooShort(minimum: Int)\n    case accountLocked\n}\n```\n\n## Throwing functions\n\nA function that can fail is marked **`throws`**. It uses **`throw`** to report an error, which immediately exits the function:\n\n```swift runnable\nenum LoginError: Error {\n    case emptyPassword\n    case tooShort(minimum: Int)\n}\n\nfunc validate(password: String) throws -> Bool {\n    if password.isEmpty { throw LoginError.emptyPassword }\n    if password.count < 8 { throw LoginError.tooShort(minimum: 8) }\n    return true\n}\n\ndo {\n    try validate(password: \"swift\")              // `try` marks the call that can throw\n    print(\"Password OK\")                         // skipped if it threw\n} catch LoginError.emptyPassword {\n    print(\"Please enter a password.\")\n} catch LoginError.tooShort(let minimum) {\n    print(\"Password must be at least \\(minimum) characters.\")\n} catch {\n    print(\"Unexpected error: \\(error)\")          // `error` is available automatically\n}\n```\n\nSwift makes errors **visible**: you can't call a throwing function without writing `try`, and you must handle the error or pass it on (by marking your own function `throws`).\n\n## `try?` and `try!`\n\n```swift runnable\nenum ParseError: Error { case notANumber }\n\nfunc parse(_ text: String) throws -> Int {\n    guard let n = Int(text) else { throw ParseError.notANumber }\n    return n\n}\n\nlet a = try? parse(\"42\")      // Optional(42)\nlet b = try? parse(\"x\")       // nil: the error is discarded\nprint(a ?? -1, b ?? -1)\n\nlet c = try! parse(\"7\")       // crashes if it throws, so only use when failure is impossible\nprint(c)\n```\n\n- **`try?`** turns the result into an optional: `nil` if it threw. It's convenient when you don't care *why* it failed.\n- **`try!`** crashes on error. Avoid it, just like `!` for optionals.\n\n## User-friendly messages\n\nConform to `LocalizedError` to give each error a message you can show in an alert:\n\n```swift runnable\nimport Foundation\n\nenum NetworkError: LocalizedError {\n    case offline\n    case server(code: Int)\n\n    var errorDescription: String? {\n        switch self {\n        case .offline: \"You're offline. Check your connection and try again.\"\n        case .server(let code): \"The server had a problem (\\(code)). Please try later.\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nlet error: Error = NetworkError.server(code: 503)\nprint(error.localizedDescription)\n```\n\n## Typed throws\n\nBy default, a throwing function can throw *any* `Error`. Swift 6 lets you declare exactly which type, so callers get that specific type in `catch`:\n\n```swift runnable\nenum ValidationError: Error { case empty, tooLong(max: Int) }\n\nfunc validateName(_ name: String) throws(ValidationError) -> String {\n    if name.isEmpty { throw .empty }\n    if name.count > 20 { throw .tooLong(max: 20) }\n    return name\n}\n\ndo {\n    _ = try validateName(\"\")\n} catch {                                 // error is a ValidationError here, not `any Error`\n    switch error {\n    case .empty: print(\"Name can't be empty\")\n    case .tooLong(let max): print(\"Max \\(max) characters\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\nTyped throws are handy for small, closed sets of errors. For most app code, plain `throws` is fine.\n\n## `defer`: always clean up\n\nA `defer` block runs when the current scope exits, however it exits (return, throw or reaching the end):\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc process() throws {\n    print(\"Start loading\")\n    defer { print(\"Stop loading spinner\") }   // runs last, even if we throw\n    throw URLErrorLike.timeout\n}\nenum URLErrorLike: Error { case timeout }\n\ndo { try process() } catch { print(\"Caught: \\(error)\") }\n```\n\nYou'll use `defer { isLoading = false }` in view models so the spinner always stops.\n\n## `Result`\n\n`Result<Success, Failure>` stores either a value or an error. It's useful when you need to keep an outcome and look at it later:\n\n```swift runnable\nenum MathError: Error { case divideByZero }\n\nfunc divide(_ a: Int, by b: Int) -> Result<Int, MathError> {\n    b == 0 ? .failure(.divideByZero) : .success(a / b)\n}\n\nfor result in [divide(10, by: 2), divide(1, by: 0)] {\n    switch result {\n    case .success(let value): print(\"Answer: \\(value)\")\n    case .failure(let error): print(\"Oops: \\(error)\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWith `async`/`await` (next lesson), plain `throws` is usually cleaner than `Result`.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Errors conform to `Error`, and enums are ideal.\n- `throws` + `throw` to fail; call with `try` inside `do { } catch { }`.\n- `try?` gives an optional; `try!` crashes, so avoid it.\n- `LocalizedError` provides user-facing messages; `defer` guarantees cleanup.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\nWrite `func withdraw(_ amount: Double, from balance: Double) throws -> Double` that throws `BankError.invalidAmount` for amounts ≤ 0 and `BankError.insufficientFunds(needed: Double)` when the balance is too low. Make `BankError` a `LocalizedError` with friendly messages, and call it for three cases inside `do/catch`, printing `error.localizedDescription`.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift runnable\nimport Foundation\n\nenum BankError: LocalizedError {\n    case invalidAmount\n    case insufficientFunds(needed: Double)\n\n    var errorDescription: String? {\n        switch self {\n        case .invalidAmount: \"Enter an amount greater than zero.\"\n        case .insufficientFunds(let needed): \"You need \\(needed) more to do that.\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfunc withdraw(_ amount: Double, from balance: Double) throws -> Double {\n    guard amount > 0 else { throw BankError.invalidAmount }\n    guard amount <= balance else { throw BankError.insufficientFunds(needed: amount - balance) }\n    return balance - amount\n}\n\nfor amount in [30.0, -5, 500] {\n    do {\n        let newBalance = try withdraw(amount, from: 100)\n        print(\"New balance: \\(newBalance)\")\n    } catch {\n        print(error.localizedDescription)\n    }\n}\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What keyword must you write before calling a throwing function?\n- [ ] throw\n- [x] try\n- [ ] catch\n- [ ] await\n> `try` marks every call that can throw, so errors are always visible in the code.\n\nQ: What does `try? parse(\"x\")` return if parse throws?\n- [ ] It crashes\n- [x] nil\n- [ ] An empty string\n- [ ] The error\n> try? converts errors into nil.\n\nQ: When does a `defer` block run?\n- [ ] Immediately\n- [ ] Only if an error is thrown\n- [x] When the enclosing scope exits, however it exits\n- [ ] At app launch\n> defer is perfect for cleanup that must always happen.\n```\n"},{"id":"swift-concurrency","title":"Concurrency: async/await & Actors","part":2,"minutes":40,"summary":"iOS apps constantly wait for the network, the disk and the database. Swift Concurrency lets you write that code simply and safely, without freezing the UI.","body":":::goals\n- Why the main thread must never be blocked\n- `async` functions and `await`\n- Running work in parallel with `async let` and task groups\n- `Task`, cancellation and the `.task` modifier preview\n- The main actor, actors and data-race safety in Swift 6\n- `AsyncSequence` basics\n:::\n\n## The problem: the main thread\n\nEvery iOS app has a **main thread** that draws the UI and handles touches, 60–120 times per second. If it has to wait for something slow, like a network request or a big file, the app freezes and feels broken. iOS may even terminate an app that hangs for too long.\n\n**Swift Concurrency** lets you write asynchronous code that reads top to bottom, while the system keeps the UI responsive:\n\n```swift\nfunc loadProfile() async throws -> Profile {\n    let user = try await api.fetchUser()          // suspends while waiting, doesn't block\n    let posts = try await api.fetchPosts(for: user.id)\n    return Profile(user: user, posts: posts)\n}\n```\n\n## `async` and `await`\n\n- An **`async`** function can *suspend*: pause while it waits, freeing the thread for other work, and resume later.\n- You call it with **`await`**, which marks each point where suspension can happen.\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc fetchUserName() async -> String {\n    try? await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(1))   // pretend network delay (suspends, doesn't block)\n    return \"Ada\"\n}\n\nprint(\"Fetching…\")\nlet name = await fetchUserName()              // top-level code can await in a script\nprint(\"Got \\(name)\")\n```\n\nFunctions can be both: `async throws`, called with `try await`.\n\n:::analogy\nA blocked thread is a chef staring at the oven for 20 minutes. With `await`, the chef puts the dish in, goes off to chop vegetables for other orders, and comes back when the timer rings.\n:::\n\n## Parallel work: `async let`\n\n`await` calls run one after another. To start several at once and wait for all of them, use **`async let`**:\n\n```swift runnable\nimport Foundation\n\nfunc fetchWeather() async -> String { try? await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(1)); return \"☀️ 24°C\" }\nfunc fetchNews() async -> String { try? await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(1)); return \"📰 Local team wins\" }\n\nlet clock = ContinuousClock()\n\nlet sequential = await clock.measure {\n    let w = await fetchWeather()\n    let n = await fetchNews()\n    print(w, \"|\", n)\n}\nprint(\"Sequential:\", sequential)        // ~2 seconds\n\nlet parallel = await clock.measure {\n    async let w = fetchWeather()        // starts immediately\n    async let n = fetchNews()           // starts immediately\n    print(await w, \"|\", await n)        // wait for both\n}\nprint(\"Parallel:\", parallel)            // ~1 second\n```\n\nFor a *variable* number of parallel jobs (e.g. download 20 images), use a **task group**:\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc price(of item: String) async -> Double {\n    try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(300))\n    return Double(item.count)\n}\n\nlet total = await withTaskGroup(of: Double.self) { group in\n    for item in [\"apple\", \"bread\", \"cheese\"] {\n        group.addTask { await price(of: item) }\n    }\n    var sum = 0.0\n    for await p in group { sum += p }\n    return sum\n}\nprint(\"Total:\", total)\n```\n\n## `Task`: starting async work from sync code\n\nYou can only `await` inside an async context. To start async work from normal code, such as a button tap, create a **`Task`**:\n\n```swift\nButton(\"Refresh\") {\n    Task {\n        await viewModel.refresh()\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn SwiftUI you'll more often use the **`.task` modifier**, which starts work when a view appears and **cancels it automatically** when the view disappears:\n\n```swift\nList(recipes) { … }\n    .task {                          // runs when the view appears\n        await viewModel.load()\n    }\n```\n\n### Cancellation\n\nTasks can be cancelled; well-behaved async code checks and stops early. `Task.sleep` and `URLSession` throw `CancellationError` when cancelled.\n\n```swift runnable\nlet task = Task {\n    for i in 1...10 {\n        guard !Task.isCancelled else { print(\"Stopped early\"); return }\n        print(\"Working \\(i)…\")\n        try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(200))\n    }\n}\ntry? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(700))\ntask.cancel()\n_ = await task.value\nprint(\"Done\")\n```\n\n## The main actor\n\nUI code must run on the main thread. In Swift this is expressed with the **main actor**: mark a type or function **`@MainActor`** and Swift guarantees it runs on the main thread. The compiler enforces it.\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class RecipesViewModel {\n    var recipes: [Recipe] = []           // UI state: only touched on the main actor\n\n    func load() async {\n        do {\n            recipes = try await service.fetchRecipes()   // the network call suspends; the UI stays responsive\n        } catch {\n            // show an error\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSwiftUI views run on the main actor. And **new app projects in recent Xcode versions make the main actor the default** for your app's code (a build setting called *Default Actor Isolation*). That keeps beginner code simple and safe: everything runs on the main actor unless you opt out, and slow `await`ed work (networking, file I/O done by Apple's APIs) happens elsewhere without blocking it.\n\n## Actors and data-race safety\n\nA **data race** happens when two threads access the same mutable data at the same time, causing random, hard-to-find bugs. Swift 6 **checks for data races at compile time**.\n\nAn **`actor`** is like a class that protects its mutable state: only one task can use it at a time, and outside code must `await` to access it.\n\n```swift runnable\nactor ImageCache {\n    private var storage: [String: String] = [:]\n\n    func image(for url: String) -> String? { storage[url] }\n    func insert(_ image: String, for url: String) { storage[url] = image }\n    var count: Int { storage.count }\n}\n\nlet cache = ImageCache()\n\nawait withTaskGroup(of: Void.self) { group in\n    for i in 1...100 {                         // 100 concurrent writes, zero data races\n        group.addTask { await cache.insert(\"img\\(i)\", for: \"url\\(i)\") }\n    }\n}\nprint(await cache.count)                       // always 100\nprint(await cache.image(for: \"url42\") ?? \"missing\")\n```\n\nValues passed between concurrent code must be **`Sendable`**, meaning safe to share. Structs and enums made of `Sendable` values are automatically `Sendable`. When the compiler complains about sending something non-Sendable across actors, it's catching a real potential bug.\n\n:::tip Don't panic about concurrency warnings\nSwift 6's checks can feel strict at first. The usual fixes: keep UI state in `@MainActor` types, use structs for data passed around, and use an `actor` for shared mutable state that background work touches.\n:::\n\n## `AsyncSequence`: values over time\n\nAn `AsyncSequence` delivers values over time, and you loop over it with `for await`:\n\n```swift runnable\nfunc countdown(from n: Int) -> AsyncStream<Int> {\n    AsyncStream { continuation in\n        Task {\n            for i in stride(from: n, through: 0, by: -1) {\n                continuation.yield(i)\n                try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(300))\n            }\n            continuation.finish()\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfor await value in countdown(from: 3) {\n    print(\"T-minus \\(value)\")\n}\nprint(\"🚀\")\n```\n\nApple APIs expose many of these: notifications, location updates, lines of a file, bytes of a download.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Never block the main thread; `await` suspends without blocking.\n- `async let` and task groups run work in parallel.\n- `Task { }` starts async work from sync code; SwiftUI's `.task` ties work to a view's lifetime and cancels it automatically.\n- `@MainActor` code runs on the main thread, which is where UI state lives.\n- `actor` protects shared mutable state; Swift 6 catches data races at compile time.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Write `func fetchScore(for player: String) async -> Int` that sleeps for 500 ms and returns `player.count * 10`. Fetch scores for three players **in parallel** with `async let` and print the total. Measure it with `ContinuousClock`.\n2. Create an `actor Counter` with `increment()` and `value`. Increment it 1,000 times from a task group and print the final value.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift runnable\nfunc fetchScore(for player: String) async -> Int {\n    try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(500))\n    return player.count * 10\n}\n\nactor Counter {\n    private(set) var value = 0\n    func increment() { value += 1 }\n}\n\nlet elapsed = await ContinuousClock().measure {\n    async let a = fetchScore(for: \"Ada\")\n    async let b = fetchScore(for: \"Grace\")\n    async let c = fetchScore(for: \"Linus\")\n    let total = await a + b + c\n    print(\"Total score:\", total)\n}\nprint(\"Took\", elapsed, \"(about 0.5s, not 1.5s)\")\n\nlet counter = Counter()\nawait withTaskGroup(of: Void.self) { group in\n    for _ in 1...1000 {\n        group.addTask { await counter.increment() }\n    }\n}\nprint(\"Counter:\", await counter.value)\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What does `await` do?\n- [ ] Blocks the thread until the result arrives\n- [x] Marks a point where the function may suspend while waiting, freeing the thread for other work\n- [ ] Runs code on a background thread\n- [ ] Cancels a task\n> Suspension is not blocking: other work continues on the thread.\n\nQ: You need to call two independent async functions and use both results. How do you run them at the same time?\n- [ ] Call them with await one after another\n- [x] Use `async let` for each, then await both\n- [ ] Use DispatchQueue.main.sync\n- [ ] Wrap each in try!\n> async let starts child tasks immediately; awaiting collects the results.\n\nQ: What's the advantage of SwiftUI's `.task` modifier over `Task { }` in `onAppear`?\n- [ ] It runs faster\n- [x] The work is automatically cancelled when the view disappears\n- [ ] It runs on a background thread\n- [ ] It retries on failure\n> .task ties the work's lifetime to the view's lifetime.\n\nQ: What does an `actor` guarantee?\n- [ ] Its code runs on the main thread\n- [x] Only one task at a time can access its mutable state, preventing data races\n- [ ] It never suspends\n- [ ] It's always faster than a class\n> Actors serialise access to their state; outside callers must await.\n\nQ: Where should UI state like a view model's `recipes` array live?\n- [ ] In an actor on a background thread\n- [x] In a `@MainActor` type, since UI updates must happen on the main thread\n- [ ] In a global variable\n- [ ] Anywhere, SwiftUI doesn't care\n> SwiftUI reads state on the main actor; keeping UI state there avoids data races.\n```\n"},{"id":"swiftui-intro","title":"Thinking in SwiftUI","part":3,"minutes":30,"summary":"SwiftUI is Apple's modern, declarative UI framework. Learn the mindset, how views and modifiers work, how SwiftUI updates the screen when state changes, and how to use previews.","body":":::goals\n- Imperative vs. declarative UI, and why SwiftUI is declarative\n- Views as lightweight structs with a `body`\n- How modifiers work and why they return new views\n- Your first interactive view with `@State`\n- Composing screens from small views, and previews\n:::\n\n## Imperative vs. declarative\n\nIn UIKit, the older framework, you build views once and then **change them manually** whenever data changes:\n\n```swift\n// Imperative (UIKit)\n@objc func buttonTapped() {\n    count += 1\n    label.text = \"Tapped \\(count) times\"      // YOU must remember to update every label\n    label.textColor = count > 5 ? .red : .label\n}\n```\n\nAs apps grow, keeping every view in sync with the data becomes a major source of bugs.\n\n**SwiftUI is declarative**: you describe what the UI should look like **for the current state**, and SwiftUI keeps the screen in sync whenever the state changes.\n\n```swift\n// Declarative (SwiftUI)\nText(\"Tapped \\(count) times\")\n    .foregroundStyle(count > 5 ? .red : .primary)\n```\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div class=\"hl\">State<small>your data</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>body<small>UI = f(state)</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Screen</div>\n</div><div class=\"diagram-caption\">Change the state, and SwiftUI recomputes the body and updates only what changed.</div></div>\n\n:::analogy\nImperative UI is giving turn-by-turn directions. Declarative UI is giving the destination address: you say **what** you want, and SwiftUI works out **how** to get the screen there.\n:::\n\n## Views are structs\n\nA SwiftUI view is a small struct that conforms to `View` and has a `body`:\n\n```swift title=\"GreetingView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct GreetingView: View {\n    let name: String                      // data it needs, passed in\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Text(\"Hello, \\(name)!\")\n            .font(.title)\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    GreetingView(name: \"Ada\")\n}\n```\n\nThings to notice:\n\n- Views are **cheap values**, not heavy objects. SwiftUI creates and throws them away constantly. Don't worry about \"creating too many views\".\n- `body` is a **computed property**. SwiftUI calls it whenever it needs to know what to display, so keep it fast and free of side effects (no network calls in `body`!).\n- `some View` means \"one specific view type\" (Lesson 9). You don't need to know the exact type.\n\n## Composing views\n\nBuild screens by **combining small views**:\n\n```swift title=\"WelcomeView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct WelcomeView: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 12) {\n            Image(systemName: \"sparkles\")\n                .font(.system(size: 48))\n                .foregroundStyle(.orange)\n            GreetingView(name: \"Ada\")\n            GreetingView(name: \"Grace\")\n            Text(\"Let's build something great.\")\n                .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    WelcomeView()\n}\n```\n\nThe `VStack { … }` part uses a **result builder**: inside the braces you just list views, and SwiftUI assembles them. You can also use `if`, `if let`, `switch` and `ForEach` inside a view builder:\n\n```swift\nVStack {\n    if isLoggedIn {\n        Text(\"Welcome back!\")\n    } else {\n        Button(\"Sign in\") { signIn() }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Modifiers\n\n**Modifiers** customise a view: font, colour, padding, background, frame and so on. Each modifier **returns a new view** that wraps the original:\n\n```swift\nText(\"Swift\")\n    .font(.largeTitle.bold())\n    .foregroundStyle(.white)\n    .padding()\n    .background(.orange)\n    .clipShape(.rect(cornerRadius: 12))\n```\n\nBecause each modifier wraps the previous result, **order matters**:\n\n```swift title=\"ModifierOrderView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct ModifierOrderView: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 20) {\n            Text(\"Padding, then background\")\n                .padding()\n                .background(.orange)          // orange covers the padding too\n\n            Text(\"Background, then padding\")\n                .background(.orange)          // orange hugs the text only\n                .padding()\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { ModifierOrderView() }\n```\n\n:::tip Discover modifiers\nPress [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[L]] to open the Library and browse views and modifiers, or select a view in the canvas and use the **Attributes inspector** on the right. Xcode writes the code for you, and you can learn from what it writes.\n:::\n\n## Your first interactive view: `@State`\n\n```swift title=\"CounterView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct CounterView: View {\n    @State private var count = 0              // state SwiftUI watches\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 16) {\n            Text(\"You tapped \\(count) times\")\n                .font(.title2)\n                .foregroundStyle(count > 10 ? .red : .primary)\n\n            Button(\"Tap me\") {\n                count += 1                    // change state → body re-runs → screen updates\n            }\n            .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { CounterView() }\n```\n\nWhat happens when you tap:\n\n1. `count += 1` changes the **state**.\n2. SwiftUI notices that `body` reads `count` and re-evaluates `body`.\n3. It compares the new description with the old one and updates only the `Text` that changed.\n\nYou never told the label to update. You changed the state, and the UI followed. ✨ We'll dig into `@State` and data flow in Lesson 15.\n\n:::note Why can't I just use `var count = 0`?\nA view struct is recreated all the time, so a plain property would reset, and structs can't change their own properties from inside `body` anyway. `@State` stores the value *outside* the struct, in storage SwiftUI manages, and tells SwiftUI to refresh when it changes.\n:::\n\n## Previews\n\n`#Preview` shows your view in Xcode's canvas. You can have several, with different data and settings:\n\n```swift\n#Preview(\"Short name\") {\n    GreetingView(name: \"Al\")\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Long name, dark\") {\n    GreetingView(name: \"Maximilian Alexander Featherstonehaugh\")\n        .preferredColorScheme(.dark)\n}\n```\n\nUse the controls under the canvas to try **Dynamic Type sizes**, **orientation**, **dark mode** and different **devices**. Previews are live, so buttons work in them.\n\n## Extracting subviews\n\nWhen `body` gets long, extract parts into their own views. Right-click a view in the editor and choose **Refactor → Extract Subview**, or write it by hand:\n\n```swift title=\"ProfileCard.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct ProfileCard: View {\n    let name: String\n    let role: String\n    let isFollowing: Bool\n    let onFollow: () -> Void          // an event it reports to its parent\n\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack {\n            Text(name.prefix(1))\n                .font(.title.bold())\n                .frame(width: 56, height: 56)\n                .background(.orange.gradient, in: .circle)\n                .foregroundStyle(.white)\n            VStack(alignment: .leading) {\n                Text(name).font(.headline)\n                Text(role).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n            }\n            Spacer()\n            Button(isFollowing ? \"Following\" : \"Follow\", action: onFollow)\n                .buttonStyle(.bordered)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    ProfileCard(name: \"Ada Lovelace\", role: \"iOS Engineer\", isFollowing: false, onFollow: {})\n}\n```\n\nThis shape (**data in, events out as closures**) is the convention for reusable views. You'll write hundreds of them.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- SwiftUI is **declarative**: describe the UI for the current state; SwiftUI keeps it in sync.\n- A view is a lightweight struct with a `body`. Keep `body` fast and side-effect free.\n- Modifiers wrap views and return new ones, so **order matters**.\n- `@State` holds local state; changing it re-renders the view.\n- Compose screens from small views that take data and closures.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Build a `BusinessCard` view with an SF Symbol, your name (`.title`) and job title (`.secondary`) in a `VStack`, with padding and a rounded background. Add light and dark previews.\n2. Extend `CounterView` with a **Reset** button that sets the count to 0, disabled when the count is already 0 (`.disabled(count == 0)`).\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct BusinessCard: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 8) {\n            Image(systemName: \"swift\")\n                .font(.system(size: 40))\n                .foregroundStyle(.orange)\n            Text(\"Ada Lovelace\").font(.title)\n            Text(\"iOS Developer\").foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n        }\n        .padding(24)\n        .background(.background.secondary, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 20))\n    }\n}\n\nstruct CounterView: View {\n    @State private var count = 0\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 16) {\n            Text(\"You tapped \\(count) times\").font(.title2)\n            HStack {\n                Button(\"Tap me\") { count += 1 }\n                    .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n                Button(\"Reset\") { count = 0 }\n                    .buttonStyle(.bordered)\n                    .disabled(count == 0)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Card\") { BusinessCard() }\n#Preview(\"Card, dark\") { BusinessCard().preferredColorScheme(.dark) }\n#Preview(\"Counter\") { CounterView() }\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What does \"declarative UI\" mean in SwiftUI?\n- [ ] You write layouts in XML\n- [x] You describe the UI for the current state, and SwiftUI updates the screen when state changes\n- [ ] You manually update each label\n- [ ] UI is designed in Interface Builder only\n> UI = f(state).\n\nQ: Why is order important in `.padding().background(.orange)` vs `.background(.orange).padding()`?\n- [ ] It isn't\n- [x] Each modifier wraps the result of the previous one, so the background covers different areas\n- [ ] The second one doesn't compile\n- [ ] Only the last modifier is applied\n> Modifiers apply from the inside out.\n\nQ: Why is `@State` needed for a counter instead of a plain `var`?\n- [ ] @State makes it faster\n- [x] SwiftUI stores the value outside the recreated view struct and refreshes the view when it changes\n- [ ] Plain properties can't hold Ints\n- [ ] @State saves the value to disk\n> Views are recreated constantly; @State gives them persistent, observed storage.\n\nQ: What should you never do inside `body`?\n- [ ] Use if statements\n- [ ] Call other views\n- [x] Start network requests or other slow side effects\n- [ ] Use modifiers\n> body can run many times; side effects belong in actions, `.task` or view models.\n```\n"},{"id":"swiftui-layout","title":"Layout with Stacks, Frames & Spacing","part":3,"minutes":35,"summary":"Arrange views with VStack, HStack and ZStack, control size with frames, spacing and padding, and understand how SwiftUI's layout system actually decides sizes.","body":":::goals\n- `VStack`, `HStack`, `ZStack`, and alignment and spacing\n- `Spacer`, `Divider`, `padding`, `frame`\n- How SwiftUI layout works: parents propose, children choose\n- `background` and `overlay`\n- Safe areas, `ScrollView` and `ViewThatFits`\n- Building a real card layout\n:::\n\n## The three stacks\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div class=\"hl\">VStack<small>children top to bottom ↓</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">HStack<small>children left to right →</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">ZStack<small>children layered back to front ⧉</small></div>\n</div></div>\n\n```swift title=\"StacksDemo.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct StacksDemo: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 16) {       // alignment + spacing\n            Text(\"Title\").font(.title)\n            HStack(spacing: 8) {\n                Image(systemName: \"star.fill\")\n                Text(\"4.8\")\n                Text(\"·\")\n                Text(\"Italian\")\n            }\n            .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n            ZStack(alignment: .bottomLeading) {\n                RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 16)\n                    .fill(.orange.gradient)\n                    .frame(height: 140)\n                Text(\"Caption on top\")\n                    .foregroundStyle(.white)\n                    .padding()\n            }\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { StacksDemo() }\n```\n\n| Stack | `alignment` controls | Options |\n|---|---|---|\n| `VStack` | horizontal alignment | `.leading`, `.center` (default), `.trailing` |\n| `HStack` | vertical alignment | `.top`, `.center`, `.bottom`, `.firstTextBaseline` |\n| `ZStack` | both | `.topLeading`, `.center`, `.bottomTrailing`, … |\n\nSwiftUI uses **leading/trailing** instead of left/right so layouts flip correctly for right-to-left languages like Arabic and Hebrew.\n\n## `Spacer` and `Divider`\n\nA `Spacer` expands to take all available space along the stack's axis, which pushes views apart:\n\n```swift\nHStack {\n    Text(\"Total\")\n    Spacer()                 // pushes the price to the far right\n    Text(\"$42.00\").bold()\n}\n\nVStack {\n    Text(\"Top\")\n    Spacer()                 // pushes \"Bottom\" to the bottom of the screen\n    Text(\"Bottom\")\n}\n```\n\n`Divider()` draws a thin line: horizontal in a `VStack`, vertical in an `HStack`.\n\n## Padding and frames\n\n```swift\nText(\"Hello\")\n    .padding()                          // system default on all sides\n    .padding(.horizontal, 24)           // specific edges\n    .padding(.top, 8)\n\nText(\"Fixed size\")\n    .frame(width: 200, height: 50)      // propose an exact size\n\nText(\"Full width, leading aligned\")\n    .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)   // take all width available\n\nImage(systemName: \"photo\")\n    .resizable()\n    .scaledToFit()\n    .frame(maxHeight: 120)\n```\n\n## How layout really works\n\nSwiftUI layout is a conversation in three steps:\n\n1. The **parent proposes** a size to its child (\"you can have 390 × 700\").\n2. The **child chooses** its own size (\"I only need 80 × 20\"). A child can't be forced to be bigger than it wants to be.\n3. The **parent positions** the child within its space (using alignment).\n\nThat explains a lot:\n\n- `Text` wants just enough space for its text, so a background only covers the text.\n- `.frame(maxWidth: .infinity)` doesn't stretch the text. It creates a flexible frame *around* it that takes the full width and aligns the text inside it.\n- `Color.orange`, `Spacer()` and `.resizable()` images are flexible: they take whatever they're offered.\n\n## Background and overlay\n\n```swift title=\"BadgeView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct BadgeView: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        Image(systemName: \"bell.fill\")\n            .font(.largeTitle)\n            .padding()\n            .background(.orange.opacity(0.15), in: .circle)          // behind, sized to the view\n            .overlay(alignment: .topTrailing) {                        // in front, sized to the view\n                Text(\"3\")\n                    .font(.caption.bold())\n                    .foregroundStyle(.white)\n                    .padding(6)\n                    .background(.red, in: .circle)\n            }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { BadgeView() }\n```\n\n`background` and `overlay` take the **size of the view they modify**, which is often exactly what you want. `ZStack`, by contrast, sizes itself to fit all its children.\n\n## Shapes\n\n```swift\nCircle().fill(.blue).frame(width: 40, height: 40)\nRoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12).stroke(.gray, lineWidth: 1)\nCapsule().fill(.green.gradient)\nRectangle().frame(height: 1)\n\nText(\"Clipped\")\n    .padding()\n    .background(.yellow)\n    .clipShape(.rect(cornerRadius: 10))          // round the corners of any view\n```\n\n## Safe areas\n\niPhones have a notch or Dynamic Island, a home indicator and rounded corners. SwiftUI keeps content inside the **safe area** automatically. Backgrounds often *should* extend to the edges:\n\n```swift\nZStack {\n    Color.orange.opacity(0.15)\n        .ignoresSafeArea()           // fill the whole screen\n    Text(\"Content stays safe\")\n}\n```\n\n## Scrolling\n\nStacks don't scroll. When content might not fit (and it often won't with large text sizes), wrap it in a `ScrollView`:\n\n```swift\nScrollView {\n    VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 12) {\n        ForEach(1...50, id: \\.self) { i in\n            Text(\"Row \\(i)\")\n        }\n    }\n    .padding()\n}\n```\n\nFor long, data-driven lists, use `List` or `LazyVStack` (Lesson 16), which only create the rows that are visible.\n\n## Adapting to space\n\n`ViewThatFits` picks the first child that fits the available space. It's great for supporting both small phones and huge accessibility text sizes:\n\n```swift\nViewThatFits {\n    HStack { Label(\"Share\", systemImage: \"square.and.arrow.up\"); Label(\"Save\", systemImage: \"heart\") }\n    VStack { Label(\"Share\", systemImage: \"square.and.arrow.up\"); Label(\"Save\", systemImage: \"heart\") }\n}\n```\n\n## Putting it together: a recipe card\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeCardView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct RecipeCardView: View {\n    let title: String\n    let cuisine: String\n    let minutes: Int\n    let rating: Double\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 0) {\n            ZStack(alignment: .topTrailing) {\n                Rectangle()\n                    .fill(.orange.gradient)\n                    .frame(height: 140)\n                    .overlay {\n                        Image(systemName: \"fork.knife\")\n                            .font(.system(size: 44))\n                            .foregroundStyle(.white.opacity(0.8))\n                    }\n                Image(systemName: \"heart\")\n                    .padding(10)\n                    .background(.thinMaterial, in: .circle)\n                    .padding(10)\n            }\n\n            VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 6) {\n                Text(title)\n                    .font(.headline)\n                    .lineLimit(1)\n                HStack(spacing: 12) {\n                    Label(\"\\(minutes) min\", systemImage: \"clock\")\n                    Label(rating.formatted(.number.precision(.fractionLength(1))), systemImage: \"star.fill\")\n                    Spacer()\n                    Text(cuisine)\n                }\n                .font(.caption)\n                .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n            }\n            .padding()\n        }\n        .background(.background, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 20))\n        .clipShape(.rect(cornerRadius: 20))\n        .shadow(color: .black.opacity(0.1), radius: 8, y: 4)\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    RecipeCardView(title: \"Classic Margherita Pizza\", cuisine: \"Italian\", minutes: 35, rating: 4.6)\n        .padding()\n        .background(Color(.systemGroupedBackground))\n}\n```\n\n`Label` combines an icon and text; `.thinMaterial` gives the frosted-glass blur Apple uses throughout iOS; and `formatted(...)` formats numbers correctly for the user's locale.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `VStack`, `HStack`, `ZStack` with `alignment` and `spacing`; `Spacer` pushes views apart.\n- Parents propose sizes, children choose, parents position.\n- `.frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment:)` fills available width; `.padding` adds space.\n- `background`/`overlay` match the modified view's size.\n- Content stays in the safe area; use `ScrollView` when content might not fit.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Build a **settings row**: an SF Symbol in a coloured rounded square on the left, a title and subtitle stacked in the middle, and a `chevron.right` on the far right.\n2. Build a **stats bar** with three equal-width columns (value on top, label below), separated by vertical `Divider`s. (Hint: give each column `.frame(maxWidth: .infinity)`.)\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct SettingsRow: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack(spacing: 12) {\n            Image(systemName: \"bell.badge.fill\")\n                .foregroundStyle(.white)\n                .frame(width: 32, height: 32)\n                .background(.red, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 8))\n            VStack(alignment: .leading) {\n                Text(\"Notifications\").font(.body)\n                Text(\"Daily recipe ideas\").font(.caption).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n            }\n            Spacer()\n            Image(systemName: \"chevron.right\").foregroundStyle(.tertiary)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\nstruct StatsBar: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack {\n            stat(\"35\", \"minutes\")\n            Divider()\n            stat(\"4\", \"servings\")\n            Divider()\n            stat(\"300\", \"kcal\")\n        }\n        .frame(height: 60)\n        .padding(.vertical, 8)\n        .background(.background.secondary, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 16))\n    }\n\n    private func stat(_ value: String, _ label: String) -> some View {\n        VStack {\n            Text(value).font(.title3.bold())\n            Text(label).font(.caption).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n        }\n        .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    VStack { SettingsRow(); StatsBar() }.padding()\n}\n```\nNotice `stat(_:_:)`: a small helper function returning `some View`. It's a lightweight alternative to a separate struct for pieces used only inside one view.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: You want an icon on the left and a price pushed to the far right of a row. What do you put between them in an HStack?\n- [ ] Divider()\n- [x] Spacer()\n- [ ] .padding()\n- [ ] ZStack\n> Spacer expands to fill the available space along the stack's axis.\n\nQ: Why does `.background(.red)` on a Text only colour the text's area?\n- [ ] A bug\n- [x] Text only chooses the size it needs, and the background takes the size of the view it modifies\n- [ ] Backgrounds always fill the screen\n- [ ] You need a ZStack\n> Parents propose, children choose. Text wants only its own size.\n\nQ: How do you make a view take all available width with its content aligned to the leading edge?\n- [x] `.frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)`\n- [ ] `.frame(width: .infinity)`\n- [ ] `.padding(.leading)`\n- [ ] `.alignment(.leading)`\n> A flexible frame fills the width; its alignment positions the content inside.\n\nQ: Why does SwiftUI use `.leading` instead of `.left`?\n- [ ] It's shorter\n- [x] So layouts automatically mirror for right-to-left languages\n- [ ] .left is deprecated in iOS 27\n- [ ] No reason\n> Leading is the start of the reading direction.\n```\n"},{"id":"swiftui-controls","title":"Text, Images & Controls","part":3,"minutes":35,"summary":"A tour of SwiftUI's built-in views, including text and fonts, images and SF Symbols, buttons, text fields, toggles, pickers, sliders and alerts, with Dynamic Type and accessibility in mind.","body":":::goals\n- Text styles and **Dynamic Type**\n- Images, SF Symbols, `Label` and `AsyncImage`\n- Buttons and button styles\n- Text input: `TextField`, `SecureField`, `TextEditor`, and the keyboard\n- `Toggle`, `Slider`, `Stepper`, `Picker`, `DatePicker`\n- Alerts, confirmation dialogs and progress views\n:::\n\n## Text\n\n```swift\nText(\"Recipes\")\n    .font(.largeTitle)                  // a text style, not a fixed size\n    .fontWeight(.bold)\n    .foregroundStyle(.primary)\n    .multilineTextAlignment(.center)\n    .lineLimit(2)\n\nText(\"Ready in **35 minutes** with _4_ ingredients\")    // Markdown works in string literals\n```\n\n### Dynamic Type\n\niPhone users can choose their preferred text size in Settings, from extra small to huge accessibility sizes, and many do. If you use **text styles** (`.largeTitle`, `.title`, `.headline`, `.body`, `.caption`…), your text scales automatically.\n\n| Style | Typical use |\n|---|---|\n| `.largeTitle` | Screen titles |\n| `.title`, `.title2`, `.title3` | Section and card titles |\n| `.headline` | Emphasised row titles |\n| `.body` | Normal text (the default) |\n| `.callout`, `.subheadline`, `.footnote` | Secondary text |\n| `.caption`, `.caption2` | Small labels |\n\n:::danger Avoid fixed font sizes\n`.font(.system(size: 17))` ignores the user's text size setting. Prefer text styles. If you truly need a custom size, use `.font(.system(size: 17, design: .rounded))` sparingly, or scale it with `@ScaledMetric`.\n:::\n\nTest it: in a preview, use the **Variants → Dynamic Type Variants** control under the canvas to see every size at once.\n\n## Images and SF Symbols\n\n```swift\nImage(systemName: \"heart.fill\")              // SF Symbol\n    .font(.title)                            // symbols scale with fonts\n    .foregroundStyle(.red)\n\nImage(systemName: \"cloud.sun.rain.fill\")\n    .symbolRenderingMode(.multicolor)        // symbols can be multicolour\n\nImage(\"pancakes\")                            // from Assets.xcassets\n    .resizable()                             // allow resizing\n    .scaledToFill()                          // fill the frame (may crop)\n    .frame(height: 200)\n    .clipped()\n    .accessibilityLabel(\"A stack of pancakes with berries\")\n\nLabel(\"35 min\", systemImage: \"clock\")        // icon + text, adapts to context\n```\n\nImages from the internet load with `AsyncImage`:\n\n```swift\nAsyncImage(url: URL(string: \"https://cdn.dummyjson.com/recipe-images/1.webp\")) { image in\n    image.resizable().scaledToFill()\n} placeholder: {\n    ProgressView()\n}\n.frame(width: 120, height: 120)\n.clipShape(.rect(cornerRadius: 12))\n```\n\n:::note Accessibility\nVoiceOver reads images aloud using their accessibility label. Add `.accessibilityLabel(\"…\")` to meaningful images, and hide decorative ones with `Image(decorative: \"flourish\")` or `.accessibilityHidden(true)`. SF Symbols get sensible default labels.\n:::\n\n## Buttons\n\n```swift\nButton(\"Save\") {\n    save()\n}\n\nButton(\"Delete\", role: .destructive) { deleteItem() }   // red, and treated specially in menus\n\nButton {\n    addToCart()\n} label: {\n    Label(\"Add to cart\", systemImage: \"cart.badge.plus\")  // any view as a label\n}\n\n// Built-in styles\nButton(\"Primary\") { }.buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\nButton(\"Secondary\") { }.buttonStyle(.bordered)\nButton(\"Plain\") { }.buttonStyle(.plain)\n\n// Sizing and state\nButton(\"Continue\") { }\n    .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n    .controlSize(.large)\n    .disabled(!formIsValid)\n```\n\n## Text input\n\nText fields need a **binding**, `$name`: a two-way connection to some state. The field reads the current value and writes changes back. (Bindings are explained fully in the next lesson.)\n\n```swift title=\"SignInForm.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct SignInForm: View {\n    @State private var email = \"\"\n    @State private var password = \"\"\n    @State private var bio = \"\"\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 12) {\n            TextField(\"Email\", text: $email)\n                .keyboardType(.emailAddress)\n                .textContentType(.emailAddress)          // enables AutoFill\n                .textInputAutocapitalization(.never)\n                .autocorrectionDisabled()\n                .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)\n\n            SecureField(\"Password\", text: $password)     // hides the text\n                .textContentType(.password)\n                .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)\n\n            TextEditor(text: $bio)                        // multi-line\n                .frame(height: 100)\n                .border(.gray.opacity(0.3))\n\n            Text(\"\\(email.count) characters typed\")\n                .font(.caption)\n                .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { SignInForm() }\n```\n\nUseful keyboard types: `.emailAddress`, `.numberPad`, `.decimalPad`, `.phonePad`, `.URL`. Use `.submitLabel(.done)` and `.onSubmit { }` to handle the Return key.\n\n## Toggles, sliders, steppers and pickers\n\n```swift title=\"PreferencesView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nenum Spice: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {\n    case mild, medium, hot\n    var id: Self { self }\n}\n\nstruct PreferencesView: View {\n    @State private var notifications = true\n    @State private var servings = 2\n    @State private var volume = 0.5\n    @State private var spice = Spice.medium\n    @State private var date = Date.now\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 20) {\n            Toggle(\"Daily notifications\", isOn: $notifications)\n\n            Stepper(\"Servings: \\(servings)\", value: $servings, in: 1...12)\n\n            Slider(value: $volume, in: 0...1) {\n                Text(\"Volume\")\n            }\n\n            Picker(\"Spice level\", selection: $spice) {\n                ForEach(Spice.allCases) { level in\n                    Text(level.rawValue.capitalized).tag(level)\n                }\n            }\n            .pickerStyle(.segmented)                // also .menu, .wheel, .inline\n\n            DatePicker(\"Cook on\", selection: $date, displayedComponents: .date)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { PreferencesView() }\n```\n\n## Progress\n\n```swift\nProgressView()                                   // spinner\nProgressView(\"Loading recipes…\")                 // spinner with a label\nProgressView(value: 0.7)                         // 70% bar\nGauge(value: 0.4) { Text(\"Battery\") }            // gauge\n```\n\n## Alerts and confirmation dialogs\n\nAlerts are **declarative** too: you describe *when* they're shown with a Boolean binding.\n\n```swift title=\"DeleteButton.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct DeleteButton: View {\n    @State private var showConfirm = false\n    @State private var showDeleted = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Button(\"Delete recipe\", role: .destructive) {\n            showConfirm = true\n        }\n        .confirmationDialog(\"Delete this recipe?\", isPresented: $showConfirm, titleVisibility: .visible) {\n            Button(\"Delete\", role: .destructive) { showDeleted = true }\n            Button(\"Cancel\", role: .cancel) { }\n        } message: {\n            Text(\"This can't be undone.\")\n        }\n        .alert(\"Deleted\", isPresented: $showDeleted) {\n            Button(\"OK\") { }\n        } message: {\n            Text(\"The recipe was removed.\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { DeleteButton() }\n```\n\nSwiftUI sets `showConfirm` back to `false` automatically when the dialog is dismissed.\n\n## Links and menus\n\n```swift\nLink(\"Swift documentation\", destination: URL(string: \"https://www.swift.org/documentation/\")!)\n\nMenu {\n    Button(\"Sort by name\") { }\n    Button(\"Sort by rating\") { }\n} label: {\n    Label(\"Sort\", systemImage: \"arrow.up.arrow.down\")\n}\n```\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Use **text styles** so text respects Dynamic Type.\n- SF Symbols via `Image(systemName:)`; `Label` for icon + text; `AsyncImage` for URLs.\n- Buttons: title + action, `role`, and styles like `.borderedProminent`.\n- Inputs bind to state with `$`: `TextField`, `Toggle`, `Stepper`, `Slider`, `Picker`, `DatePicker`.\n- Alerts and dialogs are driven by Boolean state.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\nBuild an **Order Coffee** screen:\n\n1. A segmented `Picker` for size (Small, Medium, Large) using an enum.\n2. A `Stepper` for sugar (0–5) and a `Toggle` for oat milk.\n3. A `TextField` for the customer's name.\n4. A summary `Text`: e.g. *\"Large coffee for Ada, 2 sugars, oat milk\"*.\n5. An **Order** button (`.borderedProminent`, `.controlSize(.large)`) disabled while the name is empty, which shows an alert *\"Thanks, Ada! Your coffee is on its way.\"*\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\nenum CoffeeSize: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {\n    case small, medium, large\n    var id: Self { self }\n}\n\nstruct OrderCoffeeView: View {\n    @State private var size = CoffeeSize.medium\n    @State private var sugar = 0\n    @State private var oatMilk = false\n    @State private var name = \"\"\n    @State private var showThanks = false\n\n    private var summary: String {\n        let who = name.isEmpty ? \"you\" : name\n        return \"\\(size.rawValue.capitalized) coffee for \\(who), \\(sugar) sugar\\(sugar == 1 ? \"\" : \"s\")\"\n            + (oatMilk ? \", oat milk\" : \"\")\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 20) {\n            Picker(\"Size\", selection: $size) {\n                ForEach(CoffeeSize.allCases) { Text($0.rawValue.capitalized).tag($0) }\n            }\n            .pickerStyle(.segmented)\n\n            Stepper(\"Sugar: \\(sugar)\", value: $sugar, in: 0...5)\n            Toggle(\"Oat milk\", isOn: $oatMilk)\n            TextField(\"Your name\", text: $name)\n                .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)\n\n            Text(summary).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n\n            Button(\"Order\") { showThanks = true }\n                .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n                .controlSize(.large)\n                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n                .disabled(name.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespaces).isEmpty)\n        }\n        .padding()\n        .alert(\"Thanks, \\(name)!\", isPresented: $showThanks) {\n            Button(\"OK\") { }\n        } message: {\n            Text(\"Your coffee is on its way.\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { OrderCoffeeView() }\n```\nNotice `summary` is a **computed property**: derived from state, never stored separately.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: Why use `.font(.body)` instead of `.font(.system(size: 17))`?\n- [ ] It's shorter\n- [x] Text styles scale with the user's Dynamic Type setting\n- [ ] System sizes are deprecated\n- [ ] .body is bold\n> Fixed sizes ignore accessibility text sizes.\n\nQ: What does the `$` in `TextField(\"Name\", text: $name)` do?\n- [ ] Formats the text as currency\n- [x] Passes a binding, a two-way connection, so the field can read and write the state\n- [ ] Makes the field secure\n- [ ] Nothing\n> Bindings let controls modify state owned elsewhere.\n\nQ: How is an alert shown in SwiftUI?\n- [ ] By calling `alert.show()`\n- [x] By attaching `.alert(isPresented:)` bound to a Boolean state and setting that state to true\n- [ ] By creating a UIAlertController\n- [ ] Alerts aren't supported\n> Presentation is declarative: state decides whether the alert is visible.\n\nQ: Which view loads and displays an image from a URL?\n- [ ] Image(url:)\n- [x] AsyncImage\n- [ ] URLImage\n- [ ] Image(systemName:)\n> AsyncImage downloads the image and lets you provide a placeholder.\n```\n"},{"id":"swiftui-state","title":"State, Bindings & Data Flow","part":3,"minutes":35,"summary":"The heart of SwiftUI. Learn who owns each piece of data, how @State and @Binding connect views, the single source of truth, and how to react to changes.","body":":::goals\n- `@State` for local, view-owned values\n- `@Binding` to let a child view read *and write* its parent's state\n- The **single source of truth** and data flowing down, events flowing up\n- Derived values instead of duplicated state\n- `onChange`, `@FocusState` and keeping state in the right place\n:::\n\n## Every piece of data has one owner\n\nSwiftUI's golden rule: **each piece of state has exactly one source of truth**. Other views either receive a read-only copy (a plain property) or a **binding** to it. Never keep two copies of the same value in sync by hand, because that's where bugs come from.\n\n| You need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| A value this view owns and changes (toggle, text, selection) | `@State private var` |\n| To read a value from the parent | a plain `let` property |\n| To read **and write** a value owned by the parent | `@Binding var` |\n| A shared model object (view model, store) | `@Observable` class (Lesson 21) |\n| App-wide values like colour scheme or locale | `@Environment` |\n\n## `@State`\n\n`@State` gives a view its own storage that survives view updates and triggers a refresh when it changes. Mark it `private`, because it belongs to this view alone.\n\n```swift title=\"StateView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct StateView: View {\n    @State private var name = \"\"\n    @State private var isExpanded = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 12) {\n            TextField(\"Your name\", text: $name)          // $name = a binding to the state\n                .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)\n\n            Text(name.isEmpty ? \"Hello, stranger!\" : \"Hello, \\(name)!\")\n\n            Button(isExpanded ? \"Show less\" : \"Show more\") {\n                isExpanded.toggle()\n            }\n            if isExpanded {\n                Text(\"Here are some extra details that only appear when expanded.\")\n            }\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { StateView() }\n```\n\nWriting `$name` gives a **binding**: a reference that lets another view (here, the `TextField`) read and write the state.\n\n## `@Binding`: sharing state with a child\n\nWhen a child view needs to change state owned by its parent, the parent passes a binding, and the child declares it with `@Binding`:\n\n```swift title=\"WaterTracker.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct CounterRow: View {\n    let title: String\n    @Binding var count: Int                 // not owned here: read/write access to the parent's value\n\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack {\n            Text(\"\\(title): \\(count)\")\n            Spacer()\n            Button { count -= 1 } label: { Image(systemName: \"minus.circle\") }\n                .disabled(count == 0)\n            Button { count += 1 } label: { Image(systemName: \"plus.circle\") }\n        }\n        .font(.title3)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct WaterTracker: View {\n    @State private var glasses = 0          // ← the single source of truth\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 20) {\n            CounterRow(title: \"Glasses of water\", count: $glasses)   // pass a binding down\n            ProgressView(value: Double(glasses), total: 8)\n            if glasses >= 8 {\n                Text(\"🎉 Daily goal reached!\")\n            }\n            Button(\"Reset\") { glasses = 0 }\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { WaterTracker() }\n```\n\n`CounterRow` doesn't own the count. It only has access to it. When it changes `count`, it's really changing `WaterTracker.glasses`, and **both** views update.\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div class=\"hl\">WaterTracker<small>@State glasses (the owner)</small></div>\n<div class=\"arrow\">⇄</div>\n<div>CounterRow<small>@Binding count</small></div>\n<div>ProgressView<small>reads glasses</small></div>\n</div><div class=\"diagram-caption\">Data flows down; changes flow back up through the binding to the single owner.</div></div>\n\n### Previewing views with bindings\n\nUse `@Previewable` to create state right inside a preview:\n\n```swift\n#Preview {\n    @Previewable @State var count = 3\n    CounterRow(title: \"Coffees\", count: $count)\n        .padding()\n}\n```\n\nFor a fixed value you can also pass `.constant(3)`, a binding that never changes.\n\n## Closures: the other way to send events up\n\nBindings are great for simple values. For actions, pass a **closure**:\n\n```swift\nstruct DeleteRow: View {\n    let title: String\n    let onDelete: () -> Void               // the parent decides what \"delete\" means\n\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack {\n            Text(title)\n            Spacer()\n            Button(\"Delete\", role: .destructive, action: onDelete)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Derive, don't duplicate\n\nIf a value can be computed from other state, **compute it**, and don't store it:\n\n```swift title=\"PasswordField.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct PasswordField: View {\n    @State private var password = \"\"\n\n    // Derived values: always correct, never out of sync\n    private var hasDigit: Bool { password.contains(where: \\.isNumber) }\n    private var isLongEnough: Bool { password.count >= 8 }\n    private var isStrong: Bool { hasDigit && isLongEnough }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 8) {\n            SecureField(\"Password\", text: $password)\n                .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)\n            Label(\"At least 8 characters\", systemImage: isLongEnough ? \"checkmark.circle.fill\" : \"circle\")\n            Label(\"Contains a number\", systemImage: hasDigit ? \"checkmark.circle.fill\" : \"circle\")\n            Text(isStrong ? \"Strong password 💪\" : \"Keep going…\")\n                .foregroundStyle(isStrong ? .green : .secondary)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { PasswordField() }\n```\n\n## Reacting to changes: `onChange`\n\nRun code when a value changes (for side effects, not for deriving values):\n\n```swift\n.onChange(of: searchText) { oldValue, newValue in\n    print(\"Search changed from \\(oldValue) to \\(newValue)\")\n}\n```\n\n## Focus: `@FocusState`\n\nControl which text field has the keyboard:\n\n```swift title=\"LoginFields.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct LoginFields: View {\n    enum Field { case email, password }\n    @State private var email = \"\"\n    @State private var password = \"\"\n    @FocusState private var focused: Field?\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack {\n            TextField(\"Email\", text: $email)\n                .focused($focused, equals: .email)\n                .submitLabel(.next)\n                .onSubmit { focused = .password }     // Return moves to the next field\n            SecureField(\"Password\", text: $password)\n                .focused($focused, equals: .password)\n                .submitLabel(.go)\n                .onSubmit { focused = nil }            // dismiss the keyboard\n        }\n        .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)\n        .padding()\n        .onAppear { focused = .email }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { LoginFields() }\n```\n\n## State and identity\n\nSwiftUI keeps `@State` alive as long as the view keeps its **identity**, meaning it stays in the same position in the view tree. If an `if/else` switches between two different views, each branch has its own identity and its own state. This is also why lists need stable IDs (next lesson).\n\n:::warning Don't initialise @State from a parameter and expect updates\n`@State private var count: Int` initialised from a parent's value only uses that value **once**, when the view first appears. If the parent's value changes later, the state won't follow. If the parent owns the value, use a plain `let` (read only) or `@Binding` (read/write) instead.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- One **source of truth** per value.\n- `@State private var` = owned by this view. `$state` = a binding to it.\n- `@Binding var` = read/write access to a parent's state. Closures send actions up.\n- Compute derived values; don't store them.\n- `onChange` for side effects, `@FocusState` for the keyboard.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\nBuild a **tip calculator**:\n\n1. A `TextField` for the bill amount (use `TextField(\"Amount\", value: $amount, format: .number)` with a `Double` state), a segmented `Picker` for 10/15/20%, and a `Toggle` \"Round up\".\n2. Show the tip and total using **derived** computed properties, formatted as currency with `.formatted(.currency(code: \"USD\"))`.\n3. Extract the percentage picker into its own `TipPicker` view that takes `@Binding var percent: Int`.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct TipPicker: View {\n    @Binding var percent: Int\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Picker(\"Tip\", selection: $percent) {\n            ForEach([10, 15, 20], id: \\.self) { Text(\"\\($0)%\").tag($0) }\n        }\n        .pickerStyle(.segmented)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct TipCalculator: View {\n    @State private var amount = 0.0\n    @State private var percent = 15\n    @State private var roundUp = false\n\n    private var tip: Double {\n        let raw = amount * Double(percent) / 100\n        return roundUp ? raw.rounded(.up) : raw\n    }\n    private var total: Double { amount + tip }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 16) {\n            TextField(\"Amount\", value: $amount, format: .number)\n                .keyboardType(.decimalPad)\n                .textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)\n            TipPicker(percent: $percent)\n            Toggle(\"Round up tip\", isOn: $roundUp)\n            Text(\"Tip: \\(tip.formatted(.currency(code: \"USD\")))\").font(.title2)\n            Text(\"Total: \\(total.formatted(.currency(code: \"USD\")))\").font(.headline)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { TipCalculator() }\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: A child view needs to change a Boolean owned by its parent. What should the child declare?\n- [ ] `@State var isOn: Bool`\n- [x] `@Binding var isOn: Bool`\n- [ ] `let isOn: Bool`\n- [ ] `var isOn: Bool`\n> A binding gives read/write access to state owned elsewhere.\n\nQ: What does `$name` produce when `name` is `@State`?\n- [ ] A copy of the string\n- [x] A Binding<String> connected to the state\n- [ ] The string formatted as currency\n- [ ] An optional\n> The $ prefix accesses the property wrapper's projected value, a binding.\n\nQ: You have `@State var items` and want to show \"3 items left\". What's best?\n- [ ] Another @State var remainingCount that you update everywhere\n- [x] A computed property derived from items\n- [ ] A global variable\n- [ ] onChange to copy the count\n> Derived values can never get out of sync with their source.\n\nQ: Why should `@State` properties be `private`?\n- [ ] For performance\n- [x] Because they're owned by that view alone; outside views should receive data or bindings instead\n- [ ] Swift requires it\n- [ ] So they're saved to disk\n> Keeping state private makes ownership clear.\n```\n"},{"id":"swiftui-lists","title":"Lists, Grids & Forms","part":3,"minutes":35,"summary":"Almost every app shows lists of data. Learn List and ForEach, identity, sections, swipe actions, editing, lazy stacks and grids, and building settings screens with Form.","body":":::goals\n- `List` and `ForEach` with `Identifiable` data\n- Why stable **identity** matters\n- Sections, swipe actions, delete and move\n- `LazyVStack` and `LazyVGrid` for custom scrolling layouts\n- `Form` for settings and input screens\n:::\n\n## `List`\n\n`List` shows scrollable rows in the standard iOS style, and only creates the rows that are on screen, so it handles thousands of items.\n\n```swift title=\"FruitList.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct FruitList: View {\n    let fruits = [\"Apple\", \"Banana\", \"Cherry\", \"Mango\"]\n\n    var body: some View {\n        List(fruits, id: \\.self) { fruit in      // id: tells SwiftUI how to identify each row\n            Text(fruit)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { FruitList() }\n```\n\n## Identity and `Identifiable`\n\nSwiftUI must know which row is which, so it can animate insertions, keep row state attached to the right item, and update efficiently. The best way is to make your model **`Identifiable`**, with a stable, unique `id`:\n\n```swift\nstruct Task: Identifiable {\n    let id = UUID()          // unique and stable for the lifetime of the item\n    var title: String\n    var isDone = false\n}\n\nList(tasks) { task in        // no id: needed, since Identifiable provides it\n    Text(task.title)\n}\n```\n\n:::danger Don't use array indices as IDs\n`ForEach(items.indices, id: \\.self)` breaks when items are inserted or removed: rows get mixed up and animations glitch. Use a real `id`, like a database or server ID, or a `UUID` created once with the item.\n:::\n\n## A complete task list\n\nThis example has sections, a static row, swipe actions, delete and reordering. Type it in and run it:\n\n```swift title=\"TaskListView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct TodoItem: Identifiable {\n    let id = UUID()\n    var title: String\n    var isDone = false\n}\n\nstruct TaskListView: View {\n    @State private var items = [\n        TodoItem(title: \"Buy flour\"),\n        TodoItem(title: \"Preheat oven\"),\n        TodoItem(title: \"Invite friends\", isDone: true)\n    ]\n    @State private var newTitle = \"\"\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List {\n                Section {\n                    HStack {\n                        TextField(\"New task\", text: $newTitle)\n                            .onSubmit(add)\n                        Button(\"Add\", action: add)\n                            .disabled(newTitle.isEmpty)\n                    }\n                }\n\n                Section(\"To do (\\(items.filter { !$0.isDone }.count))\") {\n                    ForEach($items) { $item in                // bindings to each element!\n                        Toggle(isOn: $item.isDone) {\n                            Text(item.title)\n                                .strikethrough(item.isDone)\n                                .foregroundStyle(item.isDone ? .secondary : .primary)\n                        }\n                        .swipeActions(edge: .leading) {\n                            Button(\"Duplicate\") {\n                                items.append(TodoItem(title: item.title))\n                            }\n                            .tint(.blue)\n                        }\n                    }\n                    .onDelete { offsets in items.remove(atOffsets: offsets) }   // swipe to delete\n                    .onMove { from, to in items.move(fromOffsets: from, toOffset: to) }\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Tasks\")\n            .toolbar { EditButton() }                 // enables reordering\n            .animation(.default, value: items.count)\n        }\n    }\n\n    private func add() {\n        let title = newTitle.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespaces)\n        guard !title.isEmpty else { return }\n        items.append(TodoItem(title: title))\n        newTitle = \"\"\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { TaskListView() }\n```\n\nThings to notice:\n\n- `List { … }` can mix static rows and `ForEach` for dynamic ones.\n- **`ForEach($items) { $item in … }`** gives you a *binding* to each element, so the `Toggle` can change `item.isDone` directly in the array.\n- `.onDelete` adds swipe-to-delete; `.onMove` + `EditButton` enable drag-to-reorder.\n- `.swipeActions` adds custom swipe buttons.\n\n## List styles and extras\n\n```swift\nList { … }\n    .listStyle(.insetGrouped)       // .plain, .grouped, .insetGrouped, .sidebar\n    .refreshable {                  // pull to refresh (async)\n        await reload()\n    }\n    .overlay {\n        if items.isEmpty {\n            ContentUnavailableView(\"No tasks\", systemImage: \"checklist\",\n                                   description: Text(\"Add your first task above.\"))\n        }\n    }\n```\n\n`ContentUnavailableView` is Apple's standard **empty state**. There's even a built-in search version: `ContentUnavailableView.search(text: query)`.\n\n## Lazy stacks and grids\n\nWhen you want a custom look instead of standard list rows, use a `ScrollView` with **lazy** stacks or grids. They also create only the views that are visible:\n\n```swift title=\"PhotoGrid.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct PhotoGrid: View {\n    let columns = [GridItem(.adaptive(minimum: 110), spacing: 12)]   // as many columns as fit\n\n    var body: some View {\n        ScrollView {\n            LazyVGrid(columns: columns, spacing: 12) {\n                ForEach(1...30, id: \\.self) { i in\n                    RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12)\n                        .fill(Color(hue: Double(i) / 30, saturation: 0.6, brightness: 0.9))\n                        .aspectRatio(1, contentMode: .fit)\n                        .overlay(Text(\"\\(i)\").font(.title2.bold()).foregroundStyle(.white))\n                }\n            }\n            .padding()\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { PhotoGrid() }\n```\n\n| Column type | Behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| `GridItem(.flexible())` × N | N equal columns |\n| `GridItem(.fixed(100))` | Exactly 100 points wide |\n| `GridItem(.adaptive(minimum: 110))` | As many columns as fit, which suits phones and iPads alike |\n\nFor horizontal carousels use `ScrollView(.horizontal) { LazyHStack { … } }`, optionally with `.scrollTargetBehavior(.viewAligned)` for snapping.\n\n## Forms\n\n`Form` is a `List` styled for settings and data entry. Controls inside it automatically get the right iOS appearance:\n\n```swift title=\"ProfileSettings.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct ProfileSettings: View {\n    @State private var name = \"Ada\"\n    @State private var notifications = true\n    @State private var theme = \"System\"\n    @State private var birthday = Date.now\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            Form {\n                Section(\"Profile\") {\n                    TextField(\"Name\", text: $name)\n                    DatePicker(\"Birthday\", selection: $birthday, displayedComponents: .date)\n                }\n                Section {\n                    Toggle(\"Notifications\", isOn: $notifications)\n                    Picker(\"Theme\", selection: $theme) {\n                        ForEach([\"System\", \"Light\", \"Dark\"], id: \\.self) { Text($0) }\n                    }\n                } header: {\n                    Text(\"Preferences\")\n                } footer: {\n                    Text(\"Notifications suggest a recipe every morning.\")\n                }\n                Section {\n                    Button(\"Sign out\", role: .destructive) { }\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Settings\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { ProfileSettings() }\n```\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `List` and `ForEach` need stable identity: make models `Identifiable`.\n- `ForEach($items) { $item in }` gives bindings to elements.\n- `.onDelete`, `.onMove`, `.swipeActions`, `.refreshable`, `ContentUnavailableView`.\n- `LazyVGrid` with adaptive columns for custom grids; `Form` for settings.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Add a **filter** to `TaskListView`: a segmented picker (All / To do / Done) above the list, showing only matching tasks. (Hint: filter a *computed* array, and use `ForEach` over the filtered items with bindings looked up by id, or toggle via a function.)\n2. Show `ContentUnavailableView` when the filtered list is empty.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct TodoItem: Identifiable {\n    let id = UUID()\n    var title: String\n    var isDone = false\n}\n\nenum TaskFilter: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {\n    case all = \"All\", todo = \"To do\", done = \"Done\"\n    var id: Self { self }\n}\n\nstruct FilteredTasksView: View {\n    @State private var items = [TodoItem(title: \"Buy flour\"), TodoItem(title: \"Bake\", isDone: true)]\n    @State private var filter = TaskFilter.all\n\n    private var visible: [TodoItem] {\n        switch filter {\n        case .all: items\n        case .todo: items.filter { !$0.isDone }\n        case .done: items.filter(\\.isDone)\n        }\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List(visible) { item in\n                Button {\n                    toggle(item)\n                } label: {\n                    Label(item.title, systemImage: item.isDone ? \"checkmark.circle.fill\" : \"circle\")\n                }\n            }\n            .overlay {\n                if visible.isEmpty {\n                    ContentUnavailableView(\"Nothing here\", systemImage: \"tray\")\n                }\n            }\n            .safeAreaInset(edge: .top) {\n                Picker(\"Filter\", selection: $filter) {\n                    ForEach(TaskFilter.allCases) { Text($0.rawValue).tag($0) }\n                }\n                .pickerStyle(.segmented)\n                .padding(.horizontal)\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Tasks\")\n        }\n    }\n\n    private func toggle(_ item: TodoItem) {\n        guard let index = items.firstIndex(where: { $0.id == item.id }) else { return }\n        items[index].isDone.toggle()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { FilteredTasksView() }\n```\nNote the pattern: the **source of truth** is `items`; `visible` is derived; changes go through a function that updates the source by `id`.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: Why should list data be Identifiable with stable IDs?\n- [ ] It makes lists sort automatically\n- [x] So SwiftUI can track which row is which across updates, keeping state and animations correct\n- [ ] It's required for text to display\n- [ ] It saves memory\n> Stable identity is how SwiftUI matches old rows to new rows.\n\nQ: What does `ForEach($items) { $item in ... }` give you?\n- [ ] A copy of each item\n- [x] A binding to each element, so controls can edit items in place\n- [ ] The index of each item\n- [ ] A sorted array\n> Binding-based ForEach lets a Toggle or TextField modify the array directly.\n\nQ: Which grid column type fits as many columns as the width allows?\n- [ ] .fixed\n- [ ] .flexible\n- [x] .adaptive(minimum:)\n- [ ] .automatic\n> Adaptive columns work well across iPhone and iPad sizes.\n\nQ: What's the standard way to show an empty state?\n- [ ] An alert\n- [x] ContentUnavailableView\n- [ ] An empty List\n- [ ] ProgressView\n> ContentUnavailableView gives the familiar iOS empty-state look.\n```\n"},{"id":"swiftui-styling","title":"Styling, Colours, Dark Mode & Liquid Glass","part":3,"minutes":30,"summary":"Make your app look polished and at home on iOS. Semantic colours and dark mode, asset catalog colours, fonts, SF Symbols rendering, reusable styles, materials and Apple's Liquid Glass design.","body":":::goals\n- Semantic colours, and why they make dark mode free\n- Custom colours in the asset catalog; the accent colour\n- Font design, weights and custom fonts\n- SF Symbols variants and rendering modes\n- Reusable styling with `ViewModifier` and `ButtonStyle`\n- Materials, gradients and Liquid Glass\n- App icon\n:::\n\n## Semantic colours\n\niOS provides **semantic** colours that adapt automatically to light mode, dark mode, and high-contrast settings:\n\n```swift\nText(\"Title\").foregroundStyle(.primary)          // black in light, white in dark\nText(\"Subtitle\").foregroundStyle(.secondary)     // softer grey that adapts\nText(\"Hint\").foregroundStyle(.tertiary)\nRectangle().fill(.background)                    // the screen background\nRectangle().fill(.background.secondary)          // a grouped / card background\nButton(\"Go\") { }.tint(.orange)                   // tint for controls\n```\n\n:::danger Hard-coded black and white break dark mode\n`Color.black` text on a dark background is invisible. Use `.primary`, `.secondary` and `.background` styles, and your app supports dark mode with almost no extra work. Test with the dark-mode control in the canvas or [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[A]] in the Simulator.\n:::\n\n## Custom colours\n\nDefine brand colours in **Assets.xcassets**: click **+ → Color Set**, name it (e.g. `Brand`), and set separate **Any** (light) and **Dark** appearances. Use it with the generated symbol:\n\n```swift\nText(\"RecipeBox\").foregroundStyle(Color(.brand))     // generated from the asset name\n// or: Color(\"Brand\")\n```\n\nThe **AccentColor** asset sets your app's tint for buttons, links, toggles and selections everywhere. It's one change for a consistent brand.\n\nIn code you can also use the built-in palette (`.orange`, `.mint`, `.indigo`…), which is carefully tuned for both modes, and `.gradient` for a subtle gradient of any colour: `.fill(.orange.gradient)`.\n\n## Fonts\n\n```swift\nText(\"Hello\").font(.title.weight(.semibold))\nText(\"Hello\").font(.system(.largeTitle, design: .rounded, weight: .bold))   // rounded design\nText(\"12:45\").font(.body.monospacedDigit())                                  // digits that don't jiggle\nText(\"Code\").font(.system(.body, design: .monospaced))\nText(\"Elegant\").font(.system(.title, design: .serif))\n```\n\nTo add a custom font: drag the `.ttf`/`.otf` into your project (tick your target), add it to the **Info** tab under *Fonts provided by application*, then use `.font(.custom(\"Nunito-Bold\", size: 20, relativeTo: .title))`. The `relativeTo:` parameter keeps it scaling with Dynamic Type.\n\n## SF Symbols\n\n```swift\nImage(systemName: \"heart.fill\").foregroundStyle(.red)\n\nImage(systemName: \"cloud.sun.rain.fill\")\n    .symbolRenderingMode(.multicolor)              // Apple's built-in colours\n\nImage(systemName: \"person.3.sequence.fill\")\n    .symbolRenderingMode(.hierarchical)            // one colour at different opacities\n    .foregroundStyle(.orange)\n\nImage(systemName: \"wifi\", variableValue: 0.5)      // variable symbols show levels\n\nImage(systemName: \"star\")\n    .symbolVariant(.fill)                          // same as \"star.fill\"\n    .imageScale(.large)\n```\n\nBrowse and search symbols in the free **SF Symbols** app. Check each symbol's availability, since newer symbols need newer iOS versions.\n\n## Reusable styles\n\nWhen the same combination of modifiers appears in many places, wrap it in a **`ViewModifier`**:\n\n```swift title=\"CardStyle.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct CardStyle: ViewModifier {\n    func body(content: Content) -> some View {\n        content\n            .padding()\n            .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)\n            .background(.background.secondary, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 16))\n    }\n}\n\nextension View {\n    func cardStyle() -> some View { modifier(CardStyle()) }   // nicer call site\n}\n\nstruct CardDemo: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 12) {\n            Text(\"Ingredients\").font(.headline).cardStyle()\n            Text(\"Steps\").font(.headline).cardStyle()\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { CardDemo() }\n```\n\nCustom **button styles** let you change how every button looks and reacts to presses:\n\n```swift title=\"PillButtonStyle.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct PillButtonStyle: ButtonStyle {\n    func makeBody(configuration: Configuration) -> some View {\n        configuration.label\n            .font(.headline)\n            .padding(.horizontal, 24)\n            .padding(.vertical, 12)\n            .background(.orange.gradient, in: .capsule)\n            .foregroundStyle(.white)\n            .scaleEffect(configuration.isPressed ? 0.95 : 1)       // press feedback\n            .animation(.snappy, value: configuration.isPressed)\n    }\n}\n\nextension ButtonStyle where Self == PillButtonStyle {\n    static var pill: PillButtonStyle { PillButtonStyle() }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    Button(\"Start cooking\") { }.buttonStyle(.pill)\n}\n```\n\n## Materials\n\n**Materials** are translucent, blurred backgrounds that let content behind them show through subtly. They look great over images and adapt to light and dark:\n\n```swift\nText(\"Over a photo\")\n    .padding()\n    .background(.ultraThinMaterial, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 12))\n// .ultraThinMaterial, .thinMaterial, .regularMaterial, .thickMaterial\n```\n\n## Liquid Glass\n\nSince iOS 26, Apple's design language is **Liquid Glass**: a translucent, light-refracting material used for navigation bars, tab bars, toolbars, sheets and controls. **Standard SwiftUI components adopt it automatically** when you build with the current SDK: `NavigationStack`, `TabView`, toolbars and system buttons all get the new look for free.\n\nFor your own floating controls, apply it with `glassEffect`:\n\n```swift title=\"GlassBadge.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct GlassBadge: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        ZStack {\n            LinearGradient(colors: [.orange, .pink], startPoint: .topLeading, endPoint: .bottomTrailing)\n                .ignoresSafeArea()\n            Label(\"4.8 · 1.2k reviews\", systemImage: \"star.fill\")\n                .font(.headline)\n                .padding()\n                .glassEffect()                          // the Liquid Glass material\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { GlassBadge() }\n```\n\nThere are also glass button styles, `.buttonStyle(.glass)` and `.buttonStyle(.glassProminent)`, for buttons that float over content.\n\n:::tip Use glass sparingly\nLiquid Glass is meant for the **navigation layer** that floats above your content (bars, controls, floating buttons), not for every card and row. Let the system components handle most of it, and keep your content itself clear and readable.\n:::\n\nIf you need to support iOS versions before 26, wrap it: `if #available(iOS 26, *) { … } else { … }`.\n\n## Dark mode and previews\n\n```swift\n#Preview(\"Light\") { CardDemo() }\n#Preview(\"Dark\") { CardDemo().preferredColorScheme(.dark) }\n```\n\nRead the current scheme in a view with `@Environment(\\.colorScheme) private var colorScheme`, but prefer adaptive colours so you rarely need it.\n\n## App icon\n\nIn `Assets.xcassets`, select **AppIcon** and drop in a **1024×1024** image. You can supply separate **Dark** and **Tinted** variants for iOS's icon appearance options. For the full layered Liquid Glass look, Apple provides the **Icon Composer** app to build icons from layers.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Use semantic styles (`.primary`, `.secondary`, `.background`) and asset colours with light/dark variants.\n- AccentColor sets your brand tint app-wide.\n- Font design and weight via `.font(.system(_, design:, weight:))`; custom fonts with `relativeTo:`.\n- Reuse styling with `ViewModifier` and `ButtonStyle`.\n- Materials and Liquid Glass (`.glassEffect()`) for the floating navigation layer.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Create a `Brand` colour set with light and dark values, and set **AccentColor** to match.\n2. Write a `ViewModifier` called `TagStyle` for small capsule-shaped tags (caption font, horizontal padding, tinted background at 15% opacity) and use it on three cuisine tags in an `HStack`.\n3. Preview your screen in light and dark mode and at the largest Dynamic Type size, and fix anything that breaks.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What's the easiest way to make text readable in both light and dark mode?\n- [ ] Always use Color.black\n- [x] Use semantic styles like .primary and .secondary\n- [ ] Check the colour scheme in every view\n- [ ] Disable dark mode\n> Semantic colours adapt automatically.\n\nQ: What does the AccentColor asset control?\n- [ ] The app icon\n- [x] The default tint for buttons, links, toggles and selection across the app\n- [ ] The launch screen\n- [ ] Only navigation titles\n> Set it once for a consistent brand colour.\n\nQ: When should you create a custom ViewModifier?\n- [ ] For every view\n- [x] When the same combination of modifiers is repeated in many places\n- [ ] Only for text\n- [ ] Never. Use copy and paste\n> ViewModifiers make styling reusable and consistent.\n\nQ: How do standard components like NavigationStack and TabView get the Liquid Glass look?\n- [ ] You must apply .glassEffect() to each one\n- [x] Automatically, when built with the current SDK\n- [ ] Only with a special library\n- [ ] Only on iPad\n> System components adopt Liquid Glass for free; use glassEffect for your own floating controls.\n```\n"},{"id":"swiftui-animation","title":"Animation & Transitions","part":3,"minutes":25,"summary":"SwiftUI makes animation almost effortless. Learn implicit and explicit animations, springs, transitions, symbol effects, matched geometry and phase animations.","body":":::goals\n- Explicit animations with `withAnimation`\n- Implicit animations with `.animation(_:value:)`\n- Springs, timing curves and durations\n- Transitions for views appearing and disappearing\n- `symbolEffect`, `contentTransition`, `matchedGeometryEffect` and `phaseAnimator`\n:::\n\n## Animation is a state change\n\nIn SwiftUI you don't animate views directly. You **change state** and tell SwiftUI to animate the difference between the old and new UI.\n\n### Explicit: `withAnimation`\n\nWrap the state change:\n\n```swift title=\"ExpandingCard.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct ExpandingCard: View {\n    @State private var expanded = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 12) {\n            HStack {\n                Text(\"Tips\").font(.headline)\n                Spacer()\n                Image(systemName: \"chevron.down\")\n                    .rotationEffect(.degrees(expanded ? 180 : 0))\n            }\n            if expanded {\n                Text(\"Salt your pasta water generously, and save a cup before draining.\")\n                    .transition(.opacity.combined(with: .move(edge: .top)))\n            }\n        }\n        .padding()\n        .background(.background.secondary, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 16))\n        .onTapGesture {\n            withAnimation(.spring(duration: 0.4, bounce: 0.3)) {\n                expanded.toggle()\n            }\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { ExpandingCard() }\n```\n\nEverything that changes because of `expanded` animates: the rotation, the text appearing, and the card growing.\n\n### Implicit: `.animation(_:value:)`\n\nAttach an animation to a view; it animates whenever `value` changes:\n\n```swift title=\"FavoriteButton.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct FavoriteButton: View {\n    @State private var isFavorite = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Button {\n            isFavorite.toggle()\n        } label: {\n            Image(systemName: isFavorite ? \"heart.fill\" : \"heart\")\n                .font(.largeTitle)\n                .foregroundStyle(isFavorite ? .red : .secondary)\n                .scaleEffect(isFavorite ? 1.2 : 1)\n                .animation(.spring(bounce: 0.5), value: isFavorite)\n        }\n        .accessibilityLabel(isFavorite ? \"Remove from favourites\" : \"Add to favourites\")\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { FavoriteButton() }\n```\n\n:::tip Always pass a value\n`.animation(.default, value: x)` only animates changes caused by `x`. The old form without `value:` was deprecated because it animated *everything*, often by surprise.\n:::\n\n## Kinds of animation\n\n| Animation | Feel |\n|---|---|\n| `.default` | A sensible spring |\n| `.spring(duration:bounce:)` | Physics-based and natural, the Apple default |\n| `.snappy`, `.smooth`, `.bouncy` | Preset springs |\n| `.easeInOut(duration: 0.3)` | Classic curve with a fixed duration |\n| `.linear(duration: 1)` | Constant speed (progress, rotation) |\n| `.default.repeatForever(autoreverses: true)` | Loops, e.g. for a pulsing effect |\n\nKeep UI animations short, around 0.2 to 0.5 s. They should explain what changed, not slow people down.\n\n## Transitions\n\nA **transition** controls how a view is **inserted or removed** (inside an `if` or a `ForEach`):\n\n```swift\nif showBanner {\n    BannerView()\n        .transition(.move(edge: .top).combined(with: .opacity))\n}\n// Others: .opacity, .scale, .slide, .push(from: .trailing), .asymmetric(insertion:removal:)\n```\n\nTransitions only run if the change is animated (`withAnimation` or `.animation(_:value:)` on a container).\n\n## Content transitions\n\nAnimate changes to text and symbols themselves:\n\n```swift title=\"LiveCounter.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct LiveCounter: View {\n    @State private var count = 0\n    @State private var isMuted = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 24) {\n            Text(\"\\(count)\")\n                .font(.system(size: 64, weight: .bold, design: .rounded))\n                .contentTransition(.numericText(value: Double(count)))   // digits roll\n\n            Image(systemName: isMuted ? \"speaker.slash.fill\" : \"speaker.wave.3.fill\")\n                .font(.largeTitle)\n                .contentTransition(.symbolEffect(.replace))              // symbol morphs\n\n            HStack {\n                Button(\"Add\") { withAnimation { count += 1 } }\n                Button(\"Mute\") { withAnimation { isMuted.toggle() } }\n            }\n            .buttonStyle(.bordered)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { LiveCounter() }\n```\n\n## Symbol effects\n\nSF Symbols have built-in animations:\n\n```swift\nImage(systemName: \"bell.fill\")\n    .symbolEffect(.bounce, value: notificationCount)   // bounces when the value changes\n\nImage(systemName: \"wifi\")\n    .symbolEffect(.variableColor.iterative, isActive: isSearching)   // loops while active\n```\n\n## Matched geometry: smooth hero animations\n\n`matchedGeometryEffect` animates a view \"moving\" between two places in your layout:\n\n```swift title=\"TabHighlight.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct TabHighlight: View {\n    @Namespace private var namespace\n    @State private var selected = \"Breakfast\"\n    let tabs = [\"Breakfast\", \"Lunch\", \"Dinner\"]\n\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack {\n            ForEach(tabs, id: \\.self) { tab in\n                Text(tab)\n                    .padding(.vertical, 8)\n                    .padding(.horizontal, 14)\n                    .background {\n                        if selected == tab {\n                            Capsule()\n                                .fill(.orange.opacity(0.2))\n                                .matchedGeometryEffect(id: \"highlight\", in: namespace)\n                        }\n                    }\n                    .onTapGesture {\n                        withAnimation(.snappy) { selected = tab }\n                    }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { TabHighlight() }\n```\n\nFor zooming from a thumbnail into a detail screen, `NavigationLink` supports `.navigationTransition(.zoom(sourceID:in:))`.\n\n## Multi-step animations: `phaseAnimator`\n\n```swift\nImage(systemName: \"hand.wave.fill\")\n    .font(.largeTitle)\n    .phaseAnimator([0, -20, 20, 0]) { view, angle in\n        view.rotationEffect(.degrees(angle))\n    }\n```\n\nThe view cycles through the phases automatically, which is great for attention-grabbing effects used sparingly.\n\n## Respect Reduce Motion\n\nSome users turn on **Reduce Motion** because large animations can cause discomfort. Check it and tone things down:\n\n```swift\n@Environment(\\.accessibilityReduceMotion) private var reduceMotion\n\nwithAnimation(reduceMotion ? nil : .spring) { expanded.toggle() }\n```\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Animate by changing state inside `withAnimation`, or with `.animation(_:value:)`.\n- Springs are the natural default; keep animations short.\n- `.transition` for insertion and removal; `.contentTransition` for changing text and symbols.\n- `symbolEffect`, `matchedGeometryEffect` and `phaseAnimator` for extra polish.\n- Respect Reduce Motion.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Build a **like button** with a heart that pops (scale spring), turns red, and shows a count that rolls with `.numericText()`.\n2. Build a **toast**: a capsule message that slides down from the top when a button is tapped and disappears after 2 seconds. (Hint: use `.task(id:)` or `Task { try? await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(2)); withAnimation { show = false } }`.)\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution for task 2\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct ToastDemo: View {\n    @State private var showToast = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        ZStack(alignment: .top) {\n            Button(\"Save recipe\") {\n                withAnimation(.snappy) { showToast = true }\n            }\n            .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n            .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, maxHeight: .infinity)\n\n            if showToast {\n                Label(\"Saved to favourites\", systemImage: \"heart.fill\")\n                    .padding()\n                    .background(.regularMaterial, in: .capsule)\n                    .transition(.move(edge: .top).combined(with: .opacity))\n                    .task {\n                        try? await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(2))\n                        withAnimation { showToast = false }\n                    }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { ToastDemo() }\n```\n`.task` on the toast starts when it appears and is cancelled automatically if it disappears early.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: How do you animate a change in SwiftUI?\n- [ ] Call view.animate()\n- [x] Change state inside withAnimation, or attach .animation(_:value:) to the view\n- [ ] Use a timer to update the frame manually\n- [ ] Animations require UIKit\n> Animations describe how to move between two states.\n\nQ: What does a transition control?\n- [ ] The colour of a view\n- [x] How a view animates when it's inserted into or removed from the hierarchy\n- [ ] Navigation between screens only\n- [ ] The duration of all animations\n> Transitions apply to views appearing and disappearing.\n\nQ: Why pass `value:` to `.animation`?\n- [ ] It sets the duration\n- [x] So only changes to that value are animated, avoiding surprising animations\n- [ ] It's optional and has no effect\n- [ ] To repeat the animation\n> Scoping animations to a value keeps them predictable.\n```\n"},{"id":"app-lifecycle","title":"The App Lifecycle & Scenes","part":4,"minutes":25,"summary":"Understand how iOS launches, backgrounds and terminates your app, react to scene phases, run work when views appear, and restore the user's place.","body":":::goals\n- The `App` protocol, scenes and `WindowGroup`\n- App states: active, inactive, background, suspended, terminated\n- Reacting to `scenePhase`\n- `onAppear`, `onDisappear` and `.task`\n- Restoring state with `@SceneStorage`\n- When you still need an app delegate\n:::\n\n## The app's structure\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeBoxApp.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\n@main\nstruct RecipeBoxApp: App {\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup {\n            ContentView()\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct ContentView: View {\n    var body: some View { Text(\"Hello\") }\n}\n```\n\n- The **`App`** is created once when your app launches.\n- A **`Scene`** is a part of your app's UI that the system manages. `WindowGroup` creates windows showing your root view. On iPad and Mac the user can open several windows (several scenes) of the same app side by side.\n\n## App states\n\niOS, not your app, decides when your app runs. Your app moves through these states:\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div>Not running</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Active<small>on screen, receiving touches</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">⇄</div>\n<div>Inactive<small>visible but not interactive (e.g. Control Centre open)</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">⇄</div>\n<div>Background<small>not visible, briefly running</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>Suspended<small>in memory, not running</small></div>\n</div><div class=\"diagram-caption\">A suspended app can be terminated at any time to free memory, without warning.</div></div>\n\nWhen the user leaves your app, it gets a few seconds in the background, then it's **suspended**: frozen in memory. If the system needs memory, it **terminates** suspended apps silently. When the user comes back, they expect to be exactly where they left off, so save important state before you're suspended.\n\n## `scenePhase`\n\nRead the current phase from the environment and react to changes:\n\n```swift title=\"PhaseAwareView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct PhaseAwareView: View {\n    @Environment(\\.scenePhase) private var scenePhase\n    @State private var log: [String] = []\n\n    var body: some View {\n        List(log, id: \\.self) { Text($0) }\n            .onChange(of: scenePhase) { _, newPhase in\n                switch newPhase {\n                case .active: log.append(\"Active: refresh data\")\n                case .inactive: log.append(\"Inactive\")\n                case .background: log.append(\"Background: save drafts now\")\n                @unknown default: break\n                }\n            }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { PhaseAwareView() }\n```\n\nTypical uses:\n\n- **`.background`**: save unsaved work, pause timers, stop expensive work.\n- **`.active`**: refresh data that may be stale, resume timers.\n\n## View lifecycle: `onAppear`, `onDisappear` and `.task`\n\n```swift\nList(recipes) { … }\n    .onAppear { print(\"List appeared\") }        // synchronous\n    .onDisappear { print(\"List disappeared\") }\n    .task {                                     // async; cancelled when the view disappears\n        await viewModel.load()\n    }\n    .task(id: searchText) {                     // restarts whenever searchText changes\n        await viewModel.search(searchText)\n    }\n```\n\nPrefer **`.task`** for async loading. It's tied to the view's lifetime, so if the user leaves the screen before loading finishes, the work is cancelled automatically. `.task(id:)` is a neat way to re-run work when a value changes, like a search that cancels the previous one.\n\n:::warning onAppear can run more than once\nViews can appear several times, for example when navigating back to them or switching tabs. Make loading code idempotent: check whether data is already loaded, or make reloading harmless.\n:::\n\n## Restoring the user's place: `@SceneStorage`\n\n`@SceneStorage` saves small values per scene and restores them after the app is terminated and relaunched, which is perfect for the selected tab or a draft:\n\n```swift title=\"DraftView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct DraftView: View {\n    @SceneStorage(\"selectedTab\") private var selectedTab = 0\n    @SceneStorage(\"draftNote\") private var draft = \"\"\n\n    var body: some View {\n        TabView(selection: $selectedTab) {\n            TextEditor(text: $draft)\n                .padding()\n                .tabItem { Label(\"Draft\", systemImage: \"square.and.pencil\") }\n                .tag(0)\n            Text(\"Settings\")\n                .tabItem { Label(\"Settings\", systemImage: \"gear\") }\n                .tag(1)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { DraftView() }\n```\n\nUse it only for small UI state. Real data belongs in SwiftData or files (Part 5).\n\n:::tip Test termination\nRun the app from Xcode, type a draft, press [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[H]] to go home, then stop the app with Xcode's ■ button (this simulates the system terminating it). Run again: your draft and tab should be restored.\n:::\n\n## Environment values\n\n`scenePhase` is one of many **environment values** SwiftUI provides to every view:\n\n```swift\n@Environment(\\.colorScheme) private var colorScheme           // .light / .dark\n@Environment(\\.dynamicTypeSize) private var typeSize\n@Environment(\\.horizontalSizeClass) private var sizeClass     // .compact (iPhone portrait) / .regular (iPad)\n@Environment(\\.locale) private var locale\n@Environment(\\.dismiss) private var dismiss                    // close a sheet or pop a screen\n@Environment(\\.openURL) private var openURL\n```\n\n`horizontalSizeClass` is handy for adapting layouts to iPad: show a grid with more columns, or a side-by-side layout.\n\n## When you need an app delegate\n\nSome system events still arrive through the older `UIApplicationDelegate`, for example registering for **remote push notifications** or configuring certain SDKs at launch. Add one with an adaptor:\n\n```swift\nfinal class AppDelegate: NSObject, UIApplicationDelegate {\n    func application(_ application: UIApplication,\n                     didFinishLaunchingWithOptions launchOptions: [UIApplication.LaunchOptionsKey: Any]? = nil) -> Bool {\n        // one-time setup\n        return true\n    }\n}\n\n@main\nstruct RecipeBoxApp: App {\n    @UIApplicationDelegateAdaptor(AppDelegate.self) private var appDelegate\n\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup { ContentView() }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nMost apps never need one.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `App` → `Scene` (`WindowGroup`) → views.\n- iOS moves apps between active, inactive, background and suspended, and can terminate suspended apps without warning.\n- React to `scenePhase` to save work in the background and refresh when active.\n- `.task` for async work tied to a view; `.task(id:)` re-runs when a value changes.\n- `@SceneStorage` restores small UI state after termination.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Add the `PhaseAwareView` to a project, run it, and trigger each phase: pull down Control Centre (inactive), go Home (background), come back (active).\n2. Build a note-taking screen with `@SceneStorage` for the draft, and verify it survives termination using the tip above.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What can happen to a suspended app without any warning?\n- [ ] It becomes active\n- [x] The system can terminate it to free memory\n- [ ] It keeps running network requests forever\n- [ ] It's uninstalled\n> Save important state when entering the background.\n\nQ: Why is `.task` preferred over `onAppear` for loading data?\n- [ ] It runs faster\n- [x] It supports async work and is cancelled automatically when the view disappears\n- [ ] It runs only once per app launch\n- [ ] It runs on a background thread\n> .task ties async work to the view's lifetime.\n\nQ: What is `@SceneStorage` good for?\n- [ ] Storing a large database\n- [x] Restoring small UI state, like the selected tab or a draft, after the app is terminated and relaunched\n- [ ] Sharing data between apps\n- [ ] Storing passwords\n> It's lightweight state restoration per scene.\n\nQ: Which environment value tells you whether you have iPad-like horizontal space?\n- [ ] colorScheme\n- [ ] scenePhase\n- [x] horizontalSizeClass\n- [ ] locale\n> .regular width usually means iPad (or a large iPhone in landscape).\n```\n"},{"id":"navigation","title":"Navigation, Tabs & Sheets","part":4,"minutes":40,"summary":"Move between screens the iOS way. Covers NavigationStack with value-based links, programmatic navigation, toolbars, tab bars, sheets and dismissing.","body":":::goals\n- `NavigationStack`, `NavigationLink` and `navigationDestination`\n- Programmatic navigation with a path\n- Titles and toolbars\n- Tab bars with `TabView` and `Tab`\n- Sheets, full-screen covers and `dismiss`\n- `NavigationSplitView` for iPad (overview)\n:::\n\n## `NavigationStack`\n\nA `NavigationStack` manages a **stack** of screens: push a new screen on top, and the back button or swipe gesture pops it off.\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div>Recipes</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div>Recipe detail</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"hl\">Step-by-step<small>top of the stack</small></div>\n</div><div class=\"diagram-caption\">Swiping from the left edge (or tapping Back) pops the top screen.</div></div>\n\n### Value-based navigation\n\nThe modern pattern: links push a **value**, and `navigationDestination(for:)` decides which screen shows that value:\n\n```swift title=\"RecipesNavigation.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct Recipe: Identifiable, Hashable {\n    let id: Int\n    let name: String\n    let minutes: Int\n}\n\nstruct RecipeListView: View {\n    let recipes = [\n        Recipe(id: 1, name: \"Pancakes\", minutes: 20),\n        Recipe(id: 2, name: \"Ramen\", minutes: 45),\n        Recipe(id: 3, name: \"Tacos\", minutes: 30)\n    ]\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List(recipes) { recipe in\n                NavigationLink(value: recipe) {          // push this value\n                    Text(recipe.name)\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Recipes\")\n            .navigationDestination(for: Recipe.self) { recipe in   // how to show a Recipe\n                RecipeDetailView(recipe: recipe)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct RecipeDetailView: View {\n    let recipe: Recipe\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 12) {\n            Text(recipe.name).font(.largeTitle.bold())\n            Text(\"Ready in \\(recipe.minutes) minutes\").foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n        }\n        .navigationTitle(recipe.name)\n        .navigationBarTitleDisplayMode(.inline)\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { RecipeListView() }\n```\n\n- The value must be **`Hashable`**.\n- Put `navigationDestination` **inside** the stack (usually on the root view), not inside a lazy container like a `List` row.\n- There's only **one** `NavigationStack`, at the root. Detail screens don't need their own.\n\n## Programmatic navigation: the path\n\nBind the stack to a **path**, an array of values, and you can navigate from code: jump deep, go back several screens, or pop to the root.\n\n```swift title=\"PathNavigation.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct PathNavigation: View {\n    @State private var path: [Int] = []             // the stack's contents\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack(path: $path) {\n            VStack(spacing: 16) {\n                Button(\"Open step 1\") { path.append(1) }\n                Button(\"Jump straight to step 3\") { path = [1, 2, 3] }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Start\")\n            .navigationDestination(for: Int.self) { step in\n                VStack(spacing: 16) {\n                    Text(\"Step \\(step)\").font(.largeTitle)\n                    Button(\"Next step\") { path.append(step + 1) }\n                    Button(\"Back to start\") { path.removeAll() }   // pop to root\n                }\n                .navigationTitle(\"Step \\(step)\")\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { PathNavigation() }\n```\n\nFor stacks with *different* value types, use `NavigationPath`, a type-erased path: `@State private var path = NavigationPath()`.\n\n:::tip Navigation state belongs to you\nBecause the path is plain state, you can save it, restore it, or drive it from a view model, for example to navigate after a successful save or when a notification is tapped.\n:::\n\n## Titles and toolbars\n\n```swift\nList { … }\n    .navigationTitle(\"Recipes\")                   // large title by default\n    .toolbar {\n        ToolbarItem(placement: .topBarTrailing) {\n            Button(\"Add\", systemImage: \"plus\") { showNewRecipe = true }\n        }\n        ToolbarItem(placement: .topBarLeading) {\n            EditButton()\n        }\n    }\n    .searchable(text: $searchText, prompt: \"Search recipes\")   // search field in the nav bar\n```\n\n## Tab bars\n\nA `TabView` shows top-level sections. Each tab usually contains its own `NavigationStack`:\n\n```swift title=\"MainTabs.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct MainTabs: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        TabView {\n            Tab(\"Home\", systemImage: \"house\") {\n                NavigationStack {\n                    Text(\"Home\").navigationTitle(\"Home\")\n                }\n            }\n            Tab(\"Favourites\", systemImage: \"heart\") {\n                NavigationStack {\n                    Text(\"Favourites\").navigationTitle(\"Favourites\")\n                }\n            }\n            .badge(3)\n            Tab(\"Settings\", systemImage: \"gear\") {\n                NavigationStack {\n                    Text(\"Settings\").navigationTitle(\"Settings\")\n                }\n            }\n            Tab(role: .search) {                 // a dedicated search tab\n                NavigationStack {\n                    Text(\"Search\").navigationTitle(\"Search\")\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { MainTabs() }\n```\n\nEach tab keeps its own navigation state, so switching tabs and coming back returns you to where you were. To select tabs from code, pass `TabView(selection: $selectedTab)` and give each `Tab` a `value:`.\n\n## Sheets and full-screen covers\n\nA **sheet** slides up over the current screen for a focused task, like creating a new item or showing settings. Like alerts, it's driven by state:\n\n```swift title=\"SheetDemo.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct NewRecipeView: View {\n    @Environment(\\.dismiss) private var dismiss      // closes the sheet\n    @State private var name = \"\"\n    let onSave: (String) -> Void\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            Form {\n                TextField(\"Recipe name\", text: $name)\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"New Recipe\")\n            .toolbar {\n                ToolbarItem(placement: .cancellationAction) {\n                    Button(\"Cancel\") { dismiss() }\n                }\n                ToolbarItem(placement: .confirmationAction) {\n                    Button(\"Save\") {\n                        onSave(name)\n                        dismiss()\n                    }\n                    .disabled(name.isEmpty)\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct SheetDemo: View {\n    @State private var showNew = false\n    @State private var names: [String] = []\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List(names, id: \\.self) { Text($0) }\n                .navigationTitle(\"My Recipes\")\n                .toolbar {\n                    Button(\"Add\", systemImage: \"plus\") { showNew = true }\n                }\n                .sheet(isPresented: $showNew) {\n                    NewRecipeView { names.append($0) }\n                        .presentationDetents([.medium, .large])     // half-height sheet\n                }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { SheetDemo() }\n```\n\n- `.sheet(item: $selectedRecipe) { recipe in … }` shows a sheet for an optional item: it's presented when the item is set and dismissed when it's `nil`.\n- `.fullScreenCover(isPresented:)` covers the whole screen, which suits onboarding or a camera.\n- `@Environment(\\.dismiss)` closes the current sheet **or** pops the current pushed screen.\n\n## iPad: `NavigationSplitView`\n\nOn iPad and Mac, a sidebar + detail layout is common. `NavigationSplitView` provides it, and automatically collapses into a stack on iPhone:\n\n```swift\nNavigationSplitView {\n    List(categories, selection: $selectedCategory) { … }     // sidebar\n} detail: {\n    RecipeGrid(category: selectedCategory)                   // detail\n}\n```\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- One `NavigationStack` at the root; `NavigationLink(value:)` + `.navigationDestination(for:)`.\n- Bind a `path` for programmatic navigation: append to push, `removeAll()` to pop to root.\n- `.navigationTitle`, `.toolbar`, `.searchable` configure the navigation bar.\n- `TabView` + `Tab` for top-level sections, each with its own stack.\n- `.sheet` / `.fullScreenCover` are state-driven; `dismiss` closes them.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\nBuild a small three-level app:\n\n1. A **Categories** list (Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner) inside a `NavigationStack`.\n2. Tapping a category pushes a list of 5 made-up recipes for it.\n3. Tapping a recipe pushes a detail screen with a **Done cooking** button that pops all the way back to Categories. (Hint: use a `NavigationPath` because the values have different types.)\n4. Add a toolbar **+** button on Categories that opens a sheet.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct Category: Hashable { let name: String }\nstruct Dish: Hashable { let name: String; let category: String }\n\nstruct CookbookView: View {\n    @State private var path = NavigationPath()\n    @State private var showAbout = false\n    let categories = [\"Breakfast\", \"Lunch\", \"Dinner\"].map(Category.init)\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack(path: $path) {\n            List(categories, id: \\.self) { category in\n                NavigationLink(category.name, value: category)\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Categories\")\n            .toolbar {\n                Button(\"About\", systemImage: \"plus\") { showAbout = true }\n            }\n            .navigationDestination(for: Category.self) { category in\n                List(1...5, id: \\.self) { i in\n                    NavigationLink(\"\\(category.name) idea \\(i)\",\n                                   value: Dish(name: \"\\(category.name) idea \\(i)\", category: category.name))\n                }\n                .navigationTitle(category.name)\n            }\n            .navigationDestination(for: Dish.self) { dish in\n                VStack(spacing: 20) {\n                    Text(dish.name).font(.title)\n                    Button(\"Done cooking\") { path = NavigationPath() }   // pop to root\n                        .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n                }\n                .navigationTitle(dish.category)\n            }\n            .sheet(isPresented: $showAbout) {\n                Text(\"New category coming soon!\").presentationDetents([.medium])\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { CookbookView() }\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What must a value pushed with `NavigationLink(value:)` conform to?\n- [ ] Codable\n- [x] Hashable\n- [ ] Identifiable only\n- [ ] View\n> Navigation values are stored in the path, which requires Hashable.\n\nQ: How do you pop to the root of a NavigationStack bound to `path: [Int]`?\n- [ ] dismiss()\n- [x] path.removeAll()\n- [ ] path.append(0)\n- [ ] Recreate the NavigationStack\n> The path is the stack; emptying it shows the root.\n\nQ: A detail screen pushed onto a stack needs its own NavigationStack. True?\n- [ ] True\n- [x] False. There's one stack at the root; pushed screens are part of it\n- [ ] Only on iPad\n- [ ] Only if it has a toolbar\n> Nested NavigationStacks cause confusing, broken navigation.\n\nQ: What does `@Environment(\\.dismiss)` do when called in a sheet?\n- [ ] Deletes the sheet's data\n- [x] Closes the sheet\n- [ ] Pops to the root of the app\n- [ ] Nothing, it's only for alerts\n> dismiss closes the current presentation, or pops the current pushed screen.\n```\n"},{"id":"observation-mvvm","title":"Observation & MVVM: View Models","part":4,"minutes":40,"summary":"Move logic and state out of views into @Observable models. Model loading states, share models with @Environment, and use @Bindable, the modern way to structure SwiftUI screens.","body":":::goals\n- The `@Observable` macro and how SwiftUI tracks what you read\n- Owning a model with `@State`, passing it down, editing it with `@Bindable`\n- Sharing models app-wide with `@Environment`\n- The MVVM pattern: views, view models, models\n- Loading / loaded / failed states with an enum\n- Making view models `@MainActor`\n:::\n\n## Why move logic out of views?\n\nSo far, state has lived in views with `@State`. That's perfect for simple UI state, like whether a section is expanded. But as screens grow:\n\n- Views fill up with logic (validation, loading, filtering) that's hard to test.\n- Several views need to share the same data.\n- Previews and tests need a way to substitute sample data.\n\nThe answer is an **observable model class**.\n\n## `@Observable`\n\nMark a class with the `@Observable` macro and SwiftUI automatically tracks which of its properties each view **reads**. When one of those properties changes, only the views that read it update.\n\n```swift title=\"CounterModel.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\nimport Observation\n\n@Observable\nfinal class CounterModel {\n    var count = 0\n    var step = 1\n\n    func increment() { count += step }\n    func reset() { count = 0 }\n}\n\nstruct ObservedCounterView: View {\n    @State private var model = CounterModel()      // this view OWNS the model\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 16) {\n            Text(\"\\(model.count)\").font(.system(size: 60, weight: .bold, design: .rounded))\n            Stepper(\"Step: \\(model.step)\", value: $model.step, in: 1...10)\n            HStack {\n                Button(\"Add\", action: model.increment).buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n                Button(\"Reset\", action: model.reset).buttonStyle(.bordered)\n            }\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { ObservedCounterView() }\n```\n\n- **Own** a model with `@State` so it's created once and kept alive for the view's lifetime.\n- `$model.step` gives a binding to a property of the model.\n- No special property wrappers are needed inside the class: just `var`s.\n\n:::note Older code: ObservableObject\nBefore iOS 17, the equivalent was `class X: ObservableObject` with `@Published` properties and `@StateObject` / `@ObservedObject` / `@EnvironmentObject` in views. You'll see it in older tutorials and codebases. `@Observable` is simpler and more efficient; use it for new code.\n:::\n\n## Passing models to child views\n\nA child that only **reads** a model just takes it as a normal property. SwiftUI still tracks what it reads:\n\n```swift\nstruct CountBadge: View {\n    let model: CounterModel          // no wrapper needed to read and observe\n    var body: some View { Text(\"Count: \\(model.count)\") }\n}\n```\n\nA child that needs **bindings** to the model's properties uses **`@Bindable`**:\n\n```swift\nstruct StepEditor: View {\n    @Bindable var model: CounterModel\n    var body: some View {\n        Stepper(\"Step: \\(model.step)\", value: $model.step)   // $ works thanks to @Bindable\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Sharing app-wide with `@Environment`\n\nFor models many screens need (the signed-in user, a shopping cart, settings), put them in the **environment** once, and any view can read them:\n\n```swift title=\"CartExample.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\n@Observable\nfinal class Cart {\n    var items: [String] = []\n    var count: Int { items.count }\n    func add(_ item: String) { items.append(item) }\n}\n\nstruct ShopView: View {\n    @Environment(Cart.self) private var cart            // read it from the environment\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 12) {\n            ForEach([\"Flour\", \"Eggs\", \"Milk\"], id: \\.self) { item in\n                Button(\"Add \\(item)\") { cart.add(item) }\n            }\n            CartBadge()\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct CartBadge: View {\n    @Environment(Cart.self) private var cart\n    var body: some View { Label(\"\\(cart.count) items\", systemImage: \"cart\") }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    ShopView()\n        .environment(Cart())                             // provide it at the top\n}\n```\n\nIn the app you'd create it once in your `App` struct: `@State private var cart = Cart()` and `.environment(cart)` on the `WindowGroup`'s root view.\n\n:::warning Missing environment objects crash\nIf a view reads `@Environment(Cart.self)` but no ancestor provided one with `.environment(...)`, the app crashes. Remember to provide it in previews too.\n:::\n\n## MVVM in SwiftUI\n\n**MVVM** (Model–View–ViewModel) is the most common architecture for SwiftUI screens:\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div>Model<small>plain data: structs like Recipe</small></div>\n<div class=\"arrow\">←</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">ViewModel<small>@Observable class: state + logic</small></div>\n<div class=\"arrow\">⇄</div>\n<div>View<small>SwiftUI struct: displays state, forwards actions</small></div>\n</div></div>\n\n- The **View** displays the view model's state and calls its methods on user actions. It contains no business logic.\n- The **ViewModel** holds the screen's state, loads data (via services, next lesson), validates input and exposes simple properties for the view.\n- The **Model** is your plain data.\n\n## Loading states\n\nModel \"loading → success or failure\" with an enum, so impossible combinations (loading *and* failed at once) can't happen:\n\n```swift title=\"QuotesScreen.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct Quote: Identifiable {\n    let id: Int\n    let text: String\n    let author: String\n}\n\nenum LoadState<Value> {\n    case idle\n    case loading\n    case loaded(Value)\n    case failed(String)\n}\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class QuotesViewModel {\n    private(set) var state: LoadState<[Quote]> = .idle      // views can read, only the VM can write\n\n    func load() async {\n        state = .loading\n        do {\n            try await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(1))          // pretend network call\n            if Bool.random() { throw URLError(.notConnectedToInternet) }\n            state = .loaded([\n                Quote(id: 1, text: \"Simplicity is prerequisite for reliability.\", author: \"Edsger Dijkstra\"),\n                Quote(id: 2, text: \"Make it work, make it right, make it fast.\", author: \"Kent Beck\")\n            ])\n        } catch {\n            state = .failed(\"Couldn't load quotes. Check your connection.\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct QuotesScreen: View {\n    @State private var viewModel = QuotesViewModel()\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            Group {\n                switch viewModel.state {\n                case .idle, .loading:\n                    ProgressView(\"Loading…\")\n                case .loaded(let quotes):\n                    List(quotes) { quote in\n                        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 4) {\n                            Text(\"“\\(quote.text)”\")\n                            Text(quote.author).font(.caption).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n                        }\n                    }\n                    .refreshable { await viewModel.load() }\n                case .failed(let message):\n                    ContentUnavailableView {\n                        Label(\"Offline\", systemImage: \"wifi.slash\")\n                    } description: {\n                        Text(message)\n                    } actions: {\n                        Button(\"Try again\") { Task { await viewModel.load() } }\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Quotes\")\n            .task { await viewModel.load() }                   // load when the screen appears\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { QuotesScreen() }\n```\n\nNote these details:\n\n- **`@MainActor`** on the view model: its state drives the UI, so it lives on the main actor. `await` calls inside still don't block.\n- **`private(set)`**: the view can read `state` but can't change it. All changes go through the view model's methods.\n- `.task` loads once when the view appears; `.refreshable` enables pull-to-refresh.\n\n:::tip The screen stays simple\nLook at `QuotesScreen`: it contains **no logic**, just a `switch` over states. That makes the UI easy to reason about, and the view model easy to test (Lesson 31).\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `@Observable` classes: SwiftUI tracks exactly the properties each view reads.\n- Own with `@State`, pass as a plain property, edit with `@Bindable`, share with `.environment()` / `@Environment(Type.self)`.\n- MVVM: views display and forward actions; view models hold state and logic.\n- Model loading with an enum; mark view models `@MainActor`; expose state with `private(set)`.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\nRefactor the **tip calculator** from Lesson 15 into MVVM:\n\n1. Create `@Observable final class TipViewModel` with `amount`, `percent` and `roundUp`, and computed `tip` and `total`.\n2. The view owns it with `@State` and binds controls with `$viewModel.amount` and so on.\n3. Extract the picker into a `TipPicker` that takes `@Bindable var viewModel: TipViewModel`.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\n@Observable\nfinal class TipViewModel {\n    var amount = 0.0\n    var percent = 15\n    var roundUp = false\n\n    var tip: Double {\n        let raw = amount * Double(percent) / 100\n        return roundUp ? raw.rounded(.up) : raw\n    }\n    var total: Double { amount + tip }\n}\n\nstruct TipPicker: View {\n    @Bindable var viewModel: TipViewModel\n    var body: some View {\n        Picker(\"Tip\", selection: $viewModel.percent) {\n            ForEach([10, 15, 20], id: \\.self) { Text(\"\\($0)%\").tag($0) }\n        }\n        .pickerStyle(.segmented)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct TipScreen: View {\n    @State private var viewModel = TipViewModel()\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Form {\n            TextField(\"Amount\", value: $viewModel.amount, format: .number)\n                .keyboardType(.decimalPad)\n            TipPicker(viewModel: viewModel)\n            Toggle(\"Round up\", isOn: $viewModel.roundUp)\n            LabeledContent(\"Tip\", value: viewModel.tip.formatted(.currency(code: \"USD\")))\n            LabeledContent(\"Total\", value: viewModel.total.formatted(.currency(code: \"USD\")))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { TipScreen() }\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: How should a view create and own an @Observable view model?\n- [ ] `let viewModel = MyViewModel()`\n- [x] `@State private var viewModel = MyViewModel()`\n- [ ] `@Binding var viewModel: MyViewModel`\n- [ ] `@Published var viewModel`\n> @State keeps the instance alive across view updates.\n\nQ: A child view needs a binding to a property of an @Observable object it receives. What does it use?\n- [ ] @State\n- [ ] @Environment\n- [x] @Bindable\n- [ ] @Binding\n> @Bindable enables $model.property bindings on an observable object.\n\nQ: Why model loading as `enum LoadState { case loading, loaded(T), failed(String) }` instead of several Booleans?\n- [ ] Enums are faster\n- [x] Impossible combinations, like loading and failed at the same time, can't be represented\n- [ ] Booleans can't be observed\n- [ ] SwiftUI requires enums\n> One enum value means exactly one state at a time.\n\nQ: Why mark a view model `@MainActor`?\n- [ ] To make network calls run on the main thread\n- [x] Because its state drives the UI, which must be updated on the main actor\n- [ ] To make it a singleton\n- [ ] It's required for @Observable\n> UI state belongs on the main actor; awaited work still doesn't block it.\n```\n"},{"id":"architecture-di","title":"App Architecture & Dependency Injection","part":4,"minutes":30,"summary":"Structure an app so it scales. Covers layers, services behind protocols, dependency injection through initialisers and the environment, previews with mock data, and a folder structure that grows with you.","body":":::goals\n- The layers of a well-structured app\n- Services (repositories) behind protocols\n- **Dependency injection** through initialisers\n- Mocks for previews and tests\n- Injecting dependencies through the SwiftUI environment\n- A folder structure that scales, and Swift packages for modules\n:::\n\n## Why architecture?\n\nA small app can live in a few files. As it grows, code without structure becomes a tangle: views call the network directly, the same data is loaded in five places, and changing one thing breaks another. **Architecture** means rules for where code goes, so that:\n\n- each type has **one clear job**,\n- parts can be **tested** and **previewed** in isolation,\n- you can **swap** one part (e.g. the API) without rewriting the rest.\n\n## The layers\n\n<div class=\"diagram\">\n<div class=\"layers\">\n<div class=\"layer ui\"><h5>Presentation</h5><div class=\"boxes\"><span>SwiftUI Views</span><span>@Observable ViewModels</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"dir\">↓ calls async methods · ↑ returns models</div>\n<div class=\"layer domain\"><h5>Services / Repositories</h5><div class=\"boxes\"><span>RecipeService (protocol)</span><span>LiveRecipeService</span><span>MockRecipeService</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"dir\">↓ ↑</div>\n<div class=\"layer data\"><h5>Data sources</h5><div class=\"boxes\"><span>URLSession (network)</span><span>SwiftData (database)</span><span>UserDefaults / Keychain / files</span></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"diagram-caption\">Dependencies point downwards. Lower layers never know about views.</div>\n</div>\n\n- **Views** display state and forward user actions.\n- **View models** hold screen state and coordinate: \"load recipes, filter them, handle errors\".\n- **Services** (also called repositories or clients) know *how* to get and save data: which URL, which database query, which cache.\n\n## Services behind protocols\n\nDefine **what** the app needs as a protocol:\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeService.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct Recipe: Identifiable, Hashable, Codable, Sendable {\n    let id: Int\n    let name: String\n    let cuisine: String\n}\n\nprotocol RecipeService: Sendable {\n    func fetchRecipes() async throws -> [Recipe]\n}\n```\n\nThen write implementations. The real one talks to the network (Lesson 23 builds it properly):\n\n```swift title=\"LiveRecipeService.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct LiveRecipeService: RecipeService {\n    func fetchRecipes() async throws -> [Recipe] {\n        let url = URL(string: \"https://dummyjson.com/recipes?limit=0&select=name,cuisine\")!\n        let (data, _) = try await URLSession.shared.data(from: url)\n        struct Response: Decodable { let recipes: [Recipe] }\n        return try JSONDecoder().decode(Response.self, from: data).recipes\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnd a mock returns sample data instantly, which is perfect for previews and tests:\n\n```swift title=\"MockRecipeService.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct MockRecipeService: RecipeService {\n    var recipes: [Recipe] = [\n        Recipe(id: 1, name: \"Margherita Pizza\", cuisine: \"Italian\"),\n        Recipe(id: 2, name: \"Pad Thai\", cuisine: \"Thai\")\n    ]\n    var shouldFail = false\n\n    func fetchRecipes() async throws -> [Recipe] {\n        if shouldFail { throw URLError(.notConnectedToInternet) }\n        return recipes\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Dependency injection\n\n**Dependency injection (DI)** means a type **receives** the things it depends on instead of creating them itself. The simplest form is passing them into the initialiser:\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeListViewModel.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class RecipeListViewModel {\n    private let service: any RecipeService          // depends on the PROTOCOL\n    private(set) var recipes: [Recipe] = []\n    private(set) var errorMessage: String?\n\n    init(service: any RecipeService) {             // injected\n        self.service = service\n    }\n\n    func load() async {\n        do {\n            recipes = try await service.fetchRecipes()\n            errorMessage = nil\n        } catch {\n            errorMessage = \"Couldn't load recipes.\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct RecipeListScreen: View {\n    @State private var viewModel: RecipeListViewModel\n\n    init(service: any RecipeService) {\n        _viewModel = State(initialValue: RecipeListViewModel(service: service))\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        List(viewModel.recipes) { recipe in\n            LabeledContent(recipe.name, value: recipe.cuisine)\n        }\n        .overlay {\n            if let message = viewModel.errorMessage {\n                ContentUnavailableView(message, systemImage: \"wifi.slash\")\n            }\n        }\n        .task { await viewModel.load() }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Sample data\") {\n    RecipeListScreen(service: MockRecipeService())\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Error\") {\n    RecipeListScreen(service: MockRecipeService(shouldFail: true))\n}\n```\n\nNow:\n\n- The **app** passes `LiveRecipeService()`.\n- **Previews** pass `MockRecipeService()`: instant, offline, and able to show the error state on demand.\n- **Tests** pass a mock and check the view model's behaviour (Lesson 31).\n\n`_viewModel = State(initialValue: …)` is how you initialise a `@State` property from an initialiser parameter. The underscore accesses the property wrapper itself.\n\n:::analogy\nWithout DI, a class goes shopping for its own ingredients, so you can't give it different ones. With DI, ingredients are delivered to the kitchen door. In tests you deliver fake ingredients, and the cook never knows the difference.\n:::\n\n## Injecting through the environment\n\nPassing services through every initialiser gets tedious when they're needed deep in the view tree. SwiftUI's environment can carry them. Define an environment value with the `@Entry` macro:\n\n```swift title=\"Dependencies.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nextension EnvironmentValues {\n    @Entry var recipeService: any RecipeService = LiveRecipeService()   // default value\n}\n```\n\nRead it in any view, and override it wherever you like:\n\n```swift\nstruct RecipesRoot: View {\n    @Environment(\\.recipeService) private var service\n\n    var body: some View {\n        RecipeListScreen(service: service)\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    RecipesRoot()\n        .environment(\\.recipeService, MockRecipeService())   // swap in the mock\n}\n```\n\nChoose whichever is clearer: initialiser injection is explicit and great for view models; the environment is convenient for app-wide services.\n\n## A folder structure that scales\n\nGroup by **feature**, with shared code alongside:\n\n<div class=\"tree\">RecipeBox/\n├── App/\n│   ├── RecipeBoxApp.swift\n│   └── Dependencies.swift\n├── Models/                 ← Recipe, Ingredient…\n├── Services/               ← RecipeService, LiveRecipeService, RecipeCache\n├── Features/\n│   ├── Home/               ← HomeView.swift, HomeViewModel.swift\n│   ├── Detail/\n│   ├── Favorites/\n│   └── Settings/\n├── Components/             ← reusable views: RecipeCard, RatingView…\n├── Resources/              ← Assets.xcassets, Localizable.xcstrings\n└── Preview Content/        ← mocks and sample data</div>\n\nFor large apps, teams split features into **local Swift packages** (**File → New → Package**) so each module builds and tests independently. For this course, folders are plenty.\n\n:::note Other architectures\nYou'll hear about alternatives like **TCA** (The Composable Architecture), **VIPER** and **Clean Architecture**. They share the same goals: separation, testability, clear data flow. MVVM with injected services is the most common starting point, and it's what the capstone uses.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Views → view models → services → data sources. Dependencies point down.\n- Hide data access behind **protocols**; write live and mock implementations.\n- **Inject** dependencies through initialisers (or the environment with `@Entry`).\n- Mocks make previews instant and tests reliable.\n- Organise folders by feature.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Sketch the layers for a **weather app** that shows today's forecast (from an API) and a list of saved cities (stored locally). Name the view models, the service protocols and their methods, and the data sources.\n2. Take `QuotesViewModel` from the previous lesson and extract the fake network call into a `QuoteService` protocol with a mock that can be told to fail. Inject it, and add two previews: success and failure.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show a possible answer for task 1\n- **Views / view models:** `ForecastView` + `ForecastViewModel`; `CitiesView` + `CitiesViewModel`.\n- **Services:** `protocol WeatherService { func forecast(for city: City) async throws -> Forecast }` and `protocol CityStore { func savedCities() throws -> [City]; func save(_ city: City) throws; func delete(_ city: City) throws }`.\n- **Implementations:** `LiveWeatherService` (URLSession), `SwiftDataCityStore` (SwiftData); `MockWeatherService` and `InMemoryCityStore` for previews and tests.\n- View models receive `any WeatherService` and `any CityStore` in their initialisers.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What is dependency injection?\n- [ ] Downloading packages\n- [x] Giving a type its dependencies from outside instead of creating them itself\n- [ ] Injecting code at runtime\n- [ ] A way to speed up builds\n> DI decouples a type from the concrete implementations it uses.\n\nQ: Why should a view model depend on `any RecipeService` rather than `LiveRecipeService`?\n- [ ] Protocols are faster\n- [x] So previews and tests can pass a mock implementation\n- [ ] Swift requires protocols\n- [ ] Live services can't be stored\n> Depending on the protocol makes the implementation swappable.\n\nQ: Which layer should contain URLSession code?\n- [ ] Views\n- [ ] View models\n- [x] Services / data sources\n- [ ] The App struct\n> Views and view models shouldn't know how data is fetched.\n\nQ: How do you initialise a `@State` property from an init parameter?\n- [x] `_viewModel = State(initialValue: MyViewModel(service: service))`\n- [ ] `self.viewModel = service`\n- [ ] `@State(service) var viewModel`\n- [ ] You can't\n> The underscore accesses the State property wrapper directly.\n```\n"},{"id":"networking","title":"Networking with URLSession & Codable","part":5,"minutes":40,"summary":"Fetch real data from the internet. HTTP and JSON basics, decoding with Codable, URLSession with async/await, handling errors and status codes, and showing it all in SwiftUI.","body":":::goals\n- HTTP, REST and JSON in a nutshell\n- Decoding JSON into Swift types with `Codable`\n- Custom keys, nested JSON, dates and optional fields\n- `URLSession` with `async`/`await`\n- Checking status codes and handling errors properly\n- Sending data with POST\n:::\n\n## HTTP & JSON in 60 seconds\n\nApps talk to servers over **HTTP**. A REST API exposes data at URLs called *endpoints*. You send a **request** (`GET` to read, `POST` to create, `PUT`/`PATCH` to update, `DELETE` to remove) and get a **response** with a **status code** (`200` OK, `404` not found, `500` server error) and usually a **JSON** body.\n\nWe'll use **[DummyJSON](https://dummyjson.com)**, a free test API that needs no key. Open this in your browser:\n\n```text\nhttps://dummyjson.com/quotes/random\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": 378,\n  \"quote\": \"If you tell the truth, you don't have to remember anything.\",\n  \"author\": \"Mark Twain\"\n}\n```\n\n:::note HTTPS only\niOS's **App Transport Security** blocks plain `http://` connections by default. Always use `https://`. (Exceptions can be configured in Info.plist, but avoid them.) Unlike some platforms, iOS apps need no special permission to use the internet.\n:::\n\n## Decoding JSON with `Codable`\n\nDefine a struct whose properties match the JSON keys, and conform to **`Decodable`** (or **`Codable`**, which is Decodable + Encodable). Swift generates all the parsing code:\n\n```swift title=\"Quote.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct Quote: Codable, Identifiable, Hashable {\n    let id: Int\n    let quote: String\n    let author: String\n}\n```\n\n```swift runnable\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct Quote: Codable, Identifiable {\n    let id: Int\n    let quote: String\n    let author: String\n}\n\nlet json = \"\"\"\n{ \"id\": 378, \"quote\": \"If you tell the truth, you don't have to remember anything.\", \"author\": \"Mark Twain\" }\n\"\"\".data(using: .utf8)!\n\ndo {\n    let quote = try JSONDecoder().decode(Quote.self, from: json)\n    print(\"“\\(quote.quote)” — \\(quote.author)\")\n} catch {\n    print(\"Decoding failed: \\(error)\")\n}\n```\n\n### Nested JSON, renamed keys, optional fields\n\nReal APIs rarely match your ideal model. Codable handles it:\n\n```swift runnable\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct QuotePage: Decodable {\n    let quotes: [QuoteDTO]          // nested array\n    let total: Int\n}\n\nstruct QuoteDTO: Decodable {\n    let id: Int\n    let text: String                // we prefer \"text\" over the API's \"quote\"\n    let author: String\n    let tags: [String]?             // optional: may be missing from the JSON\n\n    enum CodingKeys: String, CodingKey {\n        case id, author, tags\n        case text = \"quote\"         // map JSON \"quote\" → Swift `text`\n    }\n}\n\nlet json = \"\"\"\n{\n  \"quotes\": [\n    { \"id\": 1, \"quote\": \"Your heart is the size of an ocean.\", \"author\": \"Rumi\" },\n    { \"id\": 2, \"quote\": \"Stay hungry, stay foolish.\", \"author\": \"Stewart Brand\", \"tags\": [\"life\"] }\n  ],\n  \"total\": 1454,\n  \"skip\": 0,\n  \"limit\": 2\n}\n\"\"\".data(using: .utf8)!\n\nlet page = try JSONDecoder().decode(QuotePage.self, from: json)\nfor q in page.quotes {\n    print(q.id, q.text, \"—\", q.author, q.tags ?? [])\n}\nprint(\"Total quotes on the server:\", page.total)\n```\n\n- **Extra JSON keys are ignored** (`skip` and `limit` above), so APIs can add fields without breaking your app.\n- A **missing key** for a non-optional property throws an error, so make properties optional when the API might omit them.\n- For `snake_case` APIs, set `decoder.keyDecodingStrategy = .convertFromSnakeCase` so `image_url` maps to `imageUrl` automatically.\n- For dates, set `decoder.dateDecodingStrategy = .iso8601`.\n\n## Fetching with `URLSession`\n\n```swift title=\"QuoteService.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct QuoteDTO: Decodable {\n    let id: Int\n    let quote: String\n    let author: String\n}\n\nenum APIError: LocalizedError {\n    case badStatus(Int)\n    case decoding\n\n    var errorDescription: String? {\n        switch self {\n        case .badStatus(let code): \"The server responded with an error (\\(code)).\"\n        case .decoding: \"The data from the server was in an unexpected format.\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct QuoteService {\n    private let baseURL = URL(string: \"https://dummyjson.com\")!\n    private let session: URLSession = .shared\n\n    func randomQuote() async throws -> QuoteDTO {\n        let url = baseURL.appending(path: \"quotes/random\")\n        let (data, response) = try await session.data(from: url)   // suspends, never blocks the UI\n\n        guard let http = response as? HTTPURLResponse, (200..<300).contains(http.statusCode) else {\n            throw APIError.badStatus((response as? HTTPURLResponse)?.statusCode ?? -1)\n        }\n        do {\n            return try JSONDecoder().decode(QuoteDTO.self, from: data)\n        } catch {\n            throw APIError.decoding\n        }\n    }\n\n    func quotes(limit: Int = 20, skip: Int = 0) async throws -> [QuoteDTO] {\n        var components = URLComponents(url: baseURL.appending(path: \"quotes\"), resolvingAgainstBaseURL: false)!\n        components.queryItems = [                       // builds ?limit=20&skip=0 safely\n            URLQueryItem(name: \"limit\", value: String(limit)),\n            URLQueryItem(name: \"skip\", value: String(skip))\n        ]\n        let (data, _) = try await session.data(from: components.url!)\n        struct Page: Decodable { let quotes: [QuoteDTO] }\n        return try JSONDecoder().decode(Page.self, from: data).quotes\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey points:\n\n- `try await session.data(from:)` returns the response body (`Data`) and metadata (`URLResponse`).\n- **Always check the status code.** URLSession doesn't throw for `404` or `500`; it only throws for transport problems (no internet, timeout, cancelled).\n- Build URLs with `appending(path:)` and `URLComponents` instead of gluing strings together. That handles escaping for you.\n\n## Showing it in SwiftUI\n\nCombine the service with the view model pattern from Lesson 21:\n\n```swift title=\"RandomQuoteView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class RandomQuoteViewModel {\n    enum State { case loading, loaded(QuoteDTO), failed(String) }\n\n    private(set) var state: State = .loading\n    private let service = QuoteService()\n\n    func load() async {\n        state = .loading\n        do {\n            state = .loaded(try await service.randomQuote())\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n            // the view went away, nothing to show\n        } catch {\n            state = .failed(error.localizedDescription)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct RandomQuoteView: View {\n    @State private var viewModel = RandomQuoteViewModel()\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 20) {\n            switch viewModel.state {\n            case .loading:\n                ProgressView()\n            case .loaded(let quote):\n                Text(\"“\\(quote.quote)”\").font(.title2).multilineTextAlignment(.center)\n                Text(\"— \\(quote.author)\").foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n            case .failed(let message):\n                ContentUnavailableView(\"Couldn't load a quote\", systemImage: \"wifi.slash\",\n                                       description: Text(message))\n            }\n            Button(\"Another quote\") { Task { await viewModel.load() } }\n                .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n        }\n        .padding()\n        .task { await viewModel.load() }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { RandomQuoteView() }\n```\n\nRun it (the preview works too, and makes a real network request). Turn on airplane mode in the Simulator's host Mac, or use a bad URL, to see the error state.\n\n:::tip Friendly error messages\n`URLError` has useful codes. `.notConnectedToInternet`, `.timedOut` and `.cannotFindHost` can become messages like \"You're offline\". Never show raw error dumps to users.\n:::\n\n## Sending data: POST with a JSON body\n\n```swift title=\"PostExample.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct NewPost: Encodable {\n    let title: String\n    let userId: Int\n}\n\nstruct CreatedPost: Decodable {\n    let id: Int\n    let title: String\n}\n\nfunc createPost(title: String) async throws -> CreatedPost {\n    var request = URLRequest(url: URL(string: \"https://dummyjson.com/posts/add\")!)\n    request.httpMethod = \"POST\"\n    request.setValue(\"application/json\", forHTTPHeaderField: \"Content-Type\")\n    request.httpBody = try JSONEncoder().encode(NewPost(title: title, userId: 5))\n\n    let (data, response) = try await URLSession.shared.data(for: request)\n    guard (response as? HTTPURLResponse)?.statusCode == 201 else {\n        throw URLError(.badServerResponse)\n    }\n    return try JSONDecoder().decode(CreatedPost.self, from: data)\n}\n```\n\nFor APIs that need authentication, add a header: `request.setValue(\"Bearer \\(token)\", forHTTPHeaderField: \"Authorization\")`. Store tokens in the Keychain (Lesson 25), never in code or UserDefaults.\n\n## Debugging network calls\n\n- `print(String(decoding: data, as: UTF8.self))` shows the raw JSON while you debug.\n- When decoding fails, `print(error)` on a `DecodingError` tells you exactly which key or type didn't match.\n- Test endpoints in a browser or with `curl` first to see the real JSON shape.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `Codable` structs mirror JSON; use `CodingKeys` to rename, and optionals for keys that may be missing.\n- `try await URLSession.shared.data(from:)`: check the **status code** yourself.\n- Build URLs with `appending(path:)` and `URLComponents`.\n- Map errors to friendly messages; ignore `CancellationError`.\n- `URLRequest` + `JSONEncoder` for POST.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n:::exercise\n1. Build a **Quotes list** screen: load 30 quotes with `quotes(limit:skip:)` and show them in a `List`, with pull-to-refresh loading a random page (`skip: Int.random(in: 0...1400)`).\n2. Show `ContentUnavailableView` with a **Try again** button on failure.\n3. Add `.searchable` to filter the loaded quotes by author, locally.\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution\n```swift\nimport SwiftUI\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class QuoteListViewModel {\n    private(set) var quotes: [QuoteDTO] = []\n    private(set) var errorMessage: String?\n    private(set) var isLoading = false\n    var searchText = \"\"\n    private let service = QuoteService()\n\n    var filtered: [QuoteDTO] {\n        searchText.isEmpty ? quotes : quotes.filter { $0.author.localizedCaseInsensitiveContains(searchText) }\n    }\n\n    func load() async {\n        isLoading = true\n        defer { isLoading = false }\n        do {\n            quotes = try await service.quotes(limit: 30, skip: Int.random(in: 0...1400))\n            errorMessage = nil\n        } catch {\n            errorMessage = error.localizedDescription\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct QuoteListView: View {\n    @State private var viewModel = QuoteListViewModel()\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List(viewModel.filtered, id: \\.id) { q in\n                VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 4) {\n                    Text(\"“\\(q.quote)”\")\n                    Text(q.author).font(.caption).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n                }\n            }\n            .overlay {\n                if let message = viewModel.errorMessage, viewModel.quotes.isEmpty {\n                    ContentUnavailableView {\n                        Label(\"Offline\", systemImage: \"wifi.slash\")\n                    } description: {\n                        Text(message)\n                    } actions: {\n                        Button(\"Try again\") { Task { await viewModel.load() } }\n                    }\n                } else if viewModel.isLoading && viewModel.quotes.isEmpty {\n                    ProgressView()\n                }\n            }\n            .searchable(text: $viewModel.searchText, prompt: \"Filter by author\")\n            .refreshable { await viewModel.load() }\n            .task { await viewModel.load() }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Quotes\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { QuoteListView() }\n```\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What happens when the JSON contains keys your Decodable struct doesn't declare?\n- [ ] Decoding fails\n- [x] They're ignored\n- [ ] They're added as properties\n- [ ] The app crashes\n> Codable ignores unknown keys, but missing required keys do throw.\n\nQ: Does `URLSession.data(from:)` throw when the server returns 404?\n- [ ] Yes, always\n- [x] No. You must check the HTTPURLResponse status code yourself\n- [ ] Only on iOS 27\n- [ ] Only for POST requests\n> Transport errors throw; HTTP error statuses don't.\n\nQ: The API sends `\"quote\"` but you want the Swift property to be called `text`. What do you use?\n- [ ] Rename it in the JSON\n- [x] A CodingKeys enum with `case text = \"quote\"`\n- [ ] keyDecodingStrategy = .convertFromSnakeCase\n- [ ] A computed property only\n> CodingKeys maps JSON keys to property names.\n\nQ: Why use `URLComponents` with `queryItems` instead of string concatenation?\n- [ ] It's required for HTTPS\n- [x] It correctly escapes special characters in query values\n- [ ] It makes requests faster\n- [ ] String URLs are deprecated\n> Building URLs by hand breaks on spaces, ampersands and non-ASCII text.\n```\n"},{"id":"swiftdata","title":"Persisting Data with SwiftData","part":5,"minutes":40,"summary":"Save your app's data on the device with SwiftData. Define models with @Model, query them live with @Query, insert, update and delete, and use relationships and in-memory previews.","body":":::goals\n- When you need a database\n- Defining models with `@Model`\n- Setting up a `modelContainer`\n- Reading with `@Query` (sorting and filtering with `#Predicate`)\n- Inserting, updating and deleting with `modelContext`\n- Relationships, previews with in-memory data, and schema changes\n:::\n\n## Why SwiftData?\n\nUse a database when you need to store **structured data** that must survive app restarts: notes, tasks, favourites, workouts. **SwiftData** is Apple's modern persistence framework. It's built on the battle-tested Core Data engine (SQLite underneath) but designed for Swift and SwiftUI:\n\n- Models are plain Swift classes marked `@Model`.\n- `@Query` in a view **automatically updates** the UI when data changes.\n- It can sync through iCloud with a capability switch.\n\n## 1. Define a model\n\n```swift title=\"Note.swift\" nocheck\nimport Foundation\nimport SwiftData\n\n@Model\nfinal class Note {\n    var title: String\n    var body: String\n    var isPinned: Bool\n    var createdAt: Date\n\n    init(title: String, body: String = \"\", isPinned: Bool = false, createdAt: Date = .now) {\n        self.title = title\n        self.body = body\n        self.isPinned = isPinned\n        self.createdAt = createdAt\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`@Model` turns the class into a persistent, observable model. Every stored property becomes a column. Models must be classes, and need an initialiser.\n\n## 2. Set up the container\n\nTell your app which models to store, once, at the top:\n\n```swift title=\"NotesApp.swift\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\nimport SwiftData\n\n@main\nstruct NotesApp: App {\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup {\n            NotesListView()\n        }\n        .modelContainer(for: Note.self)      // creates/opens the database\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThat's all the setup. The container creates the database file in your app's sandbox and puts a **model context** into the SwiftUI environment.\n\n## 3. Query, insert, update and delete\n\n```swift title=\"NotesListView.swift\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\nimport SwiftData\n\nstruct NotesListView: View {\n    @Environment(\\.modelContext) private var context              // for writes\n    @Query(sort: [SortDescriptor(\\Note.isPinned, order: .reverse),\n                  SortDescriptor(\\Note.createdAt, order: .reverse)])\n    private var notes: [Note]                                       // live results\n    @State private var newTitle = \"\"\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List {\n                Section {\n                    HStack {\n                        TextField(\"New note\", text: $newTitle)\n                            .onSubmit(addNote)\n                        Button(\"Add\", action: addNote).disabled(newTitle.isEmpty)\n                    }\n                }\n                ForEach(notes) { note in\n                    NavigationLink(value: note) {\n                        Label(note.title, systemImage: note.isPinned ? \"pin.fill\" : \"note.text\")\n                    }\n                    .swipeActions(edge: .leading) {\n                        Button(note.isPinned ? \"Unpin\" : \"Pin\") {\n                            note.isPinned.toggle()                  // just change it: saved automatically\n                        }\n                        .tint(.orange)\n                    }\n                }\n                .onDelete { offsets in\n                    for index in offsets { context.delete(notes[index]) }\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Notes\")\n            .navigationDestination(for: Note.self) { note in\n                NoteEditor(note: note)\n            }\n            .overlay {\n                if notes.isEmpty {\n                    ContentUnavailableView(\"No notes\", systemImage: \"note.text\",\n                                           description: Text(\"Add your first note above.\"))\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private func addNote() {\n        context.insert(Note(title: newTitle))                        // insert\n        newTitle = \"\"\n    }\n}\n\nstruct NoteEditor: View {\n    @Bindable var note: Note                                         // models are observable\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Form {\n            TextField(\"Title\", text: $note.title)\n            TextField(\"Body\", text: $note.body, axis: .vertical)\n                .lineLimit(5...)\n            Toggle(\"Pinned\", isOn: $note.isPinned)\n        }\n        .navigationTitle(note.title)\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    NotesListView()\n        .modelContainer(for: Note.self, inMemory: true)              // throwaway data for previews\n}\n```\n\nLook how little code there is:\n\n- **`@Query`** fetches notes, sorted, and **re-runs automatically** whenever notes are inserted, changed or deleted. No manual refreshing.\n- **Insert** with `context.insert(_:)`, **delete** with `context.delete(_:)`.\n- **Update** by simply changing a property. Models are observable, and SwiftData **autosaves**.\n- `@Bindable var note` lets the editor bind text fields directly to the model.\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div>User action<small>insert / edit / delete</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">modelContext</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>Database</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">@Query re-runs → UI updates</div>\n</div></div>\n\n## Filtering with `#Predicate`\n\n```swift nocheck\n@Query(filter: #Predicate<Note> { $0.isPinned },\n       sort: \\Note.createdAt, order: .reverse)\nprivate var pinnedNotes: [Note]\n```\n\nFor a filter that depends on user input (like a search box), create the query in the initialiser of a subview:\n\n```swift title=\"SearchResults.swift\" nocheck\nstruct SearchResults: View {\n    @Query private var notes: [Note]\n\n    init(searchText: String) {\n        _notes = Query(filter: #Predicate<Note> { note in\n            searchText.isEmpty || note.title.localizedStandardContains(searchText)\n        }, sort: \\Note.createdAt)\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        List(notes) { Text($0.title) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Relationships\n\nModels can reference each other. SwiftData infers relationships from the property types:\n\n```swift nocheck\n@Model\nfinal class Notebook {\n    var name: String\n    @Relationship(deleteRule: .cascade, inverse: \\Page.notebook)\n    var pages: [Page] = []                    // one notebook → many pages\n    init(name: String) { self.name = name }\n}\n\n@Model\nfinal class Page {\n    var text: String\n    var notebook: Notebook?                   // each page belongs to a notebook\n    init(text: String) { self.text = text }\n}\n\n// Usage:\nlet notebook = Notebook(name: \"Recipes\")\ncontext.insert(notebook)\nnotebook.pages.append(Page(text: \"Grandma's lasagne\"))\n```\n\n`deleteRule: .cascade` deletes the pages when their notebook is deleted.\n\n## Unique values and useful attributes\n\n```swift nocheck\n@Model\nfinal class FavoriteRecipe {\n    @Attribute(.unique) var recipeID: Int     // no duplicates: inserting the same id updates instead\n    var name: String\n    @Attribute(.externalStorage) var photo: Data?   // large data stored outside the database\n    @Transient var isSelected = false               // not saved\n    init(recipeID: Int, name: String) { self.recipeID = recipeID; self.name = name }\n}\n```\n\n## Changing your models later\n\nWhen you add a new property with a **default value**, or add a new model, SwiftData migrates existing databases automatically (a *lightweight migration*). Renaming or changing types needs a `VersionedSchema` and a `SchemaMigrationPlan`, which is an advanced topic you'll look up when you need it. During early development you can also delete the app from the Simulator to start fresh.\n\n:::warning Existing users keep their data\nOnce your app is on the App Store, users have databases on their phones. Test that an update opens old data correctly before you release it.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `@Model final class` defines what's stored; `.modelContainer(for:)` sets up the database.\n- `@Query` reads (sorted, filtered with `#Predicate`) and updates the UI automatically.\n- `modelContext.insert` / `.delete`; change properties directly to update. SwiftData autosaves.\n- `@Bindable` binds forms to models; previews use `inMemory: true`.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\nExtend the notes app:\n\n1. Add a `category: String` property with a default value (`\"General\"`) and a `Picker` in the editor.\n2. Add `.searchable` to the list that filters by title using a `SearchResults`-style subview.\n3. Add a toolbar menu to sort by date or by title. (Hint: keep the sort choice in `@State` and pass `SortDescriptor`s to the subview's `Query`.)\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: How do you save changes after editing a property of a SwiftData model?\n- [ ] Call context.save() after every change, or nothing is stored\n- [x] Just change the property. SwiftData autosaves (you can still call save() explicitly)\n- [ ] Delete and re-insert the object\n- [ ] Write it to UserDefaults\n> Models are tracked by their context and saved automatically.\n\nQ: What does `@Query` do?\n- [ ] Fetches data once when the view appears\n- [x] Fetches models from the store and keeps the view updated as the data changes\n- [ ] Sends a network request\n- [ ] Creates the database\n> @Query is a live, observed fetch.\n\nQ: How should previews that use SwiftData be set up?\n- [ ] They can't use SwiftData\n- [x] With `.modelContainer(for: Model.self, inMemory: true)` so they use throwaway data\n- [ ] By copying the real database file\n- [ ] With UserDefaults\n> In-memory containers keep previews fast and isolated.\n\nQ: What does `@Attribute(.unique)` on `recipeID` do?\n- [ ] Encrypts the value\n- [x] Ensures no two models share that value; inserting a duplicate updates the existing one\n- [ ] Makes it optional\n- [ ] Excludes it from saving\n> Unique attributes give upsert behaviour.\n```\n"},{"id":"storage","title":"Settings, Files & the Keychain","part":5,"minutes":25,"summary":"Not everything belongs in a database. Store preferences with @AppStorage and UserDefaults, read and write files, and keep secrets safe in the Keychain.","body":":::goals\n- Choosing the right storage for each kind of data\n- `UserDefaults` and `@AppStorage` for small settings\n- A working in-app appearance setting (System / Light / Dark)\n- Reading and writing files in Documents and Caches\n- Storing tokens and passwords securely in the Keychain\n:::\n\n## Choosing storage\n\n| Data | Where | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Small settings and flags (\"dark mode\", \"has seen onboarding\") | `UserDefaults` / `@AppStorage` | Simple key–value storage |\n| Small UI state to restore (selected tab, draft) | `@SceneStorage` | Per-window state restoration (Lesson 19) |\n| Your app's structured data | SwiftData | Queries, relationships, live updates |\n| Downloaded JSON, images, exports | Files (Caches / Documents) | Large or file-shaped data |\n| Passwords, tokens, API keys | **Keychain** | Encrypted, and can survive reinstalls |\n\n## `@AppStorage`\n\n`@AppStorage` reads and writes `UserDefaults` and updates your views automatically, like `@State` that persists across launches:\n\n```swift title=\"SettingsView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nenum Appearance: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {\n    case system, light, dark\n    var id: Self { self }\n\n    var colorScheme: ColorScheme? {\n        switch self {\n        case .system: nil            // follow the system setting\n        case .light: .light\n        case .dark: .dark\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct SettingsView: View {\n    @AppStorage(\"appearance\") private var appearance = Appearance.system\n    @AppStorage(\"dailyTips\") private var dailyTips = true\n    @AppStorage(\"servingsDefault\") private var servings = 2\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Form {\n            Picker(\"Appearance\", selection: $appearance) {\n                ForEach(Appearance.allCases) { Text($0.rawValue.capitalized).tag($0) }\n            }\n            Toggle(\"Daily tips\", isOn: $dailyTips)\n            Stepper(\"Default servings: \\(servings)\", value: $servings, in: 1...12)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { SettingsView() }\n```\n\n`@AppStorage` supports `Bool`, `Int`, `Double`, `String`, `URL`, `Data`, and enums backed by `String` or `Int` raw values, like `Appearance` above.\n\n### Applying the appearance app-wide\n\nRead the same key at the top of the app and apply it with `.preferredColorScheme`:\n\n```swift title=\"SettingsApp.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct RootView: View {\n    @AppStorage(\"appearance\") private var appearance = Appearance.system\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            SettingsView().navigationTitle(\"Settings\")\n        }\n        .preferredColorScheme(appearance.colorScheme)   // nil = follow the system\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { RootView() }\n```\n\nChange the picker, and the whole app switches instantly. It's remembered after restart too. 🌙\n\n:::warning Keep UserDefaults small\n`UserDefaults` is loaded into memory at launch. Store small values only, never large arrays or images, and **never** passwords or tokens, since UserDefaults isn't encrypted.\n:::\n\n## Files\n\nEach app has a sandboxed container with standard folders:\n\n- **Documents**: user-created content that should be backed up.\n- **Caches**: re-downloadable data (API responses, images). The system may delete it when storage is low.\n- **Application Support**: app-managed files that should persist but aren't user documents.\n\n```swift runnable\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct Settings: Codable {\n    var favoriteCuisine: String\n    var lastOpened: Date\n}\n\nlet folder = URL.cachesDirectory                 // also: .documentsDirectory, .applicationSupportDirectory\nlet file = folder.appending(path: \"settings.json\")\n\ndo {\n    // Write\n    let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(Settings(favoriteCuisine: \"Italian\", lastOpened: .now))\n    try data.write(to: file, options: .atomic)   // atomic: never leaves a half-written file\n\n    // Read\n    let loaded = try JSONDecoder().decode(Settings.self, from: Data(contentsOf: file))\n    print(\"Loaded:\", loaded.favoriteCuisine)\n\n    // Check, list, delete\n    print(FileManager.default.fileExists(atPath: file.path()))\n    try FileManager.default.removeItem(at: file)\n    print(FileManager.default.fileExists(atPath: file.path()))\n} catch {\n    print(\"File error:\", error)\n}\n```\n\nCodable + files is a simple, powerful combination. You'll use it for offline caching in the next lesson.\n\n## The Keychain\n\nThe **Keychain** is the system's encrypted store for secrets: passwords, login tokens, API keys. Its API is a low-level C-style interface, so apps wrap it in a small helper:\n\n```swift title=\"KeychainStore.swift\"\nimport Foundation\nimport Security\n\nenum KeychainStore {\n    static func save(_ value: String, for key: String) throws {\n        let data = Data(value.utf8)\n        let query: [String: Any] = [\n            kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n            kSecAttrAccount as String: key\n        ]\n        SecItemDelete(query as CFDictionary)                  // replace any existing item\n        var attributes = query\n        attributes[kSecValueData as String] = data\n        attributes[kSecAttrAccessible as String] = kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlock\n        let status = SecItemAdd(attributes as CFDictionary, nil)\n        guard status == errSecSuccess else { throw KeychainError(status: status) }\n    }\n\n    static func read(_ key: String) -> String? {\n        let query: [String: Any] = [\n            kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n            kSecAttrAccount as String: key,\n            kSecReturnData as String: true,\n            kSecMatchLimit as String: kSecMatchLimitOne\n        ]\n        var result: AnyObject?\n        guard SecItemCopyMatching(query as CFDictionary, &result) == errSecSuccess,\n              let data = result as? Data else { return nil }\n        return String(decoding: data, as: UTF8.self)\n    }\n\n    static func delete(_ key: String) {\n        let query: [String: Any] = [\n            kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n            kSecAttrAccount as String: key\n        ]\n        SecItemDelete(query as CFDictionary)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct KeychainError: Error { let status: OSStatus }\n```\n\n```swift\ntry KeychainStore.save(token, for: \"authToken\")      // after logging in\nlet token = KeychainStore.read(\"authToken\")          // when making requests\nKeychainStore.delete(\"authToken\")                    // on sign-out\n```\n\n:::danger Never put secrets in your app's code\nAnything compiled into your app, including API keys in Swift files or Info.plist, can be extracted by anyone who downloads it. Real secrets belong on a server. The Keychain is for secrets that belong to **the user**, like their login token.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `@AppStorage` for small settings, which updates views automatically; `.preferredColorScheme` applies the appearance.\n- Files: `URL.cachesDirectory` / `.documentsDirectory` + Codable + atomic writes.\n- Keychain for tokens and passwords; never UserDefaults for secrets.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Add an **onboarding** screen shown only on first launch: use `@AppStorage(\"hasSeenOnboarding\")` and show it with `.fullScreenCover(isPresented:)`. A \"Get started\" button sets the flag.\n2. Save and load an array of `Codable` recent searches to a JSON file in Application Support, keeping only the 10 most recent.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: Where should you store a user's login token?\n- [ ] UserDefaults\n- [ ] A text file in Documents\n- [x] The Keychain\n- [ ] @AppStorage\n> The Keychain is encrypted and designed for secrets.\n\nQ: What does `@AppStorage(\"appearance\")` do?\n- [ ] Stores the value in memory only\n- [x] Reads and writes UserDefaults under that key, and updates views when it changes\n- [ ] Syncs to a server\n- [ ] Stores it in SwiftData\n> It's a SwiftUI-friendly wrapper around UserDefaults.\n\nQ: Which folder is right for re-downloadable API responses?\n- [ ] Documents\n- [x] Caches\n- [ ] The app bundle\n- [ ] Keychain\n> The system may clear Caches, which is fine for data you can fetch again.\n\nQ: Why write files with `.atomic`?\n- [ ] It compresses the file\n- [x] The write goes to a temporary file first, so a crash never leaves a half-written file\n- [ ] It encrypts the file\n- [ ] It's faster\n> Atomic writes protect against corrupted data.\n```\n"},{"id":"offline-caching","title":"Offline-First & Caching","part":5,"minutes":30,"summary":"Make your app feel instant and work without internet. Show cached data immediately, refresh in the background, detect connectivity, and handle stale data gracefully.","body":":::goals\n- What \"offline-first\" means and why users notice it\n- A disk cache for API responses using Codable and files\n- The \"show cached, then refresh\" pattern in a view model\n- Detecting connectivity with `NWPathMonitor`\n- Caching images with `URLCache`\n:::\n\n## The problem with network-only screens\n\nA network-only screen starts with a spinner every time and shows an error whenever the connection drops. Real users are on trains, in lifts and on weak mobile data. Compare:\n\n- **Network-only:** open → spinner → (maybe) data → go offline → error screen.\n- **Offline-first:** open → **cached data instantly** → fresh data quietly replaces it → go offline → still usable, with a small \"offline\" note.\n\n## The pattern\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div class=\"hl\">1. Load cache<small>instant</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>2. Show it</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">3. Fetch network</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>4. Save to cache + show</div>\n</div><div class=\"diagram-caption\">If step 3 fails, the user still has step 2's data, plus a gentle message.</div></div>\n\n## A simple disk cache\n\nStore any `Codable` value as a JSON file in the Caches folder, with the time it was saved:\n\n```swift title=\"DiskCache.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nactor DiskCache {\n    struct Entry<Value: Codable>: Codable {\n        let savedAt: Date\n        let value: Value\n    }\n\n    private let folder: URL\n\n    init(name: String = \"api-cache\") {\n        folder = URL.cachesDirectory.appending(path: name)\n        try? FileManager.default.createDirectory(at: folder, withIntermediateDirectories: true)\n    }\n\n    func save<Value: Codable>(_ value: Value, for key: String) throws {\n        let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(Entry(savedAt: .now, value: value))\n        try data.write(to: folder.appending(path: \"\\(key).json\"), options: .atomic)\n    }\n\n    func load<Value: Codable>(_ type: Value.Type, for key: String) -> Entry<Value>? {\n        guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: folder.appending(path: \"\\(key).json\")) else { return nil }\n        return try? JSONDecoder().decode(Entry<Value>.self, from: data)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIt's an `actor`, so reads and writes from different tasks can never clash.\n\n## A caching repository\n\nCombine the network service and the cache behind one type:\n\n```swift title=\"QuoteRepository.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct Quote: Codable, Identifiable, Hashable, Sendable {\n    let id: Int\n    let quote: String\n    let author: String\n}\n\nprotocol QuoteAPI: Sendable {\n    func fetchQuotes() async throws -> [Quote]\n}\n\nstruct LiveQuoteAPI: QuoteAPI {\n    func fetchQuotes() async throws -> [Quote] {\n        let url = URL(string: \"https://dummyjson.com/quotes?limit=50\")!\n        let (data, response) = try await URLSession.shared.data(from: url)\n        guard (response as? HTTPURLResponse)?.statusCode == 200 else { throw URLError(.badServerResponse) }\n        struct Page: Decodable { let quotes: [Quote] }\n        return try JSONDecoder().decode(Page.self, from: data).quotes\n    }\n}\n\nstruct QuoteRepository: Sendable {\n    let api: any QuoteAPI\n    let cache: DiskCache\n    private let key = \"quotes\"\n\n    /// Cached quotes and when they were saved, if any.\n    func cached() async -> (quotes: [Quote], savedAt: Date)? {\n        guard let entry = await cache.load([Quote].self, for: key) else { return nil }\n        return (entry.value, entry.savedAt)\n    }\n\n    /// Fetch fresh quotes from the network and update the cache.\n    func refresh() async throws -> [Quote] {\n        let fresh = try await api.fetchQuotes()\n        try? await cache.save(fresh, for: key)\n        return fresh\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## The view model\n\n```swift title=\"QuotesViewModel.swift\"\nimport Foundation\nimport Observation\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class OfflineQuotesViewModel {\n    private(set) var quotes: [Quote] = []\n    private(set) var lastUpdated: Date?\n    private(set) var isRefreshing = false\n    private(set) var errorMessage: String?\n\n    private let repository: QuoteRepository\n\n    init(repository: QuoteRepository) {\n        self.repository = repository\n    }\n\n    func load() async {\n        // 1–2. Show cached data immediately\n        if quotes.isEmpty, let cached = await repository.cached() {\n            quotes = cached.quotes\n            lastUpdated = cached.savedAt\n        }\n        // 3–4. Refresh from the network\n        await refresh()\n    }\n\n    func refresh() async {\n        isRefreshing = true\n        defer { isRefreshing = false }\n        do {\n            quotes = try await repository.refresh()\n            lastUpdated = .now\n            errorMessage = nil\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n            // ignore\n        } catch {\n            errorMessage = quotes.isEmpty\n                ? \"You're offline and there's nothing saved yet.\"\n                : \"You're offline. Showing saved quotes.\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## The view\n\n```swift title=\"OfflineQuotesView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct OfflineQuotesView: View {\n    @State private var viewModel: OfflineQuotesViewModel\n\n    init(repository: QuoteRepository) {\n        _viewModel = State(initialValue: OfflineQuotesViewModel(repository: repository))\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List {\n                if let message = viewModel.errorMessage, !viewModel.quotes.isEmpty {\n                    Label(message, systemImage: \"wifi.slash\")\n                        .font(.footnote)\n                        .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n                }\n                ForEach(viewModel.quotes) { quote in\n                    VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 4) {\n                        Text(\"“\\(quote.quote)”\")\n                        Text(quote.author).font(.caption).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            .overlay {\n                if viewModel.quotes.isEmpty {\n                    if let message = viewModel.errorMessage {\n                        ContentUnavailableView(\"No quotes\", systemImage: \"wifi.slash\", description: Text(message))\n                    } else {\n                        ProgressView()\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            .refreshable { await viewModel.refresh() }\n            .task { await viewModel.load() }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Quotes\")\n            .toolbar {\n                if let updated = viewModel.lastUpdated {\n                    ToolbarItem(placement: .status) {\n                        Text(\"Updated \\(updated, style: .relative) ago\").font(.caption)\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    OfflineQuotesView(repository: QuoteRepository(api: LiveQuoteAPI(), cache: DiskCache()))\n}\n```\n\nTest it: run the app online once, then turn off Wi-Fi on your Mac (the Simulator uses its connection) and relaunch. Quotes appear instantly with the offline note, and pull-to-refresh fails gently instead of wiping the screen. 🎉\n\n## Detecting connectivity\n\n`NWPathMonitor` from the Network framework reports connectivity changes. Use it to show an offline banner, or to retry automatically when the connection returns:\n\n```swift title=\"NetworkMonitor.swift\"\nimport Foundation\nimport Network\nimport Observation\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class NetworkMonitor {\n    private(set) var isConnected = true\n    private let monitor = NWPathMonitor()\n\n    init() {\n        monitor.pathUpdateHandler = { [weak self] path in\n            let connected = path.status == .satisfied\n            Task { @MainActor in self?.isConnected = connected }\n        }\n        monitor.start(queue: DispatchQueue(label: \"NetworkMonitor\"))\n    }\n\n    deinit { monitor.cancel() }\n}\n```\n\n:::tip Don't pre-check the network\nDon't use the monitor to *prevent* requests (\"no connection, so don't try\"). Connectivity information can be wrong or stale. Always attempt the request and handle failure; use the monitor for UI hints and automatic retries.\n:::\n\n## Caching images and HTTP responses\n\n`URLSession` and `AsyncImage` use a shared **`URLCache`** that respects the server's cache headers. You can make it bigger at app launch so images stay cached longer:\n\n```swift\nURLCache.shared = URLCache(memoryCapacity: 50_000_000,       // 50 MB in memory\n                           diskCapacity: 300_000_000)         // 300 MB on disk\n```\n\nFor offline-first *writes* (e.g. favouriting while offline), save the change locally first, which SwiftData makes easy, then sync to the server when possible.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Offline-first: show cached data instantly, refresh in the background, keep data on failure.\n- A small `actor` disk cache + Codable stores API responses with a timestamp.\n- The repository hides where data comes from; the view model manages refresh state and messages.\n- `NWPathMonitor` for connectivity hints; always attempt requests anyway.\n- A larger `URLCache` keeps images around.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Only refresh automatically when the cache is older than 10 minutes (pull-to-refresh always refreshes). Hint: compare `lastUpdated` with `Date.now.addingTimeInterval(-600)`.\n2. Add a `NetworkMonitor` to the environment and show an \"Offline\" banner at the top of the list when disconnected. Refresh automatically when the connection comes back (`.onChange(of: monitor.isConnected)`).\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: In an offline-first screen, what should the user see first when opening the app?\n- [ ] A spinner until the network responds\n- [x] The cached data, immediately, while fresh data loads\n- [ ] An empty list\n- [ ] An error if offline\n> Showing cached data instantly is the core of offline-first.\n\nQ: A refresh fails but cached data is on screen. What should happen?\n- [ ] Clear the list and show an error screen\n- [x] Keep the cached data and show a small, non-blocking message\n- [ ] Crash\n- [ ] Retry in a tight loop forever\n> Never throw away usable data because of a failed refresh.\n\nQ: Why is DiskCache an actor?\n- [ ] Actors are faster than structs\n- [x] So concurrent reads and writes from different tasks can't clash\n- [ ] Files can only be written from actors\n- [ ] To run on the main thread\n> Actors serialise access to shared mutable resources.\n\nQ: Should you skip a request because NWPathMonitor says you're offline?\n- [ ] Yes, always\n- [x] No. Attempt the request and handle failure; use the monitor for UI hints and retries\n- [ ] Only on cellular\n- [ ] Only for POST requests\n> Reachability can be wrong; the request itself is the real test.\n```\n"},{"id":"system-integration","title":"Sharing, Links, Permissions & Photos","part":6,"minutes":35,"summary":"Connect your app to the rest of iOS. Covers opening links, the share sheet, deep links into your app, the permission model with usage descriptions, the photo picker, and location.","body":":::goals\n- Opening web pages, phone numbers and other apps with `Link` and `openURL`\n- Sharing with `ShareLink`\n- Deep links: custom URL schemes and `onOpenURL`\n- How permissions work: usage descriptions and user prompts\n- Picking photos with `PhotosPicker` (no permission needed!)\n- Getting the user's location\n:::\n\n## Opening URLs\n\n```swift title=\"LinksDemo.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct LinksDemo: View {\n    @Environment(\\.openURL) private var openURL\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 16) {\n            Link(\"Open Swift.org\", destination: URL(string: \"https://swift.org\")!)   // opens Safari\n\n            Button(\"Call the restaurant\") {\n                openURL(URL(string: \"tel:+15551234567\")!)          // Phone app\n            }\n            Button(\"Email support\") {\n                openURL(URL(string: \"mailto:help@example.com?subject=RecipeBox\")!)\n            }\n            Button(\"Directions\") {\n                openURL(URL(string: \"https://maps.apple.com/?q=Pizza\")!)  // Apple Maps\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { LinksDemo() }\n```\n\nLinks in `Text` with Markdown are tappable too: `Text(\"Read our [privacy policy](https://example.com/privacy)\")`.\n\n## Sharing: `ShareLink`\n\n`ShareLink` opens the system **share sheet**: Messages, Mail, AirDrop, Notes, other apps and more.\n\n```swift\nShareLink(item: URL(string: \"https://dummyjson.com/recipes/1\")!) {\n    Label(\"Share recipe\", systemImage: \"square.and.arrow.up\")\n}\n\nShareLink(item: \"Try this pancake recipe! 🥞\",\n          subject: Text(\"Recipe idea\"),\n          message: Text(\"From RecipeBox\"))\n\n// Share a rendered image of a view:\nShareLink(item: Image(\"pancakes\"), preview: SharePreview(\"Pancakes\", image: Image(\"pancakes\")))\n```\n\nYou can share any type that conforms to `Transferable`, which `String`, `URL`, `Image` and `Data` already do.\n\n## Deep links into your app\n\nA **deep link** opens a specific screen of your app from a URL, for example from a notification, a widget, a web page or another app.\n\n1. In the target's **Info** tab, open **URL Types**, click **+**, and set **URL Schemes** to `recipebox`.\n2. Handle incoming URLs with `onOpenURL`:\n\n```swift title=\"DeepLinkApp.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct DeepLinkRoot: View {\n    @State private var path: [Int] = []\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack(path: $path) {\n            Text(\"Home\")\n                .navigationTitle(\"RecipeBox\")\n                .navigationDestination(for: Int.self) { id in\n                    Text(\"Recipe #\\(id)\").font(.largeTitle)\n                }\n        }\n        .onOpenURL { url in                             // recipebox://recipe/42\n            guard url.scheme == \"recipebox\",\n                  url.host() == \"recipe\",\n                  let id = Int(url.lastPathComponent) else { return }\n            path = [id]                                 // navigate straight to it\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { DeepLinkRoot() }\n```\n\nTest it in the Simulator from Terminal:\n\n```bash\nxcrun simctl openurl booted \"recipebox://recipe/42\"\n```\n\n(`https://` links that open your app directly are called **Universal Links**. They also require an `apple-app-site-association` file on your website and the Associated Domains capability.)\n\n## How permissions work on iOS\n\nSensitive data and hardware are protected. To access them your app must:\n\n1. **Declare a usage description** in Info.plist explaining *why*. iOS shows your text in the permission prompt.\n2. **Request access** at runtime, ideally when the user starts the feature that needs it.\n3. **Handle \"denied\"** gracefully. The user can also change their mind later in Settings.\n\n| Resource | Info.plist key | Shown as |\n|---|---|---|\n| Camera | `NSCameraUsageDescription` | Privacy – Camera Usage Description |\n| Location | `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription` | Privacy – Location When In Use Usage Description |\n| Microphone | `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` | Privacy – Microphone Usage Description |\n| Contacts | `NSContactsUsageDescription` | Privacy – Contacts Usage Description |\n| Photo library (full access) | `NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription` | Privacy – Photo Library Usage Description |\n| Face ID | `NSFaceIDUsageDescription` | Privacy – Face ID Usage Description |\n\nAdd keys in the target's **Info** tab: hover over any row, click **+**, and type \"Privacy\". Write a clear, specific reason:\n\n```plist\n<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>\n<string>RecipeBox uses the camera to scan recipe cards.</string>\n```\n\n:::danger Missing usage descriptions crash the app\nIf your app accesses a protected resource without the matching usage description, iOS **terminates it immediately**, and App Review rejects apps with vague descriptions like \"Needs camera\". Explain the benefit to the user.\n:::\n\nIf the user denies access, send them to your app's page in Settings:\n\n```swift\nButton(\"Open Settings\") {\n    openURL(URL(string: UIApplication.openSettingsURLString)!)\n}\n```\n\n## Picking photos: `PhotosPicker`\n\nThe system photo picker runs **outside your app**: the user chooses photos and your app only receives those. **No permission prompt and no usage description needed.**\n\n```swift title=\"PhotoPickerDemo.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\nimport PhotosUI\n\nstruct PhotoPickerDemo: View {\n    @State private var selection: PhotosPickerItem?\n    @State private var image: Image?\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 20) {\n            if let image {\n                image\n                    .resizable()\n                    .scaledToFill()\n                    .frame(width: 240, height: 240)\n                    .clipShape(.rect(cornerRadius: 20))\n            } else {\n                ContentUnavailableView(\"No photo\", systemImage: \"photo\")\n                    .frame(height: 240)\n            }\n\n            PhotosPicker(\"Choose a photo\", selection: $selection, matching: .images)\n                .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n        }\n        .task(id: selection) {                                      // runs when the selection changes\n            guard let selection else { return }\n            if let data = try? await selection.loadTransferable(type: Data.self),\n               let uiImage = UIImage(data: data) {\n                image = Image(uiImage: uiImage)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { PhotoPickerDemo() }\n```\n\nUse `PhotosPicker(selection: $items, maxSelectionCount: 5, matching: .images)` with an array to pick several. To save the photo with a recipe, store the `Data` (e.g. in a SwiftData property marked `@Attribute(.externalStorage)`).\n\n## Location\n\nLocation needs permission. Modern APIs make it concise:\n\n```swift title=\"LocationDemo.swift\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\nimport CoreLocation\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class LocationModel {\n    private(set) var location: CLLocation?\n    private(set) var denied = false\n    private let manager = CLLocationManager()\n\n    func start() async {\n        manager.requestWhenInUseAuthorization()            // shows the prompt (once)\n        do {\n            for try await update in CLLocationUpdate.liveUpdates() {\n                if update.authorizationDenied { denied = true; break }\n                if let loc = update.location {\n                    location = loc\n                    break                                   // we only need one fix\n                }\n            }\n        } catch {\n            denied = true\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct LocationDemo: View {\n    @State private var model = LocationModel()\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack {\n            if let loc = model.location {\n                Text(\"You're at \\(loc.coordinate.latitude, specifier: \"%.3f\"), \\(loc.coordinate.longitude, specifier: \"%.3f\")\")\n            } else if model.denied {\n                Text(\"Location access is off. Enable it in Settings to find restaurants near you.\")\n            } else {\n                Button(\"Find restaurants near me\") { Task { await model.start() } }\n            }\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}\n```\n\nRemember to add `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription`. In the Simulator, choose a location via **Features → Location**. For showing maps, SwiftUI has a `Map` view in **MapKit** with markers, annotations and more.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- `Link` and `openURL` open websites, phone, mail and maps; `ShareLink` opens the share sheet.\n- Deep links: register a URL scheme, handle it with `onOpenURL`, and drive your navigation path.\n- Protected resources need a **usage description** plus a runtime request; handle denial gracefully.\n- `PhotosPicker` needs no permission at all.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Add a **Share** button and a **Call** button to a detail screen.\n2. Register the `recipebox` URL scheme and make `recipebox://recipe/7` open the detail for recipe 7. Test it with `xcrun simctl openurl`.\n3. Add a profile avatar that opens a `PhotosPicker` and shows the chosen image in a circle.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What happens if your app accesses the camera without `NSCameraUsageDescription` in Info.plist?\n- [ ] The permission prompt shows a default message\n- [x] iOS terminates the app\n- [ ] Nothing, it works\n- [ ] The camera shows a black screen\n> Usage descriptions are mandatory for protected resources.\n\nQ: Which permission does `PhotosPicker` need?\n- [ ] Full photo library access\n- [ ] Camera access\n- [x] None, because the picker runs outside your app and only returns what the user chooses\n- [ ] Files access\n> The out-of-process picker is private by design.\n\nQ: How do you handle a URL like `recipebox://recipe/42` in SwiftUI?\n- [ ] Info.plist handles it automatically\n- [x] Register the URL scheme, then use `.onOpenURL` to parse it and navigate\n- [ ] Use a WebView\n- [ ] It's not possible\n> onOpenURL receives the URL; your code decides where to go.\n\nQ: When is the best time to request location permission?\n- [ ] At first launch, together with every other permission\n- [x] When the user starts a feature that needs it, so the reason is obvious\n- [ ] Never, just read it\n- [ ] After the app is rated\n> Contextual requests get far more \"Allow\" taps.\n```\n"},{"id":"notifications-background","title":"Notifications & Background Work","part":6,"minutes":35,"summary":"Keep users informed and data fresh. Covers local notifications (permission, scheduling, handling taps), how push notifications work, and background app refresh.","body":":::goals\n- Asking permission to send notifications\n- Scheduling local notifications with time and calendar triggers\n- Handling notification taps and showing them while the app is open\n- How remote (push) notifications work\n- Background refresh with `BGAppRefreshTask` and SwiftUI's `.backgroundTask`\n:::\n\n## Local vs. push notifications\n\n- **Local notifications** are scheduled by your app on the device: reminders, timers, \"your bread is ready\". No server needed.\n- **Push (remote) notifications** are sent from your server through Apple's Push Notification service (APNs): new messages, breaking news.\n\nBoth show up the same way to the user, and both need the user's **permission**.\n\n## Asking permission\n\n```swift title=\"NotificationManager.swift\"\nimport Foundation\nimport UserNotifications\n\nenum NotificationManager {\n    /// Returns true if notifications are allowed.\n    static func requestPermission() async -> Bool {\n        let center = UNUserNotificationCenter.current()\n        do {\n            return try await center.requestAuthorization(options: [.alert, .sound, .badge])\n        } catch {\n            return false\n        }\n    }\n\n    static func isAuthorized() async -> Bool {\n        let settings = await UNUserNotificationCenter.current().notificationSettings()\n        return settings.authorizationStatus == .authorized\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe system prompt only appears **once**. If the user declines, you can't ask again, only send them to Settings. So ask at a meaningful moment, like when they turn on \"Daily recipe reminders\", never at first launch.\n\n## Scheduling a local notification\n\nA notification has **content** (title, body, sound) and a **trigger** (when):\n\n```swift title=\"Reminders.swift\"\nimport Foundation\nimport UserNotifications\n\nenum Reminders {\n    /// Fires once, after a delay.\n    static func timer(minutes: Double, recipeName: String) async throws {\n        let content = UNMutableNotificationContent()\n        content.title = \"⏰ Time's up!\"\n        content.body = \"\\(recipeName) is ready. Take it out of the oven.\"\n        content.sound = .default\n        content.userInfo = [\"recipeID\": 42]              // data you'll read when it's tapped\n\n        let trigger = UNTimeIntervalNotificationTrigger(timeInterval: minutes * 60, repeats: false)\n        let request = UNNotificationRequest(identifier: \"timer-\\(recipeName)\", content: content, trigger: trigger)\n        try await UNUserNotificationCenter.current().add(request)\n    }\n\n    /// Fires every day at 9:00.\n    static func daily() async throws {\n        let content = UNMutableNotificationContent()\n        content.title = \"What's cooking today?\"\n        content.body = \"We picked a new recipe for you. 🍳\"\n        content.sound = .default\n\n        var time = DateComponents()\n        time.hour = 9\n        time.minute = 0\n        let trigger = UNCalendarNotificationTrigger(dateMatching: time, repeats: true)\n        let request = UNNotificationRequest(identifier: \"daily-recipe\", content: content, trigger: trigger)\n        try await UNUserNotificationCenter.current().add(request)   // same identifier replaces the old one\n    }\n\n    static func cancelDaily() {\n        UNUserNotificationCenter.current().removePendingNotificationRequests(withIdentifiers: [\"daily-recipe\"])\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHooking it up to a toggle:\n\n```swift title=\"ReminderToggle.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct ReminderToggle: View {\n    @AppStorage(\"dailyReminder\") private var enabled = false\n    @State private var showDenied = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Toggle(\"Daily recipe reminder\", isOn: $enabled)\n            .onChange(of: enabled) { _, isOn in\n                Task {\n                    if isOn {\n                        if await NotificationManager.requestPermission() {\n                            try? await Reminders.daily()\n                        } else {\n                            enabled = false\n                            showDenied = true\n                        }\n                    } else {\n                        Reminders.cancelDaily()\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            .alert(\"Notifications are off\", isPresented: $showDenied) {\n                Button(\"OK\", role: .cancel) { }\n            } message: {\n                Text(\"Turn on notifications for RecipeBox in Settings to get daily ideas.\")\n            }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { ReminderToggle().padding() }\n```\n\n:::tip Testing\nSchedule a notification a few seconds ahead, then press [[Cmd]] + [[Shift]] + [[H]] to go to the Home Screen in the Simulator. By default, notifications aren't shown while your app is in the foreground (see below).\n:::\n\n## Handling taps, and showing notifications in the foreground\n\nHandle notification events with a `UNUserNotificationCenterDelegate`, set up via an app delegate:\n\n```swift title=\"AppDelegate.swift\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\nimport UserNotifications\n\nfinal class AppDelegate: NSObject, UIApplicationDelegate, UNUserNotificationCenterDelegate {\n    func application(_ application: UIApplication,\n                     didFinishLaunchingWithOptions launchOptions: [UIApplication.LaunchOptionsKey: Any]? = nil) -> Bool {\n        UNUserNotificationCenter.current().delegate = self\n        return true\n    }\n\n    // Show banners even while the app is open\n    func userNotificationCenter(_ center: UNUserNotificationCenter,\n                                willPresent notification: UNNotification) async -> UNNotificationPresentationOptions {\n        [.banner, .sound]\n    }\n\n    // The user tapped a notification\n    func userNotificationCenter(_ center: UNUserNotificationCenter,\n                                didReceive response: UNNotificationResponse) async {\n        let info = response.notification.request.content.userInfo\n        if let id = info[\"recipeID\"] as? Int {\n            await MainActor.run {\n                // e.g. open recipebox://recipe/<id> via your deep link handling\n                print(\"Open recipe \\(id)\")\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n@main\nstruct RecipeBoxApp: App {\n    @UIApplicationDelegateAdaptor(AppDelegate.self) private var appDelegate\n    var body: some Scene { WindowGroup { ContentView() } }\n}\n```\n\n## Push notifications (overview)\n\nPush notifications require the paid Developer Program and a server:\n\n1. Add the **Push Notifications** capability in Signing & Capabilities.\n2. Call `UIApplication.shared.registerForRemoteNotifications()` after getting permission.\n3. The app delegate receives a **device token** in `application(_:didRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithDeviceToken:)`. Send it to your server.\n4. Your server sends notifications to APNs with that token (services like Firebase Cloud Messaging can do this part for you).\n\nTest pushes in the Simulator by dragging an `.apns` JSON file onto it, or with `xcrun simctl push`.\n\n## Background work\n\niOS suspends apps shortly after they leave the screen, to protect battery life. Your options for background work:\n\n| Need | Tool |\n|---|---|\n| Finish a short task when the user leaves (e.g. saving) | It usually completes; for longer work use `beginBackgroundTask` |\n| Refresh content occasionally so it's fresh when opened | **Background App Refresh** (`BGAppRefreshTask`) |\n| Long processing (e.g. database cleanup) while charging | `BGProcessingTask` |\n| Downloads/uploads that continue after the app is suspended | Background `URLSession` |\n| Music, navigation, calls | Special background modes |\n\n### Background App Refresh in SwiftUI\n\n1. In **Signing & Capabilities**, add **Background Modes** and tick **Background fetch**.\n2. In the **Info** tab, add **Permitted background task scheduler identifiers** (`BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers`) containing `com.yourname.recipebox.refresh`.\n3. Schedule a request, and handle it with the `.backgroundTask` scene modifier:\n\n```swift title=\"RefreshApp.swift\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\nimport BackgroundTasks\n\nlet refreshTaskID = \"com.yourname.recipebox.refresh\"\n\nfunc scheduleAppRefresh() {\n    let request = BGAppRefreshTaskRequest(identifier: refreshTaskID)\n    request.earliestBeginDate = .now.addingTimeInterval(4 * 60 * 60)   // no sooner than 4 hours\n    try? BGTaskScheduler.shared.submit(request)\n}\n\n@main\nstruct RecipeBoxApp: App {\n    @Environment(\\.scenePhase) private var scenePhase\n\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup { ContentView() }\n            .onChange(of: scenePhase) { _, phase in\n                if phase == .background { scheduleAppRefresh() }\n            }\n            .backgroundTask(.appRefresh(refreshTaskID)) {\n                scheduleAppRefresh()                       // schedule the next one\n                await refreshRecipesCache()                // your async work: keep it short\n            }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n:::note iOS decides when\nBackground refresh is **opportunistic**: iOS runs it based on how often the user opens your app, battery and network. It may run in hours, or not at all if the user rarely uses the app or turned Background App Refresh off. Never rely on exact timing; for time-critical alerts, use scheduled local notifications or push.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Ask for notification permission at a meaningful moment; the prompt only appears once.\n- Schedule local notifications with `UNTimeIntervalNotificationTrigger` or `UNCalendarNotificationTrigger`; reuse identifiers to replace.\n- A `UNUserNotificationCenterDelegate` handles taps and foreground presentation.\n- Push needs APNs, a device token and a server.\n- Background refresh is scheduled with `BGTaskScheduler` and handled with `.backgroundTask`, and iOS decides when it runs.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Add a **cooking timer** to a recipe screen: a `Stepper` for minutes and a \"Start timer\" button that schedules a local notification (asking permission first).\n2. Make tapping the notification open that recipe using the deep-link handling from the previous lesson.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: The user denied notification permission. Can your app show the system prompt again?\n- [ ] Yes, whenever it wants\n- [x] No. You can only direct the user to Settings\n- [ ] Yes, after reinstalling the app automatically\n- [ ] Only once a day\n> The authorization prompt appears once; after that the choice lives in Settings.\n\nQ: Which trigger fires every day at 9:00?\n- [ ] UNTimeIntervalNotificationTrigger(timeInterval: 86400, repeats: false)\n- [x] UNCalendarNotificationTrigger with hour = 9, minute = 0, repeats: true\n- [ ] A Timer in the app\n- [ ] BGAppRefreshTask\n> Calendar triggers match date components, like hour and minute.\n\nQ: By default, what happens to a notification that arrives while your app is in the foreground?\n- [ ] It's shown as usual\n- [x] It isn't shown unless your delegate's willPresent returns presentation options\n- [ ] The app crashes\n- [ ] It's queued until the app closes\n> Implement willPresent to show banners in the foreground.\n\nQ: When does a BGAppRefreshTask run?\n- [ ] Exactly at earliestBeginDate\n- [x] Opportunistically, when iOS decides, based on usage, battery and network\n- [ ] Every 15 minutes\n- [ ] Only while the app is open\n> earliestBeginDate is a minimum, not a schedule.\n```\n"},{"id":"widgets-intents","title":"Widgets, App Intents & Live Activities","part":6,"minutes":30,"summary":"Put your app on the Home Screen, Lock Screen, Siri and Spotlight. Build a WidgetKit widget with a timeline, share data with an App Group, and expose actions with App Intents.","body":":::goals\n- What widgets are and how their timelines work\n- Adding a widget extension and building a widget with SwiftUI\n- Sharing data between the app and the widget with an App Group\n- Interactive widgets and App Intents for Siri, Shortcuts and Spotlight\n- An overview of Live Activities\n:::\n\n## Widgets in one minute\n\n**Widgets** show glanceable information from your app on the Home Screen, the Lock Screen, in StandBy and in Control Centre. They're built with **SwiftUI** and **WidgetKit**, but they aren't mini-apps that run constantly: your widget provides a **timeline** of snapshots in advance, and iOS displays them at the right times.\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div>TimelineProvider<small>creates entries</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Timeline<small>entry @ 9:00, entry @ 12:00, …</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>Widget view<small>SwiftUI renders each entry</small></div>\n</div></div>\n\n## 1. Add a widget extension\n\n**File → New → Target… → Widget Extension**, name it `RecipeWidget`, untick *Include Configuration App Intent* for now, and click **Activate** when Xcode asks about the scheme. Xcode creates a new target with a template widget.\n\n## 2. The widget code\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeWidget.swift\"\nimport WidgetKit\nimport SwiftUI\n\n// 1. The data for one moment in time\nstruct RecipeEntry: TimelineEntry {\n    let date: Date\n    let recipeName: String\n    let minutes: Int\n}\n\n// 2. Provides entries to WidgetKit\nstruct RecipeProvider: TimelineProvider {\n    func placeholder(in context: Context) -> RecipeEntry {       // shown while loading (redacted)\n        RecipeEntry(date: .now, recipeName: \"Recipe of the day\", minutes: 30)\n    }\n\n    func getSnapshot(in context: Context, completion: @escaping (RecipeEntry) -> Void) {\n        completion(RecipeEntry(date: .now, recipeName: \"Margherita Pizza\", minutes: 35))   // widget gallery\n    }\n\n    func getTimeline(in context: Context, completion: @escaping (Timeline<RecipeEntry>) -> Void) {\n        let ideas = [(\"Margherita Pizza\", 35), (\"Pad Thai\", 25), (\"Pancakes\", 20)]\n        let now = Date.now\n        // One entry every 4 hours, then ask for a new timeline\n        let entries = ideas.enumerated().map { index, idea in\n            RecipeEntry(date: now.addingTimeInterval(Double(index) * 4 * 3600),\n                        recipeName: idea.0, minutes: idea.1)\n        }\n        completion(Timeline(entries: entries, policy: .atEnd))\n    }\n}\n\n// 3. The SwiftUI view\nstruct RecipeWidgetView: View {\n    let entry: RecipeEntry\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 6) {\n            Label(\"Today\", systemImage: \"fork.knife\")\n                .font(.caption.bold())\n                .foregroundStyle(.orange)\n            Text(entry.recipeName)\n                .font(.headline)\n                .lineLimit(2)\n            Spacer()\n            Label(\"\\(entry.minutes) min\", systemImage: \"clock\")\n                .font(.caption)\n                .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n        }\n        .containerBackground(.fill.tertiary, for: .widget)\n    }\n}\n\n// 4. The widget's configuration\nstruct RecipeWidget: Widget {\n    var body: some WidgetConfiguration {\n        StaticConfiguration(kind: \"RecipeWidget\", provider: RecipeProvider()) { entry in\n            RecipeWidgetView(entry: entry)\n        }\n        .configurationDisplayName(\"Recipe of the Day\")\n        .description(\"A new cooking idea a few times a day.\")\n        .supportedFamilies([.systemSmall, .systemMedium, .accessoryRectangular])\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe template also includes a `@main` **WidgetBundle** listing your widgets. Run the **RecipeWidget** scheme to see it in the Simulator, or use the canvas: widgets support `#Preview(as: .systemSmall) { RecipeWidget() } timeline: { … }`.\n\n:::note Widgets are snapshots\nWidget views can't have timers, scroll views or live network calls. Keep timelines reasonable: iOS gives each widget a daily refresh budget. When your app's data changes, call `WidgetCenter.shared.reloadAllTimelines()` to ask for an update.\n:::\n\n## 3. Share data with an App Group\n\nThe app and the widget are separate processes with separate sandboxes. To share data (say, the user's favourite recipes):\n\n1. Add the **App Groups** capability to **both** targets with the same group, e.g. `group.com.yourname.recipebox`.\n2. Read and write shared storage:\n\n```swift\nlet shared = UserDefaults(suiteName: \"group.com.yourname.recipebox\")\nshared?.set(\"Pad Thai\", forKey: \"lastViewedRecipe\")\n\nlet folder = FileManager.default.containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier: \"group.com.yourname.recipebox\")\n```\n\nSwiftData can also use the shared container, so the widget can query the same store.\n\n## 4. Tapping a widget\n\nTapping a widget opens your app. Use `.widgetURL(URL(string: \"recipebox://recipe/42\"))` on the widget view, and your `onOpenURL` handler (Lesson 27) navigates to the right screen. Medium and large widgets can have several `Link`s to different destinations.\n\n## App Intents: Siri, Shortcuts, Spotlight & interactive widgets\n\nAn **App Intent** exposes an action from your app to the system: Siri, the Shortcuts app, Spotlight, the Action button, and **buttons inside widgets**.\n\n```swift title=\"StartTimerIntent.swift\" nocheck\nimport AppIntents\n\nstruct StartTimerIntent: AppIntent {\n    static let title: LocalizedStringResource = \"Start Cooking Timer\"\n    static let description = IntentDescription(\"Starts a cooking timer in RecipeBox.\")\n\n    @Parameter(title: \"Minutes\", default: 10)\n    var minutes: Int\n\n    func perform() async throws -> some IntentResult & ProvidesDialog {\n        try await Reminders.timer(minutes: Double(minutes), recipeName: \"Your dish\")\n        return .result(dialog: \"Timer set for \\(minutes) minutes.\")\n    }\n}\n\nstruct RecipeShortcuts: AppShortcutsProvider {\n    static var appShortcuts: [AppShortcut] {\n        AppShortcut(intent: StartTimerIntent(),\n                    phrases: [\"Start a cooking timer in \\(.applicationName)\"],\n                    shortTitle: \"Cooking Timer\",\n                    systemImageName: \"timer\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWith `AppShortcutsProvider`, users can say *\"Start a cooking timer in RecipeBox\"* to Siri with **zero setup**, and the action also appears in Spotlight and the Shortcuts app. In a widget, `Button(intent: StartTimerIntent()) { … }` makes the widget interactive. App Intents are also how your app's actions become available to Apple Intelligence.\n\n## Live Activities (overview)\n\n**Live Activities** show real-time, ongoing information on the Lock Screen and in the **Dynamic Island**: a delivery on its way, a sports score, a cooking timer counting down. They're built with **ActivityKit** plus a widget extension:\n\n1. Define `ActivityAttributes` with the static data and a `ContentState` for what changes.\n2. Build the Lock Screen and Dynamic Island layouts in your widget extension with `ActivityConfiguration`.\n3. Start it from the app with `Activity.request(...)`, update it with `activity.update(...)`, and end it with `activity.end(...)`. Updates can also arrive by push.\n\nA countdown timer is a perfect first Live Activity, since `Text(timerInterval:)` counts down by itself without any updates.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Widgets are SwiftUI snapshots from a `TimelineProvider`, added as a Widget Extension target.\n- Share data with an **App Group**; reload timelines when data changes.\n- `widgetURL` / `Link` deep-link into your app.\n- **App Intents** expose actions to Siri, Shortcuts, Spotlight and interactive widgets.\n- Live Activities show ongoing events on the Lock Screen and Dynamic Island.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Add the Recipe of the Day widget to one of your projects, and make it deep-link into the app.\n2. Add an App Group and make the widget show the name of the last recipe the user viewed in the app. Call `WidgetCenter.shared.reloadAllTimelines()` when it changes.\n3. Create an App Intent \"Show a random recipe\" and try it from the Shortcuts app.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: How does a widget get its content?\n- [ ] It runs continuously like a small app\n- [x] A TimelineProvider supplies a timeline of entries that iOS renders at the right times\n- [ ] It streams video of the app\n- [ ] It downloads HTML\n> Widgets are snapshots rendered from timeline entries.\n\nQ: How can the app and its widget share data?\n- [ ] They share the same sandbox automatically\n- [x] Through an App Group container (shared UserDefaults, files or SwiftData store)\n- [ ] Through the clipboard\n- [ ] They can't\n> App Groups give both targets a shared container.\n\nQ: What do App Intents let you do?\n- [ ] Make network requests faster\n- [x] Expose your app's actions to Siri, Shortcuts, Spotlight and interactive widgets\n- [ ] Replace SwiftUI views\n- [ ] Send push notifications\n> Intents describe actions the system can run on your app's behalf.\n\nQ: After the user changes their favourites in the app, how do you update the widget?\n- [ ] Wait for the next day\n- [x] Call `WidgetCenter.shared.reloadAllTimelines()`\n- [ ] Restart the phone\n- [ ] Widgets update themselves every second\n> Reloading asks WidgetKit for a fresh timeline.\n```\n"},{"id":"uikit-interop","title":"UIKit & Interop","part":6,"minutes":30,"summary":"Many apps and libraries still use UIKit. Learn enough to read UIKit code, wrap UIKit views and controllers for SwiftUI, and host SwiftUI inside UIKit apps.","body":":::goals\n- What UIKit is and how it differs from SwiftUI\n- View controllers, views and Auto Layout in brief\n- `UIViewRepresentable` to wrap a UIKit view for SwiftUI\n- `UIViewControllerRepresentable` with a `Coordinator` for delegates\n- `UIHostingController` to put SwiftUI inside UIKit\n:::\n\n## Why learn UIKit at all?\n\nSwiftUI is the future, and everything new in this course uses it. But **UIKit** has powered iOS apps since 2008, and:\n\n- Most established apps are large UIKit codebases migrating gradually, so you'll meet UIKit at work.\n- A few features have richer UIKit APIs, and some third-party SDKs only provide UIKit views.\n- You can mix both freely, screen by screen or view by view.\n\n## UIKit in a nutshell\n\nUIKit is **imperative** and **class-based**:\n\n- A **`UIViewController`** manages one screen: its views, lifecycle (`viewDidLoad`, `viewWillAppear`…) and user interactions.\n- **`UIView`** subclasses (`UILabel`, `UIButton`, `UIImageView`, `UITableView`…) draw things on screen.\n- **Auto Layout** positions views with constraints.\n- **Delegates** and **target–action** deliver events.\n\n```swift title=\"CounterViewController.swift (UIKit)\" nocheck\nimport UIKit\n\nfinal class CounterViewController: UIViewController {\n    private var count = 0\n    private let label = UILabel()\n    private let button = UIButton(configuration: .filled())\n\n    override func viewDidLoad() {\n        super.viewDidLoad()\n        view.backgroundColor = .systemBackground\n\n        label.font = .preferredFont(forTextStyle: .title1)\n        label.text = \"Tapped 0 times\"\n        button.configuration?.title = \"Tap me\"\n        button.addAction(UIAction { [weak self] _ in self?.tapped() }, for: .touchUpInside)\n\n        let stack = UIStackView(arrangedSubviews: [label, button])\n        stack.axis = .vertical\n        stack.spacing = 16\n        stack.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false\n        view.addSubview(stack)\n        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([\n            stack.centerXAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.centerXAnchor),\n            stack.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.centerYAnchor)\n        ])\n    }\n\n    private func tapped() {\n        count += 1\n        label.text = \"Tapped \\(count) times\"      // YOU update the label manually\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCompare this with the SwiftUI counter from Lesson 12: here you create views, lay them out with constraints, and update the label by hand whenever the count changes.\n\n| UIKit | SwiftUI equivalent |\n|---|---|\n| `UIViewController` | A `View` (screen) |\n| `UILabel`, `UIButton`, `UIImageView` | `Text`, `Button`, `Image` |\n| `UIStackView` + Auto Layout | `VStack` / `HStack` + modifiers |\n| `UITableView` / `UICollectionView` | `List` / `LazyVGrid` |\n| `UINavigationController` | `NavigationStack` |\n| `UITabBarController` | `TabView` |\n| Delegates / target–action | Closures and bindings |\n| Storyboards / XIBs | Code + previews |\n\n:::note `[weak self]`\nUIKit code uses closures that capture `self` a lot. `[weak self]` prevents a **retain cycle** (two objects keeping each other alive forever, leaking memory). You'll see it constantly in UIKit and in code with long-lived closures.\n:::\n\n## UIKit inside SwiftUI: `UIViewRepresentable`\n\nTo use a UIKit view in SwiftUI, wrap it in a struct conforming to `UIViewRepresentable`:\n\n```swift title=\"ActivitySpinner.swift\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\nimport UIKit\n\nstruct ActivitySpinner: UIViewRepresentable {\n    var isAnimating: Bool\n    var color: UIColor = .systemOrange\n\n    func makeUIView(context: Context) -> UIActivityIndicatorView {    // create once\n        let view = UIActivityIndicatorView(style: .large)\n        view.color = color\n        return view\n    }\n\n    func updateUIView(_ view: UIActivityIndicatorView, context: Context) {   // on every SwiftUI update\n        isAnimating ? view.startAnimating() : view.stopAnimating()\n    }\n}\n\nstruct SpinnerDemo: View {\n    @State private var loading = true\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack {\n            ActivitySpinner(isAnimating: loading)\n            Toggle(\"Loading\", isOn: $loading).padding()\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `makeUIView` creates the UIKit view **once**.\n- `updateUIView` runs whenever the SwiftUI state it depends on changes. Push new values into the view here.\n\n## Wrapping a view controller with a Coordinator\n\nMany UIKit components report events through **delegates**. A **Coordinator** is an object you provide to act as the delegate and pass results back to SwiftUI. Here's the system camera picker, useful for taking a new photo:\n\n```swift title=\"CameraPicker.swift\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\nimport UIKit\n\nstruct CameraPicker: UIViewControllerRepresentable {\n    @Binding var image: UIImage?\n    @Environment(\\.dismiss) private var dismiss\n\n    func makeUIViewController(context: Context) -> UIImagePickerController {\n        let picker = UIImagePickerController()\n        picker.sourceType = .camera\n        picker.delegate = context.coordinator          // the coordinator receives delegate calls\n        return picker\n    }\n\n    func updateUIViewController(_ controller: UIImagePickerController, context: Context) {}\n\n    func makeCoordinator() -> Coordinator { Coordinator(parent: self) }\n\n    final class Coordinator: NSObject, UIImagePickerControllerDelegate, UINavigationControllerDelegate {\n        let parent: CameraPicker\n        init(parent: CameraPicker) { self.parent = parent }\n\n        func imagePickerController(_ picker: UIImagePickerController,\n                                   didFinishPickingMediaWithInfo info: [UIImagePickerController.InfoKey: Any]) {\n            parent.image = info[.originalImage] as? UIImage       // send the result back to SwiftUI\n            parent.dismiss()\n        }\n\n        func imagePickerControllerDidCancel(_ picker: UIImagePickerController) {\n            parent.dismiss()\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage (remember NSCameraUsageDescription, and use a real device, since the Simulator has no camera):\n// .fullScreenCover(isPresented: $showCamera) { CameraPicker(image: $photo).ignoresSafeArea() }\n```\n\n## SwiftUI inside UIKit: `UIHostingController`\n\nGoing the other way is one line. That's how teams add new SwiftUI screens to existing UIKit apps:\n\n```swift nocheck\nlet settings = UIHostingController(rootView: SettingsView())\nnavigationController?.pushViewController(settings, animated: true)\n```\n\nYou can even use SwiftUI for individual table or collection view cells with `UIHostingConfiguration`.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- UIKit is imperative and class-based: view controllers, views, Auto Layout, delegates.\n- `UIViewRepresentable` / `UIViewControllerRepresentable` wrap UIKit for SwiftUI: create in `make…`, update in `update…`.\n- A **Coordinator** handles delegate callbacks and passes results back through bindings.\n- `UIHostingController` puts SwiftUI into UIKit apps.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Wrap `UITextView` in a `UIViewRepresentable` with `@Binding var text: String`. Use a Coordinator as the `UITextViewDelegate` to write changes back to the binding in `textViewDidChange`.\n2. Use your wrapper in a SwiftUI form and show the character count below it.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: How do you use a UIKit view inside SwiftUI?\n- [ ] Add it directly to a VStack\n- [x] Wrap it in a type conforming to UIViewRepresentable\n- [ ] Use UIHostingController\n- [ ] It's not possible\n> Representables bridge UIKit into SwiftUI.\n\nQ: What's the role of a Coordinator in a representable?\n- [ ] It draws the view\n- [x] It acts as the delegate/target for UIKit events and passes results back to SwiftUI\n- [ ] It replaces the view controller\n- [ ] It manages navigation\n> Coordinators connect UIKit's delegate pattern to SwiftUI's data flow.\n\nQ: How do you show a SwiftUI view inside an existing UIKit app?\n- [x] Wrap it in UIHostingController\n- [ ] Wrap it in UIViewRepresentable\n- [ ] Convert it to a storyboard\n- [ ] You must rewrite the app\n> UIHostingController is a UIViewController that hosts SwiftUI content.\n\nQ: Why write `[weak self]` in UIKit closures?\n- [ ] To make them faster\n- [x] To avoid retain cycles that leak memory\n- [ ] It's required syntax\n- [ ] To run them on the main thread\n> A strong capture of self in a closure that self owns keeps both alive forever.\n```\n"},{"id":"testing","title":"Testing with Swift Testing & XCTest","part":7,"minutes":40,"summary":"Ship with confidence. Write fast unit tests with Swift Testing, test async view models with mocks, use parameterized tests, and automate the UI with XCTest.","body":":::goals\n- Why test, and what to test first\n- Swift Testing: `@Test`, `#expect`, `#require`, `@Suite`\n- Parameterized tests\n- Testing async view models with mock services\n- UI tests with XCUITest\n- Running tests and reading results\n:::\n\n## Why test?\n\nEvery change you make might break something that used to work. Automated tests check it for you in seconds, so you can refactor fearlessly and catch bugs before your users do.\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"stack\">\n<div>UI tests<small>few · slow · drive the real app in the Simulator</small></div>\n<div>Integration tests<small>some · several real parts together</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Unit tests<small>many · fast · one type in isolation</small></div>\n</div></div>\n\nYour project already has two test targets: **`HelloiOSTests`** for unit tests (Swift Testing) and **`HelloiOSUITests`** for UI tests (XCTest).\n\n## Swift Testing basics\n\n**Swift Testing** is Apple's modern testing framework. Say you have this function in your app:\n\n```swift title=\"Formatting.swift\"\nfunc formatMinutes(_ total: Int) -> String {\n    precondition(total >= 0, \"Minutes can't be negative\")\n    let h = total / 60, m = total % 60\n    switch (h, m) {\n    case (0, _): return \"\\(m) min\"\n    case (_, 0): return \"\\(h) h\"\n    default: return \"\\(h) h \\(m) min\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\nTests are functions marked **`@Test`**, with **`#expect`** for checks:\n\n```swift title=\"FormattingTests.swift\" nocheck\nimport Testing\n@testable import RecipeBox          // gives tests access to your app's internal types\n\nstruct FormattingTests {\n    @Test func minutesUnderAnHour() {\n        #expect(formatMinutes(45) == \"45 min\")\n    }\n\n    @Test func exactHours() {\n        #expect(formatMinutes(120) == \"2 h\")\n    }\n\n    @Test(\"Mixed hours and minutes\")              // a readable display name\n    func mixed() {\n        #expect(formatMinutes(75) == \"1 h 15 min\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Put tests in any type (usually a struct), one test per behaviour.\n- `#expect(condition)` records a failure with a helpful message showing the actual values, and the test continues.\n- **`try #require(...)`** stops the test if it fails. Use it to unwrap optionals: `let user = try #require(users.first)`.\n- Run tests with **[[Cmd]] + [[U]]**, or click the ◇ diamond next to a single test.\n\n:::tip Arrange, Act, Assert\nStructure each test in three parts: **arrange** the inputs, **act** by calling the code, **assert** with `#expect`. One behaviour per test, with a name that says what should happen.\n:::\n\n## Parameterized tests\n\nRun the same test with many inputs, and each one is reported separately:\n\n```swift nocheck\n@Test(\"Formats minutes\", arguments: [\n    (5, \"5 min\"),\n    (60, \"1 h\"),\n    (61, \"1 h 1 min\"),\n    (135, \"2 h 15 min\")\n])\nfunc formats(input: Int, expected: String) {\n    #expect(formatMinutes(input) == expected)\n}\n```\n\n## Testing a view model with a mock\n\nThis is where the architecture from Lesson 22 pays off. The view model depends on a **protocol**, so tests can inject a mock with controlled behaviour, with no network and no flakiness:\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeListViewModel.swift (app)\" nocheck\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class RecipeListViewModel {\n    private let service: any RecipeService\n    private(set) var recipes: [Recipe] = []\n    private(set) var errorMessage: String?\n    var searchText = \"\"\n\n    var visibleRecipes: [Recipe] {\n        searchText.isEmpty ? recipes : recipes.filter { $0.name.localizedCaseInsensitiveContains(searchText) }\n    }\n\n    init(service: any RecipeService) { self.service = service }\n\n    func load() async {\n        do {\n            recipes = try await service.fetchRecipes()\n            errorMessage = nil\n        } catch {\n            errorMessage = \"Couldn't load recipes.\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeListViewModelTests.swift\" nocheck\nimport Testing\nimport Foundation\n@testable import RecipeBox\n\nstruct MockRecipeService: RecipeService {\n    var result: Result<[Recipe], Error>\n    func fetchRecipes() async throws -> [Recipe] { try result.get() }\n}\n\n@MainActor\n@Suite(\"Recipe list view model\")\nstruct RecipeListViewModelTests {\n    let sample = [\n        Recipe(id: 1, name: \"Margherita Pizza\", cuisine: \"Italian\"),\n        Recipe(id: 2, name: \"Pad Thai\", cuisine: \"Thai\")\n    ]\n\n    @Test func loadsRecipes() async {\n        let vm = RecipeListViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(result: .success(sample)))\n\n        await vm.load()\n\n        #expect(vm.recipes == sample)\n        #expect(vm.errorMessage == nil)\n    }\n\n    @Test func showsErrorWhenLoadingFails() async {\n        let vm = RecipeListViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(result: .failure(URLError(.notConnectedToInternet))))\n\n        await vm.load()\n\n        #expect(vm.recipes.isEmpty)\n        #expect(vm.errorMessage != nil)\n    }\n\n    @Test func searchFiltersByName() async {\n        let vm = RecipeListViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(result: .success(sample)))\n        await vm.load()\n\n        vm.searchText = \"thai\"\n\n        #expect(vm.visibleRecipes.map(\\.name) == [\"Pad Thai\"])\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Test functions can be `async` and `throws`. Just `await` the code under test.\n- `@MainActor` on the suite lets tests call main-actor view models directly.\n- `@Suite` groups related tests and gives them a name in the test navigator.\n\n## Testing Codable decoding\n\nDecoding bugs are common and easy to test. Include a sample JSON string:\n\n```swift nocheck\n@Test func decodesRecipeJSON() throws {\n    let json = #\"{\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"Tacos\", \"cuisine\": \"Mexican\", \"extra\": \"ignored\"}\"#\n    let recipe = try JSONDecoder().decode(Recipe.self, from: Data(json.utf8))\n    #expect(recipe.name == \"Tacos\")\n    #expect(recipe.cuisine == \"Mexican\")\n}\n```\n\n## UI tests with XCTest\n\nUI tests launch your **real app** in the Simulator and interact with it like a user, finding elements by their accessibility labels:\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeBoxUITests.swift\" nocheck\nimport XCTest\n\nfinal class RecipeBoxUITests: XCTestCase {\n    @MainActor\n    func testAddingANote() throws {\n        let app = XCUIApplication()\n        app.launchArguments = [\"-ui-testing\"]          // let the app use mock data\n        app.launch()\n\n        let field = app.textFields[\"New note\"]\n        XCTAssertTrue(field.waitForExistence(timeout: 5))\n        field.tap()\n        field.typeText(\"Buy basil\")\n        app.buttons[\"Add\"].tap()\n\n        XCTAssertTrue(app.staticTexts[\"Buy basil\"].exists)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn the app, check `ProcessInfo.processInfo.arguments.contains(\"-ui-testing\")` to use mock services and in-memory storage, so UI tests are fast and repeatable. Add `.accessibilityIdentifier(\"recipeGrid\")` to views that have no text to find them.\n\n:::tip Record UI tests\nPut the cursor inside a UI test function and press the red **Record** button at the bottom of the editor. Xcode writes code as you tap around the app. It's great for learning the API.\n:::\n\n## Running tests\n\n- [[Cmd]] + [[U]] runs all tests; the **Test navigator** ([[Cmd]] + [[6]]) shows results.\n- Click the diamond next to a test or suite to run just that one.\n- Enable **code coverage** in the scheme's Test settings to see which lines your tests exercise.\n- From the terminal: `xcodebuild test -scheme RecipeBox -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=iPhone 17'`.\n\n## What to test first\n\n1. **View models**: state after loading, errors, user actions. This gives the most value.\n2. **Decoding and formatting**: JSON parsing, dates, prices.\n3. **Services with logic**: caching rules, validation.\n4. **A few UI tests** for the most important user journeys.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Swift Testing: `@Test` functions, `#expect` checks, `try #require` to unwrap, `@Suite` to group, `arguments:` for parameterized tests.\n- Depend on protocols so tests can inject mocks; `await` async code directly in tests.\n- XCUITest drives the real app via accessibility; use launch arguments for mock data.\n- [[Cmd]] + [[U]] runs everything.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Write parameterized tests for a `TipCalculator` model (Lesson 21's `TipViewModel`): 15% of 100 is 15; rounding up 12.3 gives 13; 0 amount gives 0 total.\n2. Write a test that the `OfflineQuotesViewModel` from Lesson 26 keeps its cached quotes and sets an error message when refresh fails. (Hint: a mock `QuoteAPI` that throws, and a `DiskCache` with a unique name per test.)\n:::\n\n:::solution Show solution for task 1\n```swift nocheck\nimport Testing\n@testable import TipApp\n\nstruct TipViewModelTests {\n    @Test(arguments: [(100.0, 15, false, 15.0), (82.0, 15, true, 13.0), (0.0, 20, false, 0.0)])\n    func tip(amount: Double, percent: Int, roundUp: Bool, expectedTip: Double) {\n        let vm = TipViewModel()\n        vm.amount = amount\n        vm.percent = percent\n        vm.roundUp = roundUp\n        #expect(vm.tip == expectedTip)\n        #expect(vm.total == amount + expectedTip)\n    }\n}\n```\n(82 × 15% = 12.3, which rounds up to 13.)\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What's the difference between `#expect` and `#require`?\n- [ ] They're identical\n- [x] #expect records a failure and continues; #require throws and stops the test\n- [ ] #require is for UI tests only\n- [ ] #expect only works with Booleans\n> Use #require for preconditions like unwrapping an optional.\n\nQ: Why does a mock service make view model tests better?\n- [ ] It makes the app faster\n- [x] Tests become fast, offline and deterministic, and can simulate errors on demand\n- [ ] It's required by Swift Testing\n- [ ] It tests the real server\n> You control exactly what the dependency returns.\n\nQ: How do UI tests find elements on screen?\n- [ ] By pixel coordinates\n- [x] Through accessibility: labels, identifiers and element types\n- [ ] By Swift variable names\n- [ ] By reading the source code\n> XCUITest uses the same accessibility tree VoiceOver uses.\n\nQ: What does `@testable import RecipeBox` do?\n- [ ] Imports a testing library\n- [x] Lets the test target access the app's internal types and functions\n- [ ] Runs the app\n- [ ] Marks the module as testable in the App Store\n> Without it, tests could only see public declarations.\n```\n"},{"id":"debugging","title":"Debugging & Instruments","part":7,"minutes":30,"summary":"Find and fix bugs like a professional. Covers reading crash messages, logging with Logger, breakpoints and LLDB, the view and memory graph debuggers, and profiling with Instruments.","body":":::goals\n- Reading crashes and runtime errors\n- `print` vs. `Logger` for logging\n- Breakpoints, stepping and LLDB commands\n- The view hierarchy debugger and memory graph (retain cycles)\n- Profiling with Instruments: Time Profiler, SwiftUI, Leaks\n- Common crashes and their fixes\n:::\n\n## When the app crashes\n\nWhen a crash happens while running from Xcode, the debugger **pauses at the line that crashed** and the console shows a message. The most common ones:\n\n```text\nFatal error: Unexpectedly found nil while unwrapping an Optional value\nFatal error: Index out of range\nThread 1: EXC_BAD_ACCESS\n```\n\n1. Read the **message** in the console (and the red banner in the editor).\n2. Look at the **highlighted line** and the **call stack** in the Debug navigator ([[Cmd]] + [[7]]) to see how execution got there. Frames in *your* code are shown in bold.\n3. Inspect variables in the **Variables view** at the bottom-left.\n\n:::tip Search smartly\nCopy the error message (without your own variable names) into a search engine or the Apple Developer Forums. Someone has almost always hit it before.\n:::\n\n## Logging: `print` and `Logger`\n\n`print` is fine for quick experiments. For real apps, use **`Logger`** from the unified logging system. It's fast, has levels, can be filtered in Xcode's console, and hides private data in production logs:\n\n```swift title=\"Log.swift\"\nimport OSLog\n\nextension Logger {\n    static let network = Logger(subsystem: \"com.yourname.RecipeBox\", category: \"network\")\n    static let storage = Logger(subsystem: \"com.yourname.RecipeBox\", category: \"storage\")\n}\n\nfunc example(count: Int, email: String) {\n    Logger.network.debug(\"Fetching recipes…\")\n    Logger.network.info(\"Loaded \\(count) recipes\")\n    Logger.network.error(\"Request failed for \\(email, privacy: .private)\")   // redacted in production logs\n}\n```\n\nIn Xcode's console you can filter by **category**, **level** or text, and click a log line to jump to the code that produced it.\n\n## Breakpoints\n\n1. Click the **gutter** (line numbers) to add a breakpoint, shown as a blue arrow.\n2. Run the app. When that line is about to execute, the app **pauses**.\n3. Inspect variables, hover over them in the editor, then use the debug bar:\n\n| Action | Shortcut |\n|---|---|\n| Continue | [[Ctrl]] + [[Cmd]] + [[Y]] |\n| Step over (next line) | [[F6]] |\n| Step into (enter the function) | [[F7]] |\n| Step out (finish the function) | [[F8]] |\n| Enable/disable all breakpoints | [[Cmd]] + [[Y]] |\n\nRight-click a breakpoint → **Edit Breakpoint** to add a **condition** (`recipe.id == 42`), or an **action** that logs a message and automatically continues. That's logging without changing code. In the Breakpoint navigator ([[Cmd]] + [[8]]), click **+ → Swift Error Breakpoint** to pause whenever any error is thrown.\n\n### LLDB in the console\n\nWhile paused, the console accepts debugger commands:\n\n```text\n(lldb) po recipe                       // print an object's description\n(lldb) p recipes.count                 // evaluate an expression\n(lldb) po viewModel.state\n(lldb) expr isLoading = false          // change a value while paused\n(lldb) bt                              // backtrace: the call stack\n```\n\n## Debugging SwiftUI\n\n- `let _ = Self._printChanges()` inside `body` prints **why** a view re-rendered (debug builds only). It's great for spotting unnecessary updates.\n- In the canvas, right-click a preview and choose **Debug Preview** to hit breakpoints in previews.\n- **Debug View Hierarchy** (the stacked-rectangles button in the debug bar) shows an exploded 3D view of your UI, with each view's frame and properties. Use it for \"why is this invisible / clipped / in the wrong place?\"\n\n## Memory: the memory graph and retain cycles\n\nSwift uses **Automatic Reference Counting (ARC)**: a class instance is freed when nothing references it any more. A **retain cycle** (A holds B, B holds A) means neither is ever freed, which is a memory leak.\n\n```swift nocheck\nfinal class TimerModel {\n    var timer: Timer?\n    var ticks = 0\n\n    func start() {\n        // ❌ The timer's closure captures self strongly, and self holds the timer → leak\n        timer = Timer.scheduledTimer(withTimeInterval: 1, repeats: true) { _ in\n            self.ticks += 1\n        }\n        // ✅ Capture self weakly instead\n        timer = Timer.scheduledTimer(withTimeInterval: 1, repeats: true) { [weak self] _ in\n            self?.ticks += 1\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nTo hunt leaks, click **Debug Memory Graph** in the debug bar (three connected circles). It lists every live object; purple ⚠️ icons flag likely leaks, and you can see who is holding each object.\n\n## Instruments: profiling performance\n\n**Product → Profile** ([[Cmd]] + [[I]]) builds a release-optimised app and opens **Instruments**. Useful templates:\n\n- **Time Profiler**: where the CPU spends time. Find the slow function that makes scrolling janky.\n- **SwiftUI**: which views update, how often, and how long `body` takes.\n- **Leaks / Allocations**: memory leaks and growth.\n- **Network**: requests and their timing.\n- **Hangs**: when the main thread was blocked.\n\n:::warning Measure release builds on a real device\nDebug builds are unoptimised, and the Simulator uses your Mac's powerful CPU. Performance conclusions should come from **Profile** builds on a real (ideally older) iPhone.\n:::\n\n## Common crashes and fixes\n\n| Crash | Usual cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| *Unexpectedly found nil while unwrapping* | `!` on a nil optional | Use `if let` / `guard let` / `??` |\n| *Index out of range* | `array[i]` with a bad index | Check `indices.contains(i)`, use `first`/`last` |\n| *This application has crashed because it attempted to access privacy-sensitive data* | Missing usage description | Add the `NS…UsageDescription` key |\n| *No Observable object of type … found* | Missing `.environment(...)` | Provide the object higher up (and in previews) |\n| *Publishing changes from background threads* / main actor warnings | Updating UI state off the main actor | Make the view model `@MainActor` |\n| `DecodingError.keyNotFound` | JSON doesn't match your model | Make the property optional or fix `CodingKeys` |\n| Hang / frozen UI | Heavy work on the main thread | Move it into async code / a background actor |\n\n## A debugging mindset\n\n1. **Reproduce** the bug reliably. Write down the steps.\n2. **Read** the error message, all of it.\n3. **Form a hypothesis** (\"the list is empty because the search filter is wrong\").\n4. **Test it** with a breakpoint, `po`, a log, or the view debugger.\n5. **Fix it**, then add a **test** so it never returns.\n\n:::exercise\n1. Put a breakpoint inside a view model's `load()` method, trigger it, and use `po` to inspect its state. Add a condition so it only stops when there's an error.\n2. Deliberately crash with `let x: Int? = nil; print(x!)` in a button action, read the crash, then fix it properly.\n3. Open **Debug View Hierarchy** on one of your screens and find a view's frame.\n4. Add `let _ = Self._printChanges()` to a view's body and watch the console as you interact.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What's the advantage of Logger over print?\n- [ ] It prints in colour\n- [x] Levels, categories, filtering in the console, low overhead, and privacy redaction in production\n- [ ] It sends logs to a server\n- [ ] print doesn't work in iOS apps\n> Logger is the unified logging system designed for real apps.\n\nQ: What does `po viewModel.state` do in the debugger console?\n- [ ] Changes the state\n- [x] Prints a description of the value while the app is paused\n- [ ] Restarts the app\n- [ ] Posts a notification\n> po (print object) evaluates and describes an expression.\n\nQ: A closure stored by an object references `self` strongly. What problem can this cause?\n- [ ] A crash on launch\n- [x] A retain cycle, where the object is never freed (a memory leak)\n- [ ] Slower compilation\n- [ ] Nothing\n> Use [weak self] to break the cycle.\n\nQ: Why should performance be measured with Product → Profile on a real device?\n- [ ] Instruments only works on devices\n- [x] Debug builds and the Simulator don't reflect real-world performance\n- [ ] It's required for the App Store\n- [ ] Profile builds include more logs\n> Optimised builds on real hardware show what users actually experience.\n```\n"},{"id":"app-store","title":"Shipping to TestFlight & the App Store","part":7,"minutes":35,"summary":"Take your app from Xcode to users' iPhones. Covers the Developer Program, signing, versioning, archiving and uploading, TestFlight, App Store Connect, privacy requirements and App Review.","body":":::goals\n- A pre-release checklist\n- The Apple Developer Program, bundle IDs, certificates and automatic signing\n- Version and build numbers\n- Archiving and uploading with Xcode\n- TestFlight beta testing\n- The App Store listing: screenshots, privacy details, age rating\n- App Review and releasing\n:::\n\n## Pre-release checklist\n\n- [ ] **Bundle identifier** is final (e.g. `com.yourname.recipebox`). It can't change after release.\n- [ ] **App icon** (1024×1024, with dark and tinted variants) and **display name** are polished.\n- [ ] Works on the **smallest and largest** iPhones, in **landscape** if supported, and on **iPad** if you ship there.\n- [ ] Handles **no internet**, **dark mode**, the **largest Dynamic Type sizes**, **VoiceOver** and being terminated in the background.\n- [ ] Every permission has a clear **usage description**.\n- [ ] No debug-only code: test buttons, verbose logging, hard-coded test accounts.\n- [ ] A **privacy policy** URL (required for every app).\n- [ ] Tested a **Release** build on a real device (e.g. via TestFlight).\n\n## The Apple Developer Program\n\nTo distribute on the App Store or TestFlight you need a paid **[Apple Developer Program](https://developer.apple.com/programs/)** membership (renewed yearly). Individuals enrol with their Apple Account; organisations also need a D-U-N-S number. Enrolment can take a day or two.\n\n## Signing, simply\n\niOS only runs apps **signed** by a certificate Apple issued. Signing involves:\n\n- a **certificate** (proves who you are),\n- an **App ID** (your bundle identifier, registered with Apple),\n- a **provisioning profile** (ties the certificate, App ID, capabilities and devices together).\n\nWith **Automatically manage signing** ticked in *Signing & Capabilities* and your paid team selected, **Xcode creates and renews all of this for you**. You'll rarely need to touch the developer portal manually.\n\n## Version and build numbers\n\nIn the target's **General** tab:\n\n- **Version** (`CFBundleShortVersionString`), e.g. `1.0.0`: what users see on the App Store.\n- **Build** (`CFBundleVersion`), e.g. `1`, `2`, `3`: must be **unique and increasing** for each upload of that version.\n\nTypical flow: version `1.0.0` builds 1, 2, 3 go to TestFlight; build 3 is released. Then version `1.0.1` starts again at build 1 (or keep incrementing, either works).\n\n## Create the app in App Store Connect\n\n1. Go to **[App Store Connect](https://appstoreconnect.apple.com)** → **Apps** → **+** → **New App**.\n2. Choose iOS, enter the **name** (must be unique on the App Store), primary language, your **bundle ID**, and an SKU (any internal reference, e.g. `recipebox-001`).\n\n## Archive and upload\n\n1. In Xcode, set the run destination to **Any iOS Device (arm64)**.\n2. **Product → Archive**. Xcode builds an optimised release version.\n3. The **Organizer** window opens. Select the archive and click **Distribute App** → **App Store Connect** → **Upload**.\n4. Xcode validates, signs and uploads. After a few minutes of processing, the build appears in App Store Connect under **TestFlight**.\n\n:::note Xcode Cloud\nXcode Cloud (in the **Integrate** menu / Report navigator) can build, test and upload automatically whenever you push to Git. It's worth setting up once you're shipping regularly.\n:::\n\n## TestFlight\n\n**TestFlight** lets people install beta builds through the TestFlight app:\n\n| Group | Who | Review needed |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Internal testers** | Up to 100 members of your App Store Connect team | No: available minutes after processing |\n| **External testers** | Up to 10,000 people, invited by email or a **public link** | A light Beta App Review for the first build of each version |\n\nAdd a \"What to Test\" note for each build. Testers can send feedback and screenshots straight from the app, and crashes are reported in Xcode's **Organizer → Crashes**.\n\n## The App Store listing\n\nIn App Store Connect, fill in the version page:\n\n- **Screenshots**: required for the largest iPhone size (and iPad if supported). Take them in the Simulator ([[Cmd]] + [[S]]) or design framed ones.\n- **Promotional text, description, keywords, support URL, marketing URL.**\n- **App Privacy** (\"nutrition labels\"): declare what data your app and any SDKs collect, and how it's used. It must be accurate.\n- **Age rating** questionnaire, **category**, **pricing and availability**.\n- **App Review information**: contact details, and a **demo account** if your app requires sign-in.\n\n:::warning Privacy is taken seriously\nApple requires a **privacy manifest** (`PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`) declaring your use of certain APIs (like UserDefaults or file timestamps) and any tracking domains. Xcode's app template and most SDKs include theirs; check **File → New → File → App Privacy** if you need one. Apps that track users across other companies' apps must ask permission with App Tracking Transparency.\n:::\n\n## App Review\n\nSelect your build on the version page and click **Add for Review**, then **Submit**. Most apps are reviewed within a day or two. Common rejection reasons:\n\n- Crashes or obvious bugs (test on a real device!).\n- Placeholder content, broken links or unfinished features.\n- Missing or vague **usage descriptions**, or asking for permissions you don't need.\n- Missing privacy policy, or inaccurate privacy details.\n- Login required but no demo account provided.\n- Apps that are just a website in a wrapper, with no native value.\n\nRead Apple's **[App Review Guidelines](https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/)** before your first submission. If you're rejected, the message explains why; fix it and resubmit, or reply in Resolution Center.\n\n## Releasing\n\nChoose how the approved version is released: **manually**, **automatically** after approval, or on a date. **Phased release** rolls an update out to automatic-update users over 7 days, and you can pause it if a problem appears.\n\nThen monitor **Organizer → Crashes** and **App Store Connect → Analytics / Ratings and Reviews**, and reply to reviews.\n\n:::note Staying current\nEach spring Apple requires new uploads to be built with a recent Xcode and SDK. Keep Xcode up to date and ship an update at least once a year to stay eligible.\n:::\n\n## Updates\n\nFor every update: bump the **version** (and build), archive, upload, test in TestFlight, fill in **What's New**, and submit. Consider `StoreKit`'s `requestReview` to ask happy users for ratings at a good moment, such as after they've saved their third favourite.\n\n## Recap\n\n:::recap\n- Final bundle ID, polished icon, tested edge cases and a privacy policy before shipping.\n- Paid Developer Program + automatic signing = Xcode manages certificates and profiles.\n- Unique, increasing **build** numbers; **version** is user-facing.\n- Product → Archive → Distribute → App Store Connect.\n- TestFlight internal and external testing, then the listing, privacy details and App Review.\n:::\n\n:::exercise\n1. Write a draft App Store listing for RecipeBox: name, subtitle (30 characters), promotional text, a short description, and 10 keywords.\n2. List the data RecipeBox collects for the App Privacy section. (Hint: if favourites stay on the device and you have no analytics, the answer may be \"Data Not Collected\".)\n3. If you have a paid membership: archive one of your practice apps and send it to yourself via TestFlight.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: What must change for every build you upload to App Store Connect for the same version?\n- [ ] The bundle identifier\n- [x] The build number (it must be unique and increasing)\n- [ ] The app name\n- [ ] The signing certificate\n> Build numbers identify each upload.\n\nQ: What does \"Automatically manage signing\" do?\n- [ ] Skips code signing\n- [x] Lets Xcode create and renew certificates and provisioning profiles for you\n- [ ] Submits the app for review\n- [ ] Encrypts the app\n> Automatic signing removes most manual portal work.\n\nQ: How do you create the build you upload to App Store Connect?\n- [ ] Copy the .app from the Simulator\n- [x] Product → Archive, then Distribute App from the Organizer\n- [ ] Email the project folder to Apple\n- [ ] Build for Simulator in Release mode\n> Archives are signed, optimised release builds.\n\nQ: Which testers can install a TestFlight build without any review?\n- [x] Internal testers on your App Store Connect team\n- [ ] External testers with a public link\n- [ ] Anyone with the App Store\n- [ ] Nobody\n> External testing requires Beta App Review for the first build of a version.\n```\n"},{"id":"capstone-setup","title":"Capstone 1: Plan & Set Up RecipeBox","part":8,"minutes":30,"summary":"Time to build a complete app on your own. Meet RecipeBox, plan its features and architecture, create the project, and write the core Recipe model with sample data.","body":":::goals\n- The features and screens of **RecipeBox**\n- How the architecture maps to folders and types\n- Creating and configuring the project\n- The `Recipe` model, decoding the real API, and sample data for previews\n:::\n\n## Meet RecipeBox 🍳\n\nOver the next four lessons you'll build **RecipeBox**, a complete app that uses almost everything you've learned. It loads recipes from the free [DummyJSON recipes API](https://dummyjson.com/docs/recipes), works offline, and saves favourites.\n\n### Features\n\n1. **Home**: a grid of recipe cards (photo, name, time, rating, cuisine) with **search** and **pull-to-refresh**.\n2. **Recipe detail**: a large photo, key facts, ingredients and numbered steps, plus **favourite** and **share** buttons.\n3. **Favourites**: recipes you've hearted, saved with **SwiftData**, which also work offline.\n4. **Settings**: appearance (System / Light / Dark) and app info.\n5. **Offline-first**: the last loaded recipes are cached on disk and shown instantly on launch.\n6. **Adaptive**: more grid columns on iPad and in landscape; supports Dynamic Type and VoiceOver.\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div class=\"hl\">Home<small>search · grid · refresh</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>Recipe detail<small>ingredients · steps · ♥ · share</small></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"flow\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">\n<div class=\"hl\">Favourites<small>SwiftData</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Settings<small>@AppStorage</small></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"diagram-caption\">Home, Favourites and Settings are tabs; Detail is pushed onto each tab's navigation stack.</div></div>\n\n### Architecture\n\n<div class=\"diagram\">\n<div class=\"layers\">\n<div class=\"layer ui\"><h5>Views</h5><div class=\"boxes\"><span>RootView (tabs)</span><span>HomeView</span><span>RecipeDetailView</span><span>FavoritesView</span><span>SettingsView</span><span>RecipeCard</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"dir\">↓ ↑</div>\n<div class=\"layer domain\"><h5>View model</h5><div class=\"boxes\"><span>HomeViewModel (@Observable, @MainActor)</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"dir\">↓ ↑</div>\n<div class=\"layer data\"><h5>Services & storage</h5><div class=\"boxes\"><span>RecipeService → LiveRecipeService / MockRecipeService</span><span>RecipeCache (actor, disk)</span><span>FavoriteRecipe (SwiftData @Model)</span><span>@AppStorage</span></div></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n### Folder structure\n\n<div class=\"tree\">RecipeBox/\n├── App/\n│   ├── RecipeBoxApp.swift\n│   └── RootView.swift\n├── Models/\n│   ├── Recipe.swift\n│   ├── Recipe+Samples.swift\n│   └── FavoriteRecipe.swift\n├── Services/\n│   ├── RecipeService.swift\n│   └── RecipeCache.swift\n├── Features/\n│   ├── Home/            HomeView.swift · HomeViewModel.swift\n│   ├── Detail/          RecipeDetailView.swift\n│   ├── Favorites/       FavoritesView.swift\n│   └── Settings/        SettingsView.swift · Appearance.swift\n├── Components/          RecipeCard.swift · RecipeImage.swift · FavoriteToggle.swift\n└── Assets.xcassets</div>\n\n:::tip How to approach the capstone\nTry to write each file yourself **before** looking at the code in the lesson. Reuse your notes and earlier lessons. The provided code is a reference, not something to paste. If you're stuck for more than 15 minutes, peek, understand, then close it and type it yourself.\n:::\n\n## Step 1: create the project\n\n1. **File → New → Project → iOS → App**.\n2. Product Name `RecipeBox`, your Team (or None), Organization Identifier `com.yourname`, Interface **SwiftUI**, Language **Swift**, Testing System **Swift Testing**, Storage **None** (we'll add SwiftData ourselves so you see every step).\n3. In the target's **General** tab, set **Minimum Deployments** to **iOS 18.0**.\n4. In **Assets.xcassets**, set **AccentColor** to a warm orange (e.g. `#F05138`) for both appearances.\n5. Delete `ContentView.swift`; we'll create `RootView` instead. (Xcode will show an error in `RecipeBoxApp.swift` until then.)\n6. Create the folders: right-click the `RecipeBox` folder → **New Folder** for `App`, `Models`, `Services`, `Features` (with `Home`, `Detail`, `Favorites`, `Settings` inside), and `Components`. Move `RecipeBoxApp.swift` into `App`.\n\n## Step 2: look at the API\n\nOpen [dummyjson.com/recipes/1](https://dummyjson.com/recipes/1) in your browser:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": 1,\n  \"name\": \"Classic Margherita Pizza\",\n  \"ingredients\": [\"Pizza dough\", \"Tomato sauce\", \"Fresh mozzarella cheese\", \"…\"],\n  \"instructions\": [\"Preheat the oven to 475°F (245°C).\", \"…\"],\n  \"prepTimeMinutes\": 20,\n  \"cookTimeMinutes\": 15,\n  \"servings\": 4,\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n  \"cuisine\": \"Italian\",\n  \"caloriesPerServing\": 300,\n  \"tags\": [\"Pizza\", \"Italian\"],\n  \"userId\": 166,\n  \"image\": \"https://cdn.dummyjson.com/recipe-images/1.webp\",\n  \"rating\": 4.6,\n  \"reviewCount\": 98,\n  \"mealType\": [\"Dinner\"]\n}\n```\n\n`https://dummyjson.com/recipes?limit=0` returns all ~50 recipes as `{ \"recipes\": [ … ], \"total\": 50, … }`.\n\n## Step 3: the Recipe model\n\n```swift title=\"Models/Recipe.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nstruct Recipe: Codable, Identifiable, Hashable, Sendable {\n    let id: Int\n    let name: String\n    let ingredients: [String]\n    let instructions: [String]\n    let prepTimeMinutes: Int\n    let cookTimeMinutes: Int\n    let servings: Int\n    let difficulty: String\n    let cuisine: String\n    let caloriesPerServing: Int\n    let tags: [String]\n    let image: String\n    let rating: Double\n    let mealType: [String]\n\n    var totalMinutes: Int { prepTimeMinutes + cookTimeMinutes }\n    var imageURL: URL? { URL(string: image) }\n\n    /// Plain-text version for sharing.\n    var shareText: String {\n        \"\"\"\n        \\(name) (\\(cuisine), \\(totalMinutes) min)\n\n        Ingredients:\n        \\(ingredients.map { \"• \\($0)\" }.joined(separator: \"\\n\"))\n        \"\"\"\n    }\n}\n\n/// The shape of `GET /recipes`.\nstruct RecipeListResponse: Codable, Sendable {\n    let recipes: [Recipe]\n}\n```\n\n- Property names match the JSON keys exactly, so Codable needs no `CodingKeys`. Keys we don't need (`userId`, `reviewCount`) are simply ignored.\n- It's `Hashable` so we can push it with `NavigationLink(value:)`, and `Sendable` so it can move safely between actors.\n- `totalMinutes`, `imageURL` and `shareText` are **computed**, derived from the stored data.\n\n## Step 4: sample data for previews and tests\n\nPreviews and tests shouldn't hit the network. Give them realistic sample recipes:\n\n```swift title=\"Models/Recipe+Samples.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nextension Recipe {\n    static let sample = Recipe(\n        id: 1,\n        name: \"Classic Margherita Pizza\",\n        ingredients: [\"Pizza dough\", \"Tomato sauce\", \"Fresh mozzarella cheese\", \"Fresh basil leaves\", \"Olive oil\"],\n        instructions: [\n            \"Preheat the oven to 475°F (245°C).\",\n            \"Roll out the pizza dough and spread tomato sauce evenly.\",\n            \"Top with slices of fresh mozzarella and basil leaves.\",\n            \"Bake for 12–15 minutes until the crust is golden.\"\n        ],\n        prepTimeMinutes: 20, cookTimeMinutes: 15, servings: 4,\n        difficulty: \"Easy\", cuisine: \"Italian\", caloriesPerServing: 300,\n        tags: [\"Pizza\", \"Italian\"],\n        image: \"https://cdn.dummyjson.com/recipe-images/1.webp\",\n        rating: 4.6, mealType: [\"Dinner\"]\n    )\n\n    static let samples: [Recipe] = [\n        sample,\n        Recipe(id: 2, name: \"Vegetarian Stir-Fry\", ingredients: [\"Tofu\", \"Broccoli\", \"Soy sauce\"],\n               instructions: [\"Stir-fry the vegetables.\", \"Add tofu and sauce.\"],\n               prepTimeMinutes: 15, cookTimeMinutes: 20, servings: 3, difficulty: \"Medium\",\n               cuisine: \"Asian\", caloriesPerServing: 250, tags: [\"Vegetarian\", \"Stir-fry\"],\n               image: \"https://cdn.dummyjson.com/recipe-images/2.webp\", rating: 4.7, mealType: [\"Lunch\"]),\n        Recipe(id: 3, name: \"Chocolate Chip Cookies\", ingredients: [\"Butter\", \"Sugar\", \"Chocolate chips\"],\n               instructions: [\"Cream butter and sugar.\", \"Fold in chips and bake.\"],\n               prepTimeMinutes: 15, cookTimeMinutes: 10, servings: 24, difficulty: \"Easy\",\n               cuisine: \"American\", caloriesPerServing: 150, tags: [\"Cookies\", \"Dessert\"],\n               image: \"https://cdn.dummyjson.com/recipe-images/3.webp\", rating: 4.9, mealType: [\"Snack\", \"Dessert\"])\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n(`sample` is defined inside `extension Recipe`, so `samples` can refer to it directly.)\n\n## Step 5: a temporary root view\n\nSo the project builds, create a placeholder we'll replace in Lesson 36:\n\n```swift title=\"App/RootView.swift (temporary)\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct RootView: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        List(Recipe.samples) { recipe in\n            LabeledContent(recipe.name, value: \"\\(recipe.totalMinutes) min\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview { RootView() }\n```\n\n```swift title=\"App/RecipeBoxApp.swift (temporary)\" nocheck\nimport SwiftUI\n\n@main\nstruct RecipeBoxApp: App {\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup {\n            RootView()\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBuild and run ([[Cmd]] + [[R]]). You should see three sample recipes. Commit this to Git (**Integrate → Commit**, or `git commit` in Terminal): *\"Project setup and Recipe model\"*. Commit at the end of each capstone lesson so you can always go back.\n\n:::recap\n- RecipeBox: Home (search, grid, refresh), Detail, Favourites (SwiftData), Settings; offline-first.\n- Views → `HomeViewModel` → `RecipeService` + `RecipeCache` + SwiftData.\n- `Recipe` is a `Codable`, `Hashable`, `Sendable` struct matching the API, with computed helpers.\n- Sample data powers previews and tests.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: Why does Recipe conform to Hashable?\n- [ ] To decode JSON\n- [x] So it can be used as a navigation value with NavigationLink(value:)\n- [ ] To save it to disk\n- [ ] It's required by Identifiable\n> Navigation values must be Hashable.\n\nQ: The API includes `userId` and `reviewCount`, but Recipe doesn't declare them. What happens when decoding?\n- [ ] Decoding fails\n- [x] They're ignored\n- [ ] They're stored in a dictionary\n- [ ] The app crashes\n> Codable ignores unknown keys.\n\nQ: Why have `Recipe.samples`?\n- [ ] To show on the App Store\n- [x] So previews and tests work instantly and offline, without the network\n- [ ] Because the API is slow\n- [ ] They're required by SwiftUI\n> Sample data keeps development fast and deterministic.\n```\n"},{"id":"capstone-data","title":"Capstone 2: Services, Cache & Favourites","part":8,"minutes":45,"summary":"Build RecipeBox's data layer. A RecipeService protocol with live and mock implementations, an offline disk cache, a SwiftData favourites model, and the HomeViewModel that ties them together.","body":":::goals\n- A `RecipeService` protocol with a live (network) and a mock implementation\n- A `RecipeCache` actor that saves the last response to disk\n- The `FavoriteRecipe` SwiftData model, storing the full recipe for offline use\n- `HomeViewModel`: show cached data instantly, refresh from the network, search\n:::\n\n## The data flow\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div>DummyJSON API</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">LiveRecipeService</div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div>\n<div>HomeViewModel</div><div class=\"arrow\">⇄</div>\n<div class=\"hl\">RecipeCache<small>disk</small></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"flow\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">\n<div>Views</div><div class=\"arrow\">⇄</div><div class=\"hl\">SwiftData<small>FavoriteRecipe</small></div>\n</div></div>\n\n## 1. The service\n\n```swift title=\"Services/RecipeService.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\nprotocol RecipeService: Sendable {\n    func fetchRecipes() async throws -> [Recipe]\n}\n\nenum RecipeServiceError: LocalizedError {\n    case badStatus(Int)\n\n    var errorDescription: String? {\n        switch self {\n        case .badStatus(let code): \"The recipe server had a problem (\\(code)). Please try again later.\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct LiveRecipeService: RecipeService {\n    var session: URLSession = .shared\n    private let url = URL(string: \"https://dummyjson.com/recipes?limit=0\")!   // limit=0 → all recipes\n\n    func fetchRecipes() async throws -> [Recipe] {\n        let (data, response) = try await session.data(from: url)\n        if let http = response as? HTTPURLResponse, !(200..<300).contains(http.statusCode) {\n            throw RecipeServiceError.badStatus(http.statusCode)\n        }\n        return try JSONDecoder().decode(RecipeListResponse.self, from: data).recipes\n    }\n}\n\nstruct MockRecipeService: RecipeService {\n    var recipes: [Recipe] = Recipe.samples\n    var error: (any Error)? = nil\n    var delay: Duration = .zero\n\n    func fetchRecipes() async throws -> [Recipe] {\n        if delay > .zero { try await Task.sleep(for: delay) }\n        if let error { throw error }\n        return recipes\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe mock can simulate **success**, **failure** (`error: URLError(.notConnectedToInternet)`) and **slowness** (`delay: .seconds(2)`), which is perfect for previews of every state.\n\n## 2. The disk cache\n\n```swift title=\"Services/RecipeCache.swift\"\nimport Foundation\n\n/// Saves the most recent list of recipes to the Caches folder so the app works offline.\nactor RecipeCache {\n    struct Snapshot: Codable, Sendable {\n        let savedAt: Date\n        let recipes: [Recipe]\n    }\n\n    private let fileURL: URL\n\n    init(fileName: String = \"recipes-cache.json\") {\n        fileURL = URL.cachesDirectory.appending(path: fileName)\n    }\n\n    func load() -> Snapshot? {\n        guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: fileURL) else { return nil }\n        return try? JSONDecoder().decode(Snapshot.self, from: data)\n    }\n\n    func save(_ recipes: [Recipe]) throws {\n        let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(Snapshot(savedAt: .now, recipes: recipes))\n        try data.write(to: fileURL, options: .atomic)\n    }\n\n    func clear() {\n        try? FileManager.default.removeItem(at: fileURL)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nTests can pass a unique `fileName` so they never interfere with each other or with the real app's cache.\n\n## 3. The favourites model (SwiftData)\n\nFavourites must work **offline** and survive even if the API changes, so we store the whole recipe (encoded as JSON) alongside a few fields we want to sort and display:\n\n```swift title=\"Models/FavoriteRecipe.swift\"\nimport Foundation\nimport SwiftData\n\n@Model\nfinal class FavoriteRecipe {\n    @Attribute(.unique) var recipeID: Int\n    var name: String\n    var cuisine: String\n    var totalMinutes: Int\n    var imageURLString: String\n    var addedAt: Date\n    var payload: Data                       // the full Recipe, JSON-encoded\n\n    init(recipe: Recipe) {\n        recipeID = recipe.id\n        name = recipe.name\n        cuisine = recipe.cuisine\n        totalMinutes = recipe.totalMinutes\n        imageURLString = recipe.image\n        addedAt = .now\n        payload = (try? JSONEncoder().encode(recipe)) ?? Data()\n    }\n\n    /// The full recipe, decoded from the stored JSON.\n    var recipe: Recipe? {\n        try? JSONDecoder().decode(Recipe.self, from: payload)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnd a small helper that adds or removes a favourite, given the current favourites from a `@Query`:\n\n```swift title=\"Models/FavoriteRecipe+Toggle.swift\"\nimport SwiftData\n\nextension ModelContext {\n    /// Adds the recipe to favourites, or removes it if it's already there.\n    func toggleFavorite(_ recipe: Recipe, in favorites: [FavoriteRecipe]) {\n        if let existing = favorites.first(where: { $0.recipeID == recipe.id }) {\n            delete(existing)\n        } else {\n            insert(FavoriteRecipe(recipe: recipe))\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`@Attribute(.unique)` guarantees one row per recipe, even if something inserts twice.\n\n## 4. The home view model\n\nThis is the brain of the Home screen. It follows the offline-first pattern from Lesson 26:\n\n```swift title=\"Features/Home/HomeViewModel.swift\"\nimport Foundation\nimport Observation\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class HomeViewModel {\n    private(set) var recipes: [Recipe] = []\n    private(set) var isLoading = false\n    private(set) var errorMessage: String?\n    private(set) var lastUpdated: Date?\n    var searchText = \"\"\n\n    private let service: any RecipeService\n    private let cache: RecipeCache\n    private var hasLoaded = false\n\n    init(service: any RecipeService, cache: RecipeCache = RecipeCache()) {\n        self.service = service\n        self.cache = cache\n    }\n\n    /// Recipes matching the search text (name, cuisine or tag).\n    var visibleRecipes: [Recipe] {\n        let query = searchText.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespaces)\n        guard !query.isEmpty else { return recipes }\n        return recipes.filter { recipe in\n            recipe.name.localizedCaseInsensitiveContains(query)\n                || recipe.cuisine.localizedCaseInsensitiveContains(query)\n                || recipe.tags.contains { $0.localizedCaseInsensitiveContains(query) }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Called when the screen appears: show the cache instantly, then refresh once.\n    func loadIfNeeded() async {\n        guard !hasLoaded else { return }\n        hasLoaded = true\n        if let snapshot = await cache.load() {\n            recipes = snapshot.recipes\n            lastUpdated = snapshot.savedAt\n        }\n        await refresh()\n    }\n\n    /// Fetches fresh recipes from the network (pull-to-refresh uses this too).\n    func refresh() async {\n        isLoading = true\n        defer { isLoading = false }\n        do {\n            let fresh = try await service.fetchRecipes()\n            recipes = fresh.sorted { $0.rating > $1.rating }\n            lastUpdated = .now\n            errorMessage = nil\n            try? await cache.save(recipes)\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n            // The view disappeared: nothing to report.\n        } catch {\n            errorMessage = recipes.isEmpty\n                ? \"Couldn't load recipes. Check your internet connection and try again.\"\n                : \"You're offline. Showing saved recipes.\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWalk through what happens on first launch **with** internet, first launch **without** internet, and a later launch **without** internet:\n\n| Situation | What the user sees |\n|---|---|\n| First launch, online | Spinner → recipes (and they're cached) |\n| First launch, offline | Spinner → \"Couldn't load recipes…\" with a Try Again button |\n| Later launch, offline | Cached recipes **instantly**, plus a small \"You're offline\" note |\n\n## 5. Check it compiles\n\nBuild with [[Cmd]] + [[B]]. There's nothing new to see on screen yet, but a clean build means your data layer is ready. Commit: *\"Data layer: service, cache, favourites, view model\"*.\n\n:::exercise Stretch goals\n1. Add a `mealType` filter to `HomeViewModel`: a `selectedMealType: String?` property and a computed `allMealTypes: [String]` (unique, sorted), applied in `visibleRecipes`.\n2. Make `loadIfNeeded()` skip the network refresh if the cache is less than 15 minutes old. (Keep pull-to-refresh always refreshing.)\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: Why store the full recipe JSON in FavoriteRecipe instead of only its id?\n- [ ] SwiftData can't store Ints\n- [x] So favourites can be displayed and opened offline, without re-fetching from the API\n- [ ] To make the database bigger\n- [ ] It's required by @Model\n> Keeping a local copy makes favourites fully offline.\n\nQ: Why is RecipeCache an actor?\n- [ ] Actors are required for file access\n- [x] It protects the cache file from simultaneous reads and writes by different tasks\n- [ ] To run on the main thread\n- [ ] For SwiftData\n> Actors serialise access to shared resources.\n\nQ: What does `loadIfNeeded()` do before calling the network?\n- [ ] Nothing\n- [x] Shows the cached snapshot, if there is one, so the screen isn't empty\n- [ ] Deletes the cache\n- [ ] Checks the network with NWPathMonitor\n> Show cached data instantly, then refresh.\n\nQ: A refresh fails but cached recipes are showing. What's the error message?\n- [ ] \"Couldn't load recipes…\"\n- [x] \"You're offline. Showing saved recipes.\"\n- [ ] No message at all\n- [ ] The raw URLError\n> The message depends on whether the user still has something to look at.\n```\n"},{"id":"capstone-ui","title":"Capstone 3: Screens, Navigation & Settings","part":8,"minutes":60,"summary":"Bring RecipeBox to life. Build reusable components, the Home grid with search and refresh, a rich detail screen, the favourites list, settings, and the tab-based app shell.","body":":::goals\n- Reusable components: `RecipeImage`, `RecipeCard`, `FavoriteToggle`\n- The Home screen: adaptive grid, search, pull-to-refresh, empty and error states\n- The detail screen: header image, facts, ingredients, steps, share and favourite\n- The favourites list with SwiftData `@Query` and swipe to delete\n- Settings with `@AppStorage`, and the `TabView` app shell\n:::\n\n## 1. Components\n\n### `RecipeImage`\n\n`AsyncImage` with a nice placeholder and failure state, used everywhere a recipe photo appears:\n\n```swift title=\"Components/RecipeImage.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct RecipeImage: View {\n    let url: URL?\n\n    var body: some View {\n        AsyncImage(url: url, transaction: Transaction(animation: .easeInOut)) { phase in\n            switch phase {\n            case .success(let image):\n                image.resizable().scaledToFill()\n            case .failure:\n                placeholder(systemImage: \"fork.knife\")\n            default:\n                placeholder(systemImage: nil)\n            }\n        }\n        .accessibilityHidden(true)            // decorative: the recipe name is always shown nearby\n    }\n\n    private func placeholder(systemImage: String?) -> some View {\n        Rectangle()\n            .fill(.orange.opacity(0.12))\n            .overlay {\n                if let systemImage {\n                    Image(systemName: systemImage).font(.largeTitle).foregroundStyle(.orange)\n                } else {\n                    ProgressView()\n                }\n            }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### `FavoriteToggle`\n\nA heart button that reads favourites with `@Query` and toggles them in SwiftData. Because it queries by itself, any screen can drop it in:\n\n```swift title=\"Components/FavoriteToggle.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\nimport SwiftData\n\nstruct FavoriteToggle: View {\n    let recipe: Recipe\n    @Environment(\\.modelContext) private var context\n    @Query private var favorites: [FavoriteRecipe]\n\n    private var isFavorite: Bool { favorites.contains { $0.recipeID == recipe.id } }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Button {\n            withAnimation(.snappy) {\n                context.toggleFavorite(recipe, in: favorites)\n            }\n        } label: {\n            Image(systemName: isFavorite ? \"heart.fill\" : \"heart\")\n                .foregroundStyle(isFavorite ? .red : .primary)\n                .contentTransition(.symbolEffect(.replace))\n        }\n        .sensoryFeedback(.selection, trigger: isFavorite)       // a light haptic tap\n        .accessibilityLabel(isFavorite ? \"Remove \\(recipe.name) from favourites\"\n                                       : \"Add \\(recipe.name) to favourites\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### `RecipeCard`\n\n```swift title=\"Components/RecipeCard.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct RecipeCard: View {\n    let recipe: Recipe\n\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 0) {\n            RecipeImage(url: recipe.imageURL)\n                .frame(height: 180)\n                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n                .clipped()\n\n            VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 6) {\n                Text(recipe.name)\n                    .font(.headline)\n                    .foregroundStyle(.primary)\n                    .lineLimit(1)\n                HStack(spacing: 14) {\n                    Label(\"\\(recipe.totalMinutes) min\", systemImage: \"clock\")\n                    Label(recipe.rating.formatted(.number.precision(.fractionLength(1))), systemImage: \"star.fill\")\n                    Label(recipe.cuisine, systemImage: \"globe\")\n                        .lineLimit(1)\n                }\n                .font(.caption)\n                .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n                .labelStyle(.titleAndIcon)\n            }\n            .padding(14)\n        }\n        .background(.background.secondary)\n        .clipShape(.rect(cornerRadius: 20))\n        .contentShape(.rect(cornerRadius: 20))\n        .accessibilityElement(children: .combine)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`.accessibilityElement(children: .combine)` makes VoiceOver read the whole card as one item: *\"Classic Margherita Pizza, 35 min, 4.6, Italian\"*.\n\nNotice the card has **no heart button inside it**. The whole card will be wrapped in a `NavigationLink`, and a button nested inside another button is unreliable for taps, VoiceOver and UI tests. Instead, Home overlays the heart *on top of* the link.\n\n## 2. Home\n\n```swift title=\"Features/Home/HomeView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct HomeView: View {\n    @State private var viewModel: HomeViewModel\n\n    init(viewModel: HomeViewModel) {\n        _viewModel = State(initialValue: viewModel)\n    }\n\n    private let columns = [GridItem(.adaptive(minimum: 300), spacing: 16)]\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            ScrollView {\n                if let message = viewModel.errorMessage, !viewModel.recipes.isEmpty {\n                    Label(message, systemImage: \"wifi.slash\")\n                        .font(.footnote)\n                        .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n                        .padding(.horizontal)\n                }\n                LazyVGrid(columns: columns, spacing: 16) {\n                    ForEach(viewModel.visibleRecipes) { recipe in\n                        NavigationLink(value: recipe) {\n                            RecipeCard(recipe: recipe)\n                        }\n                        .buttonStyle(.plain)\n                        .overlay(alignment: .topTrailing) {       // a sibling of the link, not inside it\n                            FavoriteToggle(recipe: recipe)\n                                .font(.title3)\n                                .padding(10)\n                                .background(.thinMaterial, in: .circle)\n                                .padding(10)\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n                .padding()\n            }\n            .background(Color(.systemGroupedBackground))\n            .overlay { stateOverlay }\n            .navigationTitle(\"RecipeBox\")\n            .navigationDestination(for: Recipe.self) { recipe in\n                RecipeDetailView(recipe: recipe)\n            }\n            .searchable(text: $viewModel.searchText, prompt: \"Recipes, cuisines, tags\")\n            .refreshable { await viewModel.refresh() }\n            .task { await viewModel.loadIfNeeded() }\n        }\n    }\n\n    @ViewBuilder\n    private var stateOverlay: some View {\n        if viewModel.recipes.isEmpty {\n            if viewModel.isLoading {\n                ProgressView(\"Loading recipes…\")\n            } else if let message = viewModel.errorMessage {\n                ContentUnavailableView {\n                    Label(\"No Recipes\", systemImage: \"wifi.slash\")\n                } description: {\n                    Text(message)\n                } actions: {\n                    Button(\"Try Again\") { Task { await viewModel.refresh() } }\n                        .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)\n                }\n            }\n        } else if viewModel.visibleRecipes.isEmpty {\n            ContentUnavailableView.search(text: viewModel.searchText)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Loaded\") {\n    HomeView(viewModel: HomeViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(), cache: RecipeCache(fileName: \"preview.json\")))\n        .modelContainer(for: FavoriteRecipe.self, inMemory: true)\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Offline, no cache\") {\n    HomeView(viewModel: HomeViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(error: URLError(.notConnectedToInternet)),\n                                      cache: RecipeCache(fileName: \"empty-preview.json\")))\n        .modelContainer(for: FavoriteRecipe.self, inMemory: true)\n}\n```\n\nNotice how the two previews show **different states** just by injecting a different mock. That's the payoff of dependency injection.\n\n## 3. Recipe detail\n\n```swift title=\"Features/Detail/RecipeDetailView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct RecipeDetailView: View {\n    let recipe: Recipe\n\n    var body: some View {\n        ScrollView {\n            VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 24) {\n                RecipeImage(url: recipe.imageURL)\n                    .frame(height: 300)\n                    .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n                    .clipped()\n\n                VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 8) {\n                    Text(recipe.name)\n                        .font(.largeTitle.bold())\n                    Text(\"\\(recipe.cuisine) · \\(recipe.difficulty)\")\n                        .font(.title3)\n                        .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n                }\n                .padding(.horizontal)\n\n                factsRow\n                    .padding(.horizontal)\n\n                section(title: \"Ingredients\", systemImage: \"basket\") {\n                    ForEach(recipe.ingredients, id: \\.self) { ingredient in\n                        Label(ingredient, systemImage: \"circle.fill\")\n                            .labelStyle(BulletLabelStyle())\n                    }\n                }\n\n                section(title: \"Steps\", systemImage: \"list.number\") {\n                    ForEach(Array(recipe.instructions.enumerated()), id: \\.offset) { index, step in\n                        HStack(alignment: .firstTextBaseline, spacing: 12) {\n                            Text(\"\\(index + 1)\")\n                                .font(.headline)\n                                .foregroundStyle(.white)\n                                .frame(width: 28, height: 28)\n                                .background(.orange, in: .circle)\n                            Text(step)\n                                .fixedSize(horizontal: false, vertical: true)\n                        }\n                        .accessibilityElement(children: .combine)\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            .padding(.bottom, 32)\n        }\n        .ignoresSafeArea(edges: .top)\n        .navigationBarTitleDisplayMode(.inline)\n        .toolbar {\n            ToolbarItem(placement: .topBarTrailing) {\n                ShareLink(item: recipe.shareText, subject: Text(recipe.name)) {\n                    Label(\"Share\", systemImage: \"square.and.arrow.up\")\n                }\n            }\n            ToolbarItem(placement: .topBarTrailing) {\n                FavoriteToggle(recipe: recipe)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private var factsRow: some View {\n        HStack {\n            fact(value: \"\\(recipe.totalMinutes)\", label: \"minutes\", systemImage: \"clock\")\n            Divider()\n            fact(value: \"\\(recipe.servings)\", label: \"servings\", systemImage: \"person.2\")\n            Divider()\n            fact(value: \"\\(recipe.caloriesPerServing)\", label: \"kcal\", systemImage: \"flame\")\n            Divider()\n            fact(value: recipe.rating.formatted(.number.precision(.fractionLength(1))), label: \"rating\", systemImage: \"star\")\n        }\n        .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n        .padding(.vertical, 12)\n        .background(.background.secondary, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 16))\n    }\n\n    private func fact(value: String, label: String, systemImage: String) -> some View {\n        VStack(spacing: 4) {\n            Image(systemName: systemImage).foregroundStyle(.orange)\n            Text(value).font(.headline)\n            Text(label).font(.caption).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n        }\n        .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n        .accessibilityElement(children: .combine)\n    }\n\n    private func section<Content: View>(title: String, systemImage: String,\n                                        @ViewBuilder content: () -> Content) -> some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 12) {\n            Label(title, systemImage: systemImage)\n                .font(.title2.bold())\n            content()\n        }\n        .padding(.horizontal)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct BulletLabelStyle: LabelStyle {\n    func makeBody(configuration: Configuration) -> some View {\n        HStack(alignment: .firstTextBaseline, spacing: 10) {\n            configuration.icon\n                .font(.system(size: 6))\n                .foregroundStyle(.orange)\n            configuration.title\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    NavigationStack {\n        RecipeDetailView(recipe: .sample)\n    }\n    .modelContainer(for: FavoriteRecipe.self, inMemory: true)\n}\n```\n\nThe `section(title:systemImage:content:)` helper takes a **`@ViewBuilder` closure**, the same mechanism `VStack` uses, so each section's content reads naturally.\n\n## 4. Favourites\n\n```swift title=\"Features/Favorites/FavoritesView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\nimport SwiftData\n\nstruct FavoritesView: View {\n    @Environment(\\.modelContext) private var context\n    @Query(sort: \\FavoriteRecipe.addedAt, order: .reverse) private var favorites: [FavoriteRecipe]\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List {\n                ForEach(favorites) { favorite in\n                    if let recipe = favorite.recipe {\n                        NavigationLink(value: recipe) {\n                            HStack(spacing: 12) {\n                                RecipeImage(url: URL(string: favorite.imageURLString))\n                                    .frame(width: 64, height: 64)\n                                    .clipShape(.rect(cornerRadius: 12))\n                                VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 4) {\n                                    Text(favorite.name).font(.headline)\n                                    Text(\"\\(favorite.cuisine) · \\(favorite.totalMinutes) min\")\n                                        .font(.subheadline)\n                                        .foregroundStyle(.secondary)\n                                }\n                            }\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n                .onDelete { offsets in\n                    for index in offsets { context.delete(favorites[index]) }\n                }\n            }\n            .overlay {\n                if favorites.isEmpty {\n                    ContentUnavailableView(\"No Favourites Yet\", systemImage: \"heart\",\n                                           description: Text(\"Tap the heart on any recipe to save it here. Favourites work offline.\"))\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Favourites\")\n            .navigationDestination(for: Recipe.self) { recipe in\n                RecipeDetailView(recipe: recipe)\n            }\n            .toolbar {\n                if !favorites.isEmpty { EditButton() }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    FavoritesView()\n        .modelContainer(for: FavoriteRecipe.self, inMemory: true)\n}\n```\n\n`@Query` keeps this list in sync automatically: heart a recipe on the Home tab and it's already here when you switch tabs.\n\n## 5. Settings\n\n```swift title=\"Features/Settings/Appearance.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nenum Appearance: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {\n    case system, light, dark\n    var id: Self { self }\n\n    var title: String {\n        switch self {\n        case .system: \"System\"\n        case .light: \"Light\"\n        case .dark: \"Dark\"\n        }\n    }\n\n    var colorScheme: ColorScheme? {\n        switch self {\n        case .system: nil\n        case .light: .light\n        case .dark: .dark\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```swift title=\"Features/Settings/SettingsView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\nimport SwiftData\n\nstruct SettingsView: View {\n    @AppStorage(\"appearance\") private var appearance = Appearance.system\n    @Environment(\\.modelContext) private var context\n    @Query private var favorites: [FavoriteRecipe]\n    @State private var confirmClear = false\n\n    private var version: String {\n        let info = Bundle.main.infoDictionary\n        let short = info?[\"CFBundleShortVersionString\"] as? String ?? \"1.0\"\n        let build = info?[\"CFBundleVersion\"] as? String ?? \"1\"\n        return \"\\(short) (\\(build))\"\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            Form {\n                Section(\"Appearance\") {\n                    Picker(\"Theme\", selection: $appearance) {\n                        ForEach(Appearance.allCases) { Text($0.title).tag($0) }\n                    }\n                    .pickerStyle(.segmented)\n                }\n\n                Section(\"Favourites\") {\n                    LabeledContent(\"Saved recipes\", value: \"\\(favorites.count)\")\n                    Button(\"Remove All Favourites\", role: .destructive) { confirmClear = true }\n                        .disabled(favorites.isEmpty)\n                }\n\n                Section(\"About\") {\n                    LabeledContent(\"Version\", value: version)\n                    Link(\"Recipes from DummyJSON\", destination: URL(string: \"https://dummyjson.com/docs/recipes\")!)\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Settings\")\n            .confirmationDialog(\"Remove all favourites?\", isPresented: $confirmClear, titleVisibility: .visible) {\n                Button(\"Remove All\", role: .destructive) {\n                    for favorite in favorites { context.delete(favorite) }\n                }\n            } message: {\n                Text(\"This can't be undone.\")\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    SettingsView()\n        .modelContainer(for: FavoriteRecipe.self, inMemory: true)\n}\n```\n\n## 6. The app shell\n\n```swift title=\"App/RootView.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct RootView: View {\n    let service: any RecipeService\n    @AppStorage(\"appearance\") private var appearance = Appearance.system\n    @State private var homeViewModel: HomeViewModel\n\n    init(service: any RecipeService) {\n        self.service = service\n        _homeViewModel = State(initialValue: HomeViewModel(service: service))\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        TabView {\n            Tab(\"Recipes\", systemImage: \"fork.knife\") {\n                HomeView(viewModel: homeViewModel)\n            }\n            Tab(\"Favourites\", systemImage: \"heart\") {\n                FavoritesView()\n            }\n            Tab(\"Settings\", systemImage: \"gear\") {\n                SettingsView()\n            }\n        }\n        .preferredColorScheme(appearance.colorScheme)\n    }\n}\n\n#Preview {\n    RootView(service: MockRecipeService())\n        .modelContainer(for: FavoriteRecipe.self, inMemory: true)\n}\n```\n\n```swift title=\"App/RecipeBoxApp.swift\"\nimport SwiftUI\nimport SwiftData\n\n@main\nstruct RecipeBoxApp: App {\n    /// UI tests launch the app with \"-ui-testing\" to use mock data and a throwaway database.\n    private let isUITesting = ProcessInfo.processInfo.arguments.contains(\"-ui-testing\")\n\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup {\n            RootView(service: isUITesting ? MockRecipeService() : LiveRecipeService())\n        }\n        .modelContainer(for: FavoriteRecipe.self, inMemory: isUITesting)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## 7. Run it! 🎉\n\nRun the app and try everything:\n\n- [ ] Recipes load in a grid with photos. Pull down to refresh.\n- [ ] Search \"pasta\", \"Italian\" or \"dessert\". Clear the search, and try a word with no results.\n- [ ] Open a recipe: photo, facts, ingredients, numbered steps. Share it.\n- [ ] Heart recipes from the grid **and** from the detail screen; open **Favourites**; swipe to delete.\n- [ ] Switch **Settings → Theme** between Light, Dark and System. The whole app follows.\n- [ ] Rotate, and try an iPad simulator: more grid columns.\n- [ ] Turn off your Mac's Wi-Fi and relaunch: cached recipes appear with the offline note, and favourites still open.\n- [ ] Turn on **VoiceOver** (Settings → Accessibility) or use the **Accessibility Inspector**, and navigate the grid.\n\nIf something doesn't work, use the techniques from Lesson 32: breakpoints in `HomeViewModel.refresh()`, `po` in the console, and the view debugger.\n\n:::exercise Stretch goals\n1. Add a horizontal row of **meal type chips** (Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner, Dessert…) above the grid using the filter from the previous lesson's stretch goal.\n2. Add a **\"Surprise me\"** toolbar button that navigates to a random recipe. (Hint: give the `NavigationStack` a `path`.)\n3. Add a zoom transition from card to detail with `.matchedTransitionSource(id:in:)` and `.navigationTransition(.zoom(sourceID:in:))`.\n:::\n\n## Check your understanding\n\n```quiz\nQ: Why does FavoriteToggle use its own @Query instead of receiving `isFavorite` from its parent?\n- [ ] @Query is faster than parameters\n- [x] It makes the component self-contained, so any screen can use it without extra wiring\n- [ ] Parents can't pass Booleans\n- [ ] SwiftData requires it\n> Self-contained components are easy to reuse; SwiftData keeps every query in sync.\n\nQ: How do the two HomeView previews show different states?\n- [ ] By editing the view's code\n- [x] By injecting different MockRecipeService configurations\n- [ ] By turning off the Mac's Wi-Fi\n- [ ] Previews always show the same state\n> Dependency injection makes every state previewable.\n\nQ: What does `.accessibilityElement(children: .combine)` do on RecipeCard?\n- [ ] Hides the card from VoiceOver\n- [x] Makes VoiceOver read the card's contents as a single element\n- [ ] Combines two cards\n- [ ] Adds a label\n> Grouping related content makes VoiceOver navigation faster.\n```\n"},{"id":"capstone-finish","title":"Capstone 4: Test, Polish, Ship & What's Next","part":8,"minutes":45,"summary":"Finish RecipeBox like a professional. Write tests for decoding, the cache and the view model, add a UI test, polish accessibility and details, ship to TestFlight, then plan your next steps.","body":":::goals\n- Unit tests for decoding, the cache and `HomeViewModel`, with Swift Testing\n- A UI test with XCTest\n- A polish checklist: icon, accessibility, localisation, edge cases\n- Archiving and sending RecipeBox to TestFlight\n- Where to go next\n:::\n\n## 1. Decoding tests\n\nOpen the `RecipeBoxTests` target and replace the template test file:\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeBoxTests/RecipeDecodingTests.swift\"\nimport Testing\nimport Foundation\n@testable import RecipeBox\n\nstruct RecipeDecodingTests {\n    let json = \"\"\"\n    {\n      \"recipes\": [{\n        \"id\": 7, \"name\": \"Tacos\", \"ingredients\": [\"Tortillas\", \"Beef\"],\n        \"instructions\": [\"Warm the tortillas.\", \"Fill and serve.\"],\n        \"prepTimeMinutes\": 15, \"cookTimeMinutes\": 10, \"servings\": 3,\n        \"difficulty\": \"Easy\", \"cuisine\": \"Mexican\", \"caloriesPerServing\": 450,\n        \"tags\": [\"Mexican\"], \"userId\": 1, \"image\": \"https://cdn.dummyjson.com/recipe-images/7.webp\",\n        \"rating\": 4.8, \"reviewCount\": 12, \"mealType\": [\"Dinner\"]\n      }],\n      \"total\": 1, \"skip\": 0, \"limit\": 1\n    }\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @Test func decodesTheAPIResponse() throws {\n        let response = try JSONDecoder().decode(RecipeListResponse.self, from: Data(json.utf8))\n        let recipe = try #require(response.recipes.first)\n\n        #expect(recipe.name == \"Tacos\")\n        #expect(recipe.totalMinutes == 25)\n        #expect(recipe.imageURL?.lastPathComponent == \"7.webp\")\n        #expect(recipe.ingredients.count == 2)\n    }\n\n    @Test func shareTextListsIngredients() {\n        #expect(Recipe.sample.shareText.contains(\"• Pizza dough\"))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Cache tests\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeBoxTests/RecipeCacheTests.swift\"\nimport Testing\nimport Foundation\n@testable import RecipeBox\n\nstruct RecipeCacheTests {\n    let cache = RecipeCache(fileName: \"test-\\(UUID().uuidString).json\")   // unique per test\n\n    @Test func emptyCacheReturnsNil() async {\n        #expect(await cache.load() == nil)\n    }\n\n    @Test func savesAndLoadsRecipes() async throws {\n        try await cache.save(Recipe.samples)\n\n        let snapshot = try #require(await cache.load())\n        #expect(snapshot.recipes == Recipe.samples)\n        await cache.clear()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## 3. View model tests\n\nThis is where dependency injection pays off. Every scenario is a few lines, with no network:\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeBoxTests/HomeViewModelTests.swift\"\nimport Testing\nimport Foundation\n@testable import RecipeBox\n\n@MainActor\nstruct HomeViewModelTests {\n    private func makeCache() -> RecipeCache {\n        RecipeCache(fileName: \"vm-test-\\(UUID().uuidString).json\")\n    }\n\n    @Test func loadsRecipesSortedByRating() async {\n        let vm = HomeViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(), cache: makeCache())\n\n        await vm.loadIfNeeded()\n\n        #expect(vm.recipes.map(\\.rating) == Recipe.samples.map(\\.rating).sorted(by: >))\n        #expect(vm.errorMessage == nil)\n        #expect(vm.isLoading == false)\n    }\n\n    @Test func offlineWithoutCacheShowsError() async {\n        let vm = HomeViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(error: URLError(.notConnectedToInternet)),\n                               cache: makeCache())\n\n        await vm.loadIfNeeded()\n\n        #expect(vm.recipes.isEmpty)\n        #expect(vm.errorMessage?.contains(\"Couldn't load\") == true)\n    }\n\n    @Test func offlineWithCacheKeepsRecipes() async throws {\n        let cache = makeCache()\n        try await cache.save(Recipe.samples)\n        let vm = HomeViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(error: URLError(.notConnectedToInternet)), cache: cache)\n\n        await vm.loadIfNeeded()\n\n        #expect(vm.recipes.count == Recipe.samples.count)\n        #expect(vm.errorMessage?.contains(\"offline\") == true)\n    }\n\n    @Test(arguments: [(\"pizza\", 1), (\"ITALIAN\", 1), (\"dessert\", 1), (\"\", 3), (\"sushi\", 0)])\n    func searchMatchesNameCuisineAndTags(query: String, expectedCount: Int) async {\n        let vm = HomeViewModel(service: MockRecipeService(), cache: makeCache())\n        await vm.loadIfNeeded()\n\n        vm.searchText = query\n\n        #expect(vm.visibleRecipes.count == expectedCount)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nPress [[Cmd]] + [[U]]. All green? 🎉 If a test fails, the message shows the actual values, so read it carefully before changing code.\n\n## 4. A UI test\n\nThe app already swaps in mock data when launched with `-ui-testing` (see `RecipeBoxApp`). In the `RecipeBoxUITests` target:\n\n```swift title=\"RecipeBoxUITests/RecipeBoxUITests.swift\" nocheck\nimport XCTest\n\nfinal class RecipeBoxUITests: XCTestCase {\n    @MainActor\n    func testFavouritingARecipeShowsItInFavourites() throws {\n        let app = XCUIApplication()\n        app.launchArguments = [\"-ui-testing\"]\n        app.launch()\n\n        let heart = app.buttons[\"Add Classic Margherita Pizza to favourites\"]\n        XCTAssertTrue(heart.waitForExistence(timeout: 5))\n        heart.tap()\n\n        app.tabBars.buttons[\"Favourites\"].tap()\n        XCTAssertTrue(app.staticTexts[\"Classic Margherita Pizza\"].waitForExistence(timeout: 2))\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe test finds the heart by its **accessibility label**, another reason good labels matter.\n\n## 5. Polish checklist\n\n- [ ] **App icon**: a 1024×1024 image in AppIcon, with Dark and Tinted variants.\n- [ ] **Launch screen**: in the target's **Info** tab, set a *Launch Screen* background colour that matches your app.\n- [ ] **Accessibility**: navigate the whole app with **VoiceOver**; run **Xcode → Open Developer Tool → Accessibility Inspector → Audit**; try the largest Dynamic Type size and **Bold Text**.\n- [ ] **Localisation**: add a String Catalog, build to collect strings, and add a second language.\n- [ ] **Dark mode** on every screen.\n- [ ] **Edge cases**: first launch offline, empty search results, favourites with no internet, rotating on every screen, being terminated in the background.\n- [ ] **iPad**: check the grid and navigation on an iPad simulator, or remove iPad from *Supported Destinations* if you don't want to support it yet.\n- [ ] **Performance**: scroll the grid fast on a real device; profile with Instruments if anything stutters.\n- [ ] **Haptics and animation**: subtle `sensoryFeedback` on important actions, short animations, respect Reduce Motion.\n\n## 6. Ship it to TestFlight\n\nFollow Lesson 33:\n\n1. Set **Version** `1.0.0` and **Build** `1`.\n2. Select **Any iOS Device (arm64)**, then **Product → Archive**.\n3. **Distribute App → App Store Connect → Upload**.\n4. In App Store Connect, add yourself (and friends) as testers, then install from the **TestFlight** app. Now you're running your own app from the App Store's infrastructure! 🚀\n5. When you're ready: screenshots, description, privacy details (\"Data Not Collected\" if you added no analytics), a privacy policy URL, and **Submit for Review**.\n\n:::tip Put it on GitHub\nPush RecipeBox to a public GitHub repository with a README showing screenshots, the feature list, the architecture diagram and the tech stack. It's an excellent portfolio piece for iOS job applications.\n:::\n\n## 🎓 Congratulations!\n\nYou started with \"What is iOS?\" and you've built a real, offline-first app with:\n\n<div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"flow\">\n<div class=\"hl\">Swift<small>types · optionals · async/await · actors</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">SwiftUI<small>layout · state · lists · navigation · animation</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Architecture<small>MVVM · Observation · DI</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Data<small>URLSession · Codable · SwiftData · caching</small></div>\n<div class=\"hl\">Quality<small>Swift Testing · debugging · TestFlight</small></div>\n</div></div>\n\nThat's the modern Apple toolkit professionals use every day. Seriously, well done. 🎉\n\n## Where to go next\n\n### Build more apps\n\nThe best way to grow is to build things *without* a tutorial. Ideas, roughly in order of difficulty:\n\n1. **Habit tracker**: SwiftData, local notifications, charts with **Swift Charts**, a widget.\n2. **Expense tracker**: forms, validation, SwiftData relationships, CSV export with `ShareLink`, Face ID lock with **LocalAuthentication**.\n3. **Weather app**: location, a real API with a key (kept out of Git), WidgetKit, maps with **MapKit**.\n4. **Chat app**: Sign in with Apple, a backend (e.g. CloudKit or Firebase), push notifications, photo messages.\n\n### Topics to learn next\n\n| Topic | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **Swift Charts** | Beautiful, accessible charts in a few lines |\n| **MapKit & Core Location** | Maps, search and directions |\n| **CloudKit / SwiftData sync** | Sync data across the user's devices via iCloud |\n| **StoreKit 2** | In-app purchases and subscriptions |\n| **Live Activities & Dynamic Island** | Real-time updates on the Lock Screen |\n| **Apple Intelligence & App Intents** | Integrate your app with Siri and system intelligence features |\n| **Foundation Models / Core ML** | On-device machine learning and language features |\n| **visionOS, watchOS, macOS** | Bring your SwiftUI skills to every Apple platform |\n| **Swift packages & modularisation** | How larger teams structure apps |\n| **CI/CD** with Xcode Cloud or GitHub Actions | Automated builds, tests and TestFlight uploads |\n\n### Resources\n\n- [developer.apple.com/tutorials](https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/app-dev-training): Apple's free, hands-on SwiftUI tutorials.\n- [The Swift Programming Language](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/): the official book, free online.\n- [Apple Developer Documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/) and **WWDC session videos** (in the Apple Developer app): the best way to learn each year's new APIs.\n- [Human Interface Guidelines](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/): how great iOS apps look and behave.\n- [Swift Forums](https://forums.swift.org) and the Apple Developer Forums: ask questions and learn from others.\n\n:::analogy One last piece of advice\nEvery experienced iOS developer still searches the docs, watches WWDC videos to catch up, and gets confused by signing errors. That's normal! What makes a professional isn't knowing everything but knowing how to find out. You now have the foundation to learn anything else on Apple platforms. Go build something you care about. 🚀\n:::\n\n## Final check\n\n```quiz\nQ: Why do the view model tests create a RecipeCache with a unique file name?\n- [ ] File names must be unique on iOS\n- [x] So tests don't share cached data with each other or with the real app\n- [ ] It makes tests faster\n- [ ] SwiftData requires it\n> Isolated tests are reliable tests.\n\nQ: How does the UI test get predictable data without the network?\n- [ ] It turns off Wi-Fi\n- [x] It launches the app with \"-ui-testing\", which makes the app use MockRecipeService and an in-memory database\n- [ ] It edits the API\n- [ ] UI tests can't use mock data\n> Launch arguments let the app switch dependencies for testing.\n\nQ: What's the best way to check your app's accessibility?\n- [ ] Only look at it in dark mode\n- [x] Navigate it with VoiceOver, run the Accessibility Inspector audit, and try large text sizes\n- [ ] Add accessibility labels to every Text\n- [ ] Run the unit tests\n> Experiencing the app like assistive-technology users do reveals real problems quickly.\n```\n"}]};
